{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short; git rev-parse HEAD; cat collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; cat collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md'","aggregated_output":"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-Z3PZFHZs' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-12 03:35:37.869 xcodebuild[89062:57440368]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-12 03:35:38.102 xcodebuild[89062:57440367] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-PrUzw0LT' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-12 03:35:38.651 xcodebuild[89066:57440386]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-12 03:35:38.823 xcodebuild[89066:57440385] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.report.md\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md\n?? collab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.prompt.md\n?? collab/example-suspended-state.clj\n?? collab/numbering-probe/deps.edn\n?? collab/numbering-probe/run.log\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame_test.cljc\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-hJAcdm9p' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.044 xcodebuild[89073:57440446]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.218 xcodebuild[89073:57440445] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-qIuPIPR1' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.733 xcodebuild[89077:57440462]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.917 xcodebuild[89077:57440461] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697\nI’ll read the architecture rules, note, and carrier draft, then challenge the numbering rule with the prototype and check its claims against the phase 6a code. I’ll keep the worktree read-only.\nI found a gap in the orbit lemma: equal content encodings erase aliasing within an element, so `[X X]` can tie with `[Y Z]` even though they aren’t interchangeable. I’m testing that case and nested ties. The Clojure launcher tried to write its classpath cache; I’ll use an existing classpath to keep the review read-only.\nThe prototype produces different BLAKE3 roots for the same set when I reverse `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`. The same failure occurs for map entries `{X X, Y Z}`. Nested sets also return an empty numbering, leaving source ids in the carrier. The reported fixtures and all 21 phase 6a row tests reproduce, but those tests miss these cases.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 19:42:34 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 02:42:34 Asia/Bangkok\n\nDiscovery-order numbering remains a viable direction, but **this note is not sound as written**. D4 admits choices that change the root id, and nested ties can leave cursor cells unnumbered.\n\nReview was read-only at HEAD `7a6d7cc9`; phase 6a was inspected through Git at `d77da9f0`. No files were edited, staged, or committed. Completion timestamps use the available clock, which precedes the brief’s Created-GMT.\n\nFindings below use `note` for `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`, `probe` for `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, and `rows` for phase 6a’s `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`.\n\n| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Blocking** | note:153, 183 | D4’s occurrence-locality condition does not preserve alias topology **within** an element. Equal `CE` does not imply interchangeability. `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` passes D4 and produces different roots after element reversal. | Require a proven alias-preserving correspondence between tied elements, including their internal traversal. Conservatively refuse uncertified cases. Equal bodies and disjoint identities alone are insufficient. |\n| **Blocking** | note:153; probe:146–162 | Entering D4 elements can discover nested tie groups. The prototype processes only the old residue, then returns success. `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` returns an empty numbering and emits source ids. | Specify and implement processing of newly discovered deferred groups, including further fixpoint/orbit checks. Require total numbering before emission. |\n| **High** | note:70–88, 219 | The stated refusal class is too narrow. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` refuses even though X and Y are interchangeable and there are no guest pairs or map keys. | Describe D5 by its actual predicate. Separate “not certified by this procedure” from “non-interchangeable,” and remove the unsupported rarity claim. |\n| **High** | note:352–354; rows:570–575 | Decode invokes `emit`. A newly introduced lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` exception will propagate unless explicitly translated. Recompute-and-compare alone does not implement the proposed decode taxonomy. | Translate only `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to `:yin.k/undecodable` at recomputation, preserving resource refusals and validator precedence. |\n| **Medium** | probe:48, 111–113, 131–139; note:299 | The prototype differs from D1–D5: numbered `CE` uses keyword `:yin.k/cc-n`, not integer `n`; parked-table values are incorrectly visited as `V`, although they are structural refs; the orbit walker omits `:refs`. Install children still use 6a numbering. | Make the prototype schema-driven and match the specified encoding. Test parked states, K frames, and children under discovery numbering in every scope. |\n| **Medium** | note:18, 124, 192–196 | “Linear work” is not established. `CE` sorts unordered containers; repeated encoding and nested expansion add work. Acyclic DAGs can have large expanded traversals. | State a bound in terms of expanded positions, sorting, rounds, and encoded bytes. Account for that work before allocation under the retained budgets. |\n| **Medium** | note:223–262 | Amendments leave stale wording: A2 leaves “no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity”; A5 leaves §10.10’s “never falls back to encounter order”; A8 leaves the old byte-string body-label explanation and step-1 reference. | Update those passages explicitly. Distinguish canonical traversal from arbitrary host enumeration. |\n| **Low** | note:274–289 | The portable-id example changes the carried `id-counter`, so the two complete tasks are already structurally different. Some engine line references are inaccurate. | Hold every carried field, including the counter, equal in the example; correct the citations. |\n\n**1. Position inventory**\n\nF1 is substantially correct: I found **no omitted identity-bearing position** in the carrier §5 tables or phase 6a’s row schema.\n\nThe ordered positions cover:\n\n- Root free environment, store, module stores, parked table, waits, and result.\n- Act environments, windows, K frames; tail K frames; captured acts.\n- Closure bindings and module-store contents.\n- Pending bare cursor-cell slots and guest value/envelope slots.\n- Install children, each with an independent task-local numbering scope.\n\nOnly guest set elements and guest map entries lack a structural order. Census declaration edges are correctly excluded.\n\nOne wording correction: D1 should explicitly retain **numeric ascending register order** for windows, as the carrier specifies, rather than grouping windows indiscriminately under byte ordering. The prototype’s parked-table omission is an implementation defect, not an inventory omission.\n\n**2. Attempts to break D1–D5**\n\nI ran the prototype using `java -cp` with an existing classpath because the requested Clojure launcher attempted a prohibited cache write. All custom inputs were evaluated in memory.\n\nThe following constructor fixes the surrounding task exactly:\n\n```clojure\n(require '[probe.discovery :as p]\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows :as r]\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as t])\n\n(def b (t/cursor-cell-body :s 0))\n(def c #(vector :cursor-cell %))\n(def row #(vector :row %))\n(def v #(row (r/node :vec (mapv c %))))\n(def s #(row (r/node :set %)))\n(def task\n  (fn [ids result]\n    (t/task-node (mapv #(vector % b) ids) result)))\n```\n\nAll named cursor cells below have the same body.\n\n**Counterexample A — D4’s orbit lemma**\n\n```clojure\n(task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])]))\n(task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:y :z]) (v [:x :x])]))\n```\n\nThese differ only in set enumeration. Both pass D4.\n\n| Enumeration | Numbering | BLAKE3 root digest |\n|---|---|---|\n| `[X X]`, `[Y Z]` | X=1, Y=2, Z=3 | `65ba4530bd8908e734a8be4fcc594d2989c5a8a7d1017779ef8f62991df38dab` |\n| `[Y Z]`, `[X X]` | Y=1, Z=2, X=3 | `387f294a2f1963f2f2241e8527d8698dedff5d50de4f46bb762b456a01375090` |\n\nThe emitted multisets contain respectively `[1 1], [2 3]` and `[1 2], [3 3]`. There is no alias-preserving automorphism exchanging the two elements. Both abstract inputs successfully emit and decode under existing 6a.\n\n**Counterexample B — guest map keys and values**\n\n```clojure\n(task [:x :y :z]\n      (row (r/node :map [(c :x) (c :x) (c :y) (c :z)])))\n\n(task [:x :y :z]\n      (row (r/node :map [(c :y) (c :z) (c :x) (c :x)])))\n```\n\nThe roots differ:\n\n```text\n409548a71f080cae6f3f66911409370d83a5df9e928135d95c497e9b770d9328\nbe62ba43d9d07dc7b5e29375e195ab839ab37b721074af816e540615f6167ee9\n```\n\nThis is the same missing alias distinction across an entry’s key and value.\n\n**Counterexample C — nested unordered containers**\n\n```clojure\n(task [:x :y :z :w]\n      (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)])\n          (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]))\n```\n\nThe prototype returns `{:numbering {}}`. Renaming X/Y/Z/W to Q/R/S/T changes the emitted root:\n\n```text\nfddde4522dbebc4ee6fa2d78a1bd1d2691faccc26c4ca14ee17c620ecdcddf0b\nabb32996b0ac30b720f17c6cfacf0323dcb16a47d58cc8e54c33571ce5d58080\n```\n\nThe outer orbit step discovers inner ties but never processes them.\n\nOther requested challenges:\n\n- `[#{X Y} X]` correctly resolves to X=1, Y=2 through D3.\n- `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` refuses because identities cross tie groups.\n- A parked act containing an ordered X reference is ignored by the prototype, causing an unnecessary refusal.\n- An install child using the review fixture gets its old 6a numbering. Switching all scopes to discovery changes the parent root, confirming that current child results do not validate the proposed rule.\n\n**3. Proof**\n\nThe proof fails at the orbit lemma’s claim that equal `CE` yields “the same internal pattern.” Body erasure removes exactly the alias information needed to establish that claim.\n\nThe renaming induction is reasonable for D1–D3 with a complete schema walk and fixed encoding. “No source id is read” is insufficient for permutation invariance: listed order remains an input to D4.\n\nD3’s monotone numbering supports a finite bound on productive rounds. It does not prove that D4 finishes nested discovery or that every reached cursor cell receives a number. Consequently, the asserted bijection, determinism, and cross-engine conclusion are not established.\n\nSorting and repeated expansion also invalidate the unqualified linear-work claim.\n\n**4. Refusal taxonomy and practical class**\n\nThe proposed families are appropriate:\n\n- Lift: `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\n- Decode recomputation: `:yin.k/undecodable`, same kind.\n- Actual budget exhaustion: retain qualified unsupported/resource refusal.\n\nA deliberate representational restriction is not a resource exhaustion. Run it at carrier validation step 6, after structural/canonical-form checks and before frame validation. Implement the decode translation explicitly.\n\nThe remaining class is **not demonstrated rare**. The two-group example above is a simple reusable collection, not an exotic graph. Moreover, repairing D4 conservatively will enlarge the refused class beyond what F4 currently describes. That limitation needs an accurate owner-facing description before adoption.\n\n**5. Impact on 6a and pins**\n\nThe proposed deletions are broadly correct: refinement, positions, class construction, exhaustive search, growing-label checks, and search/label-byte budget keys.\n\nKeep the carrier schema, BLAKE3 addressing, census numeric order, final row ordering, recompute-and-compare, and numbering-before-frame-validation order. Add a complete traversal, deferred processing, certified orbit rule, total-numbering assertion, refusal translation, and meaningful work accounting.\n\nI recomputed these pins:\n\n| Fixture | BLAKE3 digest | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Two-cell fixture | `fab223d9b3540fdce0ccf2132f8a4041917a158a32295470a4936754742d3ac4` | Unchanged |\n| Worked frame | `c4a7c7e6de3b44400efec5930bc3d85d86b29153b4b2d548acba630bfbc3da7d` | Unchanged |\n| Review counterexample | `a3713a5e1d1a051d1c0aab255bdc48349993fbf21d68ba947e74fc4408c68c28` | Matches proposed pin |\n| `[X X Y]` | `4993b5b192de7d903a90f7bc87dde8c7d5ac007c2b013ef62b998dc988282576` | Matches proposed pin |\n| `[X Y Y]` | `536c810b07c7cab1dd812fcb1bbf0c7433dc41af4e39037c5f278e82abf1e032` | Matches; unchanged from 6a |\n\nThe symmetric-pair, repeated-pair, map, and ten-cursor-cell pins also reproduced. The prototype reproduced the reported 400-state statistics. Existing 6a row tests passed: **21 tests, 324 assertions**.\n\nConsumers include `emit`, child emission, `decode*`, and `canonical-under`. Continuation §5.4’s round-trip and cross-engine laws depend on canonical re-emission. `canonical-under` still recomputes through `emit`; its mathematical guarantee therefore inherits this defect.\n\nAcceptance items 12–20 need the three counterexamples above, multi-group cases, parked/K coverage, discovery-numbered children, total-numbering checks, and decode round trips. Random testing alone missed both blocking defects. Keep a large-input regression even when deleting its obsolete label-budget expectation.\n\n**6. Portable ids**\n\nThe rejection is sound **for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged** under the owner’s structure-only root law. Local counters, execution sequence, occurrence/counter hashes, and random names cannot replace structural canonicalization.\n\nCorrect the example by making both tasks’ carried counters equal—for example, consume an unrelated fresh name in the second history. Do not claim every imaginable portable naming scheme is impossible: ids recomputed from snapshot structure are effectively the canonicalization problem again.\n\nThe note calls this F7; F6 is prototype evidence.\n\n**7. Grounding, amendments, and portability**\n\nI spot-checked more than twelve cited locations against branch code:\n\n| Reference | Check |\n|---|---|\n| rows:37–57 | Row schema and identity-bearing positions |\n| rows:140–145 | Numeric census order |\n| rows:148–156 | Emitted collection ordering |\n| rows:185–189 | Independent child numbering |\n| rows:200–202 | Budget keys |\n| rows:212–230 | Label-byte checking |\n| rows:252–255 | Initial labels |\n| rows:257–284 | Content encoding |\n| rows:285–325 | Positions |\n| rows:331–354 | Refinement and search |\n| rows:570–578 | Recompute, compare, then frames |\n| rows_test:28 | Two-cell fixture |\n| rows_test:159 | Alias fixtures |\n| rows_test:171 | Symmetric pair |\n| rows_test:206 | Numbering precedes frame defect |\n| rows_test:307–338 | Pins and numeric census |\n| rows_test:392–421 | Seven-cursor-cell and large-input tests |\n\nThe scratch rows implementation and tests matched their branch versions by checksum. Engine citations need correction: `issue-ref` starts at 311, `gensym` at 2312, and unminted cursor creation is principally 557–568.\n\nAmendments should also:\n\n- State that `[:child]` deliberately replaces the existing `[:child <child root id>]`; “one change; nothing else changes” is inaccurate.\n- Define `[:n k]` unambiguously as an integer and align the prototype.\n- Remove the stale body-label explanation in continuation §5.5.\n- Update §10.10’s encounter-order wording.\n- Change the diagnostics’ “two kinds” count when adding a third lift refusal.\n\nThe direction is compatible with phase 5’s pure image derivations and scanners. Phase 6’s engine-independent frames and sharing requirements remain unchanged, but its equality laws require the repaired canonicality proof.\n\nRetain continuation §12’s portability rules: nil-valued keys remain present; membership uses `contains?`; large sequences use `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`; pinned fixtures are `.cljc` data rather than whitespace-sensitive EDN strings on Dart. These were inspected, not validated by new Node or Dart runs.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 20:34:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 03:34:00 Asia/Bangkok\n\n(Host clock as read by `date -u` at completion; as in the previous\nreport, this host's clock precedes the brief's Created-GMT.)\n\n# Architect report: revision of the discovery-order numbering note after the codex review\n\nEdited: `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\n(rewritten, 724 lines) and the prototype\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (rewritten, 751 lines;\nrun log at `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`). Nothing staged or\ncommitted. The 6a code was read from branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`)\nthrough the probe project's copies, as before.\n\n## The repair in one paragraph\n\nThe content encoding now numbers the cursor cells *inside* an element\nprovisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to the\nenclosing numbering (de Bruijn style: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`,\n`[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`), and the encoding of an element is the\nspelling produced by a *local run* of the same procedure over it (nested\ncontainers sorted, nested ties deferred and re-examined, what it cannot\nresolve spelled `[:tie …]`). There is one worklist per run, so a tie\ndiscovered inside an entered element joins the root worklist and is\nre-examined. The orbit rule is replaced by a **component rule**: at a\nfixpoint, tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell form a\ncomponent; a component holding two elements of one group is the D5\nrefusal; otherwise components are encoded by a local run over their\nelements in group order, sorted, and the least run is entered (equal\nencodings are isomorphic, so host order among them is harmless, proved\nas lemma C). The proof is an isomorphism-invariance theorem with four\nlemmas (§3 of the note).\n\n## Per item\n\n**1. BLOCKING, orbit lemma unsound.** Changed: the spelling (note §2\n\"Spelling\"), D4 (components, component encodings, least-run entry), §3\n(theorem, lemmas A–D). I evaluated the orchestrator's direction and\nadopted it, with one addition it needs to be sound: local first-occurrence\nindices inside an element are not enough when the element itself holds\nnested unordered containers or when tied elements share cells across\ngroups, so the element's key is the output of a full local run (which\nresolves nested ties by the same rules and spells unresolved ones as\n`[:tie]`), and the orbit candidates are components, not elements. The\nnote shows why the weaker version fails: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and\n`[#{P Q} #{R S}]` would key equal under single-pass local indices with\n`[:tie]` parts, yet they are not isomorphic, and entering them in either\norder gives different rows; the local run with the component rule keys\nthem differently (prototype fixture `b35b8e12…`). Verified: review A\n`#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and B `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations\nand after renaming plus shuffling; the roots `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`\nequal the digests codex computed for the `X=1, Y=2, Z=3` numbering, an\nindependent cross-check of the emission. Hand derivations in note §6.\n\n**2. BLOCKING, nested tie groups.** Changed: D3 states that entering an\nelement inside a group runs D1 on it, so newly discovered groups join\nthe one worklist of the run and are re-examined next round; D4 and D5\nrecurse through component local runs; a \"Totality\" paragraph requires a\nbijection onto `1…n` before emission, asserted by the implementation as\na defect (never a refusal, never a partial carrier). Prototype:\n`discovery-numbering` throws \"numbering is not total\" with the missing\nand extra cells; it fired once during revision on a malformed fixture of\nmine (a census missing the worked frame's pending cursor cell), which is\nthe behaviour wanted. Verified: review C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` numbers all\nfour cells, root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming (and 6a\nreaches the same root); `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` numbers `X cc-1` first and\nresolves the inner sets by the fixpoint and the component rule, root\n`6ea03f9b…`.\n\n**3. HIGH, the refusal class.** Changed: F4 states the predicate in one\nsentence (two elements of one guest set or map with equal keys, linked by\na chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells at a fixpoint), separates \"not\ncertified by this procedure\" from \"non-interchangeable\" with examples\non each side, withdraws the rarity claim, and says the real-state rate\nis unmeasured because the repository has no version-3 task corpus (Stage\nE scenarios and the v2 handoff census tests hold cursor cells but no v3\nlift exists before 6b). `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` now numbers (root `92747c0f…`,\nequal to 6a's). Verified by a permissive diagnostic mode in the\nprototype (D5 replaced by host-order entry, 24 shuffles): the loop plus\n2-cycle, the 2-cycle and the 3-cycle give one root (interchangeable,\nrefused anyway); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` gives 2 roots and the\nseven-cell state 4 roots (genuinely need a tie-break). Fixture census:\nof the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\nD5. Random census under a tie-biased generator: 25 of 2000 value states\n(15 witnessed non-interchangeable, 10 not distinguished by 12 shuffles),\n8 of 200 frame-bearing, 2 of 100 with children; the note reports these\nas rates under that distribution, not as a claim about real states.\n\n**4. HIGH, decode.** Changed: §2 \"Validation and decode\" specifies that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\nraised by the recomputation is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable` of the\nsame kind (path prefixed by the root id), resource refusals propagate as\nthemselves, no other non-portable kind can arise there because step 5\nran, and the validator order (after step 5, before `validate-frames`)\nstays; A4 carries it into carrier §8 step 6 and the diagnostics\nparagraph. Prototype `decode*` implements the translation and the\ncomparison. Unverified: the prototype's recompute runs after 6a's\n`decode*` returns (so after `validate-frames`), because 6a's `decode*` is\nprivate and monolithic; the note states the required order and the\nacceptance item 16 tests it.\n\n**5. MEDIUM, prototype follows the spec.** Changed: the prototype is\nschema-driven (one `enter-node!`/`spell-node` pair over `row-table`\ndescriptors plus two synthetic kinds, `:entry` and `:component`);\nnumbered cells spell `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]` with `k` an integer and\nlocal ones `[:cursor-cell [:u j body]]`; parked-table values are entered\nthrough their `[:ref :act :tail]` descriptor (the old `:V` bug is gone);\ninstall children are numbered by the same procedure in the prototype's\nown emitter; `:window` is entered in numeric register order. New\nfixtures: act window, K-frame window, environment matched to the free\nenvironment, and a parked act at a park site (code from the frame tests'\n`:park` segment), all decodable. Search: 2000 random value states with\nnested sets, sets of pairs including `[X X]`, cursor-cell map keys and\ncells aliased across containers, plus 200 frame-bearing and 100\nwith-children states; renaming every cursor cell and shuffling every\nset, map and census: **0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures**,\n2033 decode round trips ok, 232 not decodable through 6a's own\nrecompute under its default budget (the probe decodes through 6a's\n`decode*`, which still runs 6a's numbering; a probe limitation, not a\nnumbering outcome), 35 refusals. The decode round trip caught one real\nprototype bug during revision: a memoized local run returned the whole\nnumbering of another element's context, so an unrelated cursor cell was\nadopted and shifted the provisional indices (seed 1016 gave two roots\nover 40 shuffles). The memo now returns the numbering restricted to the\nitem's own cells; the note's \"Memoization\" paragraph records the\nrequirement. Also fixed in the generator during revision: reference-free\nvectors, and set elements equal under decoded equality, both refused by\ndecode and both generator artifacts.\n\n**6. MEDIUM, \"linear work\".** Changed: §2 \"Work\" states\n`W = O(P · (d + 1) · (n + 1) · log m)` with memoized local runs (`P`\nexpanded positions, counted per reference so DAG expansion is bounded by\n`P` rather than the row count; `d` container depth; `n` cursor cells;\n`m` largest container) and `O(P · (n + 1)^(d+1) · log m)` without, and\nmakes `:work` charge one unit per position visited or spelled and one\nper literal byte copied, so every spelling byte is paid for before\nallocation; `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`, `:rows` and `:bytes` stay and\ntogether bound what a spelling can copy; `:search` and `:label-bytes`\ngo. The recommendation line now says \"work bounded and accounted\" instead\nof \"linear work\". A5 updates carrier §10.10's wording accordingly.\nUnverified: the bound is argued, not measured; the prototype does not\ncharge budgets.\n\n**7. MEDIUM, stale wording.** Changed: §0 defines canonical traversal\norder versus host enumeration order; A2 now replaces the whole tail of\nthe Validation paragraph including \"no encounter-order tie-break ever\ndecides validity\" and \"never falls back to encounter order\"; A5 replaces\n§10.10's \"never falls back to encounter order\" and answer 9's\n\"refinement-and-search\"; A8 gives three explicit replacements for\ncontinuation §5.5 (the body-label sentence with its \"step 1\" reference,\nthe \"sorting their elements' encodings (step 2)\" sentence, and a\nqualification of \"never derived from traversal order\" to *host*\ntraversal order); A4 corrects the \"two kinds\" counts in carrier §8 and\n§11 and states the `[:child]` and integer `[:n k]` changes in A1.\n\n**8. LOW.** Changed: the portable-id example holds the id counter equal\n(task B consumes one unrelated fresh name), and the paragraph concedes\nthe review's point that an id recomputed from structure is the\ncanonicalization problem again; engine citations are given against\nbranch `srvm-phase6a` (`gen-id` 227–230, `issue-ref` 311–323,\n`handle-cursor-unminted` 557–568, `gensym` 2312–2318, the `:stream/cursor`\neffect site 2595–2602, read with `git show` on the branch); D1 and the\nF1 table say `:window` is in numeric ascending register order as the\ncarrier specifies.\n\n## Pinned ids, old → new\n\n- Unchanged from the first draft and from 6a: `fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n  `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `536c810b…`, ten-cell root\n  `6e7c7a6b…`.\n- Unchanged from the first draft (differ from 6a): `a3713a5e…`,\n  `4993b5b1…`.\n- First draft gave two roots depending on enumeration → one root:\n  review A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` `65ba4530…`; review B `{X X, Y Z}` `409548a7…`.\n- First draft refused → numbered: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` `92747c0f…`.\n- First draft left unnumbered (emitted source ids) → numbered: review C\n  `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` `18b70de6…`.\n- New pins: `[#{X Y} X]` `7dc3d5c4…`; `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` `6ea03f9b…`;\n  `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` `b35b8e12…`.\n- Still refused: loop plus 2-cycle (6a's `55ff168e…` stays removed; `L`\n  now gets `cc-1` before the refusal), seven-cell.\n- Every numbering was derived by hand in note §6 and the digest taken\n  from the codec under that numbering; A, B, C and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`\n  agree with an independent computation (codex's, or 6a's).\n\n## Unverified or disputed\n\n- No codex counterexample is disputed; all three reproduced on the first\n  prototype and are fixed.\n- The prototype's decode check runs after 6a's frame validation\n  (item 4); the real codec must place it at step 6.\n- Three-host runs, kondo, budgets charging: not run or not implemented\n  (documents and a JVM scratch prototype only).\n- The real-state rate of D5 is unmeasured (no v3 task corpus; item 3).\n- The work bound is argued, not measured (item 6).\n- Uncommitted changes in the p6a worktree, if any, remain invisible.\n\n## Owner questions\n\nUnchanged from the first report, restated in note §9: accept the D5\nclass as documented (recommended yes); refusal kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` rather than `:resource-limit` (recommended).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
