{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **Proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-12 (Architect,\n     4\tclaude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the problem is not\n     5\tthat the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity is local\", and\n     6\tthe orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by **discovery order**\n     7\tin a canonical traversal. **Revised 2026-10-12** after the independent\n     8\treview (gpt-6.1-sol, `collab/1792140000000-…findings.md`), which broke\n     9\tthe first draft in two places: the orbit lemma was unsound (equal\n    10\tcontent encodings did not imply interchangeable elements: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`)\n    11\tand nested tie groups were never processed (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). The\n    12\trule, the proof, the refusal class, the decode path, the work bound, the\n    13\tamendments and the prototype are rewritten below; every pinned id that\n    14\tmoved is listed in §6. Subordinate to\n    15\t[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\n    16\t(the carrier, body version 3) and\n    17\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\n    18\tvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\n    19\tcell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\n    20\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\n    21\t(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\n    22\tcode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Recommendation in one line:** adopt discovery-order numbering now (§7,\n    25\toption a): a function of structure, renaming- and enumeration-invariant,\n    26\tprovable by hand (§3), with work bounded and accounted before allocation\n    27\t(§2 \"Work\"); it deletes carrier §6's refinement and search, keeps every\n    28\tpinned root id except the one state only the search could number, and\n    29\trefuses a precisely described class (D5) that it cannot certify.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 0. Vocabulary used below\n    32\t\n    33\t- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\n    34\t  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed\n    35\t  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\n    36\t  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\n    37\t  accident of the host and the procedure must never let it decide a\n    38\t  number. The first draft's amendments blurred the two; §4 keeps them\n    39\t  apart.\n    40\t- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\n    41\t  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\n    42\t- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\n    43\t  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\n    44\t  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\n    45\t- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\n    46\t  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\n    47\t  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\n    48\t  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one element (or\n    49\t  one component, D4) from the enclosing run's current numbering; it\n    50\t  exists only to compute a sort key and is discarded.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 1. Findings\n    53\t\n    54\t**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\n    55\t(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\n    56\tcursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\n    57\t\n    58\t| Position | Order | Canonical index |\n    59\t|---|---|---|\n    60\t| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\n    61\t| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\n    62\t| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\n    63\t| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\n    64\t| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |\n    65\t| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\n    66\t| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\n    67\t| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\n    68\t| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\n    69\t\n    70\tSo the environment's key order is canonical (bytes of the symbol as\n    71\tspelled, the order `:bindings` rows are emitted in), and the only\n    72\tunordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest `:map` entries.\n    73\tThe review confirmed the inventory complete. The example state in\n    74\t`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one cursor cell\n    75\t(`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's environment under\n    76\t`c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next` pending's\n    77\t`cursor-cell-C` slot.\n    78\t\n    79\t**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\n    80\tstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\n    81\tit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\n    82\tfixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first\n    83\tposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\n    84\tnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\n    85\texisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\n    86\torder is a total order by construction.\n    87\t\n    88\t**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\n    89\tcarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\n    90\tcell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike and the\n    91\treview's counterexample followed. The repaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\")\n    92\tnumbers the cursor cells *inside* an element provisionally, in the\n    93\telement's own discovery order, relative to the enclosing numbering: `[X X]`\n    94\tspells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology\n    95\tinside an element is part of its sort key, as a de Bruijn index is part\n    96\tof a term. The key of an element is the spelling produced by a *local\n    97\trun* over it, which applies the same rules recursively (nested\n    98\tcontainers sorted, nested ties deferred and re-examined, D4 inside) and\n    99\tspells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`. Equal keys form a **tie\n   100\tgroup**, deferred to the run's worklist and re-examined to a fixpoint\n   101\t(D3) as other positions assign numbers. No key ever grows beyond the\n   102\tsize of its element's expansion.\n   103\t\n   104\t**F4. The residue, the component rule, and the refusal predicate.** What\n   105\tsurvives the fixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor\n   106\tcells occur only inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they\n   107\tshare an unnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n   108\t**component**. Two cases:\n   109\t- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n   110\t  component is then encoded by a local run over its elements in group\n   111\t  order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, spells its\n   112\t  shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by that\n   113\t  encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n   114\t  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components, and the\n   115\t  entered state is the same up to isomorphism whichever order the host\n   116\t  lists them in (§3, lemma C). `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\n   117\t  `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n   118\t- *Not certified* (D5): some component holds two elements of one tie\n   119\t  group, at some fixpoint of some run. Then entering either element first\n   120\t  is a choice this procedure cannot show to be harmless, and it refuses.\n   121\t  This is **\"not certified by this procedure\"**, not \"non-interchangeable\":\n   122\t  `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` are refused\n   123\t  although every entering order gives the same rows (the swap and the\n   124\t  rotation are automorphisms); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the\n   125\t  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused\n   126\t  and genuinely need a tie-break (§6 shows two orders with different\n   127\t  roots). The loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n   128\t  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) now numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and\n   129\t  refuses on `{A, B}`.\n   130\t  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused iff, at a\n   131\t  fixpoint, two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are\n   132\t  connected by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied\n   133\t  elements.* Such a state needs two or more cursor cells with equal bodies\n   134\t  that are held *only* through a guest set or map whose elements mention\n   135\t  more than one of them each. How often real states do that is **not\n   136\t  measured**: the repository has no version-3 task corpus yet (the Stage E\n   137\t  scenarios and the v2 handoff census tests hold cursor cells but no v3\n   138\t  lift exists before 6b), so §6 reports only the fixture inventory (two\n   139\t  of the repository's fixtures hit D5, both built to) and the random\n   140\t  generator's rate under a tie-biased distribution. The first draft's\n   141\t  rarity claim is withdrawn. D5 can be narrowed later by a bounded search\n   142\t  over the residue without changing the numbering of any state D1–D4\n   143\t  number today, because D1–D4's decisions precede it.\n   144\t\n   145\t**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\n   146\trefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\n   147\tof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\n   148\tcensus), terminating, with work bounded by §2 \"Work\".\n   149\t\n   150\t**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\n   151\tcursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\n   152\tmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\n   153\tsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\n   154\tframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\n   155\tstate) and 100 states with install children: the prototype gave equal\n   156\troot ids after renaming every cursor cell and shuffling every set, map\n   157\tand census, with no failure; every numbered carrier decoded through 6a's\n   158\tdecoder and recomputed to its own root; it refused 35 of 2300. The\n   159\treview's three counterexamples number canonically; the pinned fixture\n   160\troot `fab223…` and the ten-cell census root are unchanged; the new pins\n   161\tfor the counterexamples equal the digests the review computed\n   162\tindependently for the same numbering.\n   163\t\n   164\t**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n   165\t\n   166\t## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n   167\t\n   168\tInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\n   169\tcells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\n   170\ttask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\n   171\trefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\n   172\trule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n   173\t\n   174\t**Body label.** `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, the\n   175\tcursor cell's census body as plain data.\n   176\t\n   177\t**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\n   178\ttraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\n   179\tnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\n   180\trun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\n   181\tenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j <body label>]]`\n   182\tand a cursor cell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it.\n   183\tNothing else changes from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself,\n   184\t`[:row B]` expanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n   185\t`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (this deliberately\n   186\treplaces step 2's `[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a\n   187\tname-keyed install entry, never inside an unordered container, so its\n   188\tcontent never decides an order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed\n   189\tstructural rows are spelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in\n   190\tnumeric register order; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise\n   191\tsorted vector of its elements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`),\n   192\tduplicates retained, where a tie group the run could not resolve is\n   193\tspelled in place as `[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element).\n   194\tAll comparisons are bytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic\n   195\tvectors. `CE` is hash-free and linear in the expansion of its argument.\n   196\t\n   197\t**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\n   198\trow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n   199\t(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\n   200\tkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n   201\t`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n   202\t§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\n   203\tspecifies (the same as byte order for `N`, but the rule is numeric). The\n   204\tcensus is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is not entered. A\n   205\t`:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a **key**: the\n   206\tspelling of a local run over the element under the numbering at the\n   207\tmoment the container is entered (every element keyed from that one\n   208\tnumbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\n   209\tequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\n   210\torder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\n   211\tcontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\n   212\tan entry's key before its value.\n   213\t\n   214\t**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n   215\t`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\n   216\tcell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\n   217\tcounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\n   218\tlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\n   219\tenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\n   220\tno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n   221\t`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\n   222\tcontents table, so content rows keep sharing by Merkle id whether or not\n   223\tthey hold a cursor cell's first occurrence.\n   224\t\n   225\t**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\n   226\tre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\n   227\tthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\n   228\tre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\n   229\tcell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\n   230\tit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (the review's\n   231\t`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`) joins the same worklist and is re-examined in the\n   232\tnext round; there is one worklist per run, and the root run's worklist\n   233\tcollects deferred groups from every depth.\n   234\t\n   235\t**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\n   236\tlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\n   237\tcell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\n   238\tfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\n   239\tso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) If no component holds\n   240\ttwo elements of one group: encode each component by a local run over the\n   241\tsynthetic ordered row `[:component g₁ e₁ g₂ e₂ …]` of its elements in\n   242\tgroup order (`gᵢ` the group's deferral sequence number, a datum), require\n   243\tthat run to be complete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling, else D5), sort the\n   244\tcomponents by their encodings, and **enter the run of least encoding**:\n   245\teach of its components has its local run's cursor cells numbered, in that\n   246\trun's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's numbering;\n   247\tcomponents of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3, lemma C:\n   248\tequal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same). Remove the\n   249\tentered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group whose\n   250\telements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\n   251\tcarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\n   252\thost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n   253\t\n   254\t**D5. Refusal.** If at any fixpoint of any run a component holds two\n   255\telements of one group (directly, or inside a component's local run), the\n   256\tlift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\n   257\t`:yin.k/path` the row path of the container holding that group, and a\n   258\t`:yin.k/cursor-cells` list of the component's unnumbered cursor cells.\n   259\tA receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing refuses `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   260\tkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is a resource refusal and neither\n   261\tis `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n   262\t\n   263\t**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\n   264\tBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\n   265\tbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\n   266\tdefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\n   267\trefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n   268\t\n   269\t**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\n   270\tlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\n   271\trestricted to the item's cursor cells, and of the enclosing local\n   272\tcounter; an implementation may memoize on that key and must return the\n   273\tnumbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the prototype's one\n   274\tbug during revision was returning the whole numbering of another\n   275\telement's context, which the decode round trip caught).\n   276\t\n   277\t**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\n   278\tthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\n   279\ton mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n   280\t(`rows.cljc:570-575`); it must be wrapped so that **only** a lift-side\n   281\t`:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` raised by the\n   282\trecomputation is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n   283\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the root id;\n   284\ta resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\n   285\titself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\n   286\talready checked literals and markers. The validator order stays: step 6\n   287\t(numbering) after step 5 and before `validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`;\n   288\ttest `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`).\n   289\t\n   290\t**Work.** Let `P` be the number of expanded positions of the task (the\n   291\ttree after expanding every `[:row B]` and structural reference in place;\n   292\ta shared row counts once per reference, so `P` can exceed the row count\n   293\tand the implementation must bound it, not the row count), `d` the\n   294\tcontainer nesting depth, `n` the number of cursor cells, `m` the largest\n   295\telement count of one container. Then, with memoized local runs, the\n   296\tprocedure visits each position at most `(n + 1)` times per nesting level\n   297\t(a local run over an element is recomputed only when the numbering\n   298\trestricted to that element's cursor cells changes, at most `n` times),\n   299\tevery encoding is linear in its element's expansion, and each container\n   300\tsort costs `O(B · log m)` compare work for `B` key bytes; so\n   301\t`W = O(P · (d + 1) · (n + 1) · log m)` positions-and-bytes, and the bytes\n   302\tmaterialized at any time are `O(W)` plus the memo, itself `O(P · (d + 1)\n   303\t· (n + 1))`. Without memoization the bound is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d+1) · log m)`.\n   304\tBoth are accounted before allocation by the budgets that stay: `:work`\n   305\tis charged one unit per position visited or spelled **and** one unit per\n   306\tliteral byte copied into a spelling (so every allocated spelling byte\n   307\thas been paid for), `:depth` bounds `d` and the structural depth as\n   308\ttoday, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` bounds the carried rows and\n   309\t`:bytes` the carried bytes, which together bound every literal a spelling\n   310\tcan copy. The `:search` and `:label-bytes` budgets disappear with the\n   311\tsearch and the growing labels. Exceeding a budget is the resource refusal\n   312\tof carrier §10.10, never a canonicality verdict.\n   313\t\n   314\t## 3. Proof of canonicality\n   315\t\n   316\tCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n   317\t**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\n   318\tother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\n   319\torder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n   320\t(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\n   321\tLet `S` be an abstract task.\n   322\t\n   323\t**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\n   324\tof a local run over it, and a local run reads only structure, body\n   325\tlabels and numbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order\n   326\tinside nested containers, which it never uses except through keys, by\n   327\tinduction on nesting depth, and (ii) the host order of components of\n   328\tequal encoding in its D4 step, covered by lemma C. So isomorphic items\n   329\tunder corresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted\n   330\torder of a container's elements is a function of the configuration up\n   331\tto isomorphism.\n   332\t\n   333\t**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\n   334\tconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n   335\t\n   336\t**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\n   337\tof one run of equal encodings, none holding two elements of one group,\n   338\tand let `U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are\n   339\tpairwise disjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of\n   340\tan unnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\n   341\tEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ e₁ …]` rows are a faithful\n   342\tspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\n   343\tindex and body, groups by sequence number), so they exhibit, for each\n   344\tpair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a bijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the\n   345\tsame structure group by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives\n   346\tthe cells of `C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index;\n   347\tin order `σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The\n   348\tblock-swap bijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an\n   349\tisomorphism of the two resulting configurations: numbers agree by\n   350\tconstruction, structures agree because the swapped components are\n   351\tisomorphic and nothing outside them mentions their cells, and the\n   352\tworklists agree because the same groups remain, with the same elements\n   353\tremoved. ∎\n   354\t\n   355\t**Lemma D (refusal is invariant).** Whether a component holds two\n   356\telements of one group depends only on which tied elements share\n   357\tunnumbered cursor cells, which is a function of the configuration up to\n   358\tisomorphism; and whether a component's local run is complete is, by\n   359\tlemma A applied to that run, likewise invariant.\n   360\t\n   361\t**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\n   362\tsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\n   363\trows. *Proof.* By induction on the number of steps. D1 and D2 decisions\n   364\tare functions of the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only\n   365\trepeats D1 on deferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the\n   366\tleast run is canonical (lemma A on component encodings) and its only\n   367\tlisted-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant (lemma D).\n   368\tEmission spells cursor cells by number only and sorts every emitted\n   369\tunordered slot by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5),\n   370\tso isomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n   371\t\n   372\tConsequences:\n   373\t\n   374\t1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\n   375\t   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\n   376\t   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\n   377\t   point lemma C covers.\n   378\t2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\n   379\t   3), so every traversal is finite. In the root run each D3 round that\n   380\t   is not the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least\n   381\t   one cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; local runs are finite\n   382\t   by the same argument one level down and there are finitely many\n   383\t   nesting levels. So the procedure ends after at most `n` numbering\n   384\t   events per run plus one terminal round, within the bound of §2 \"Work\".\n   385\t3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n   386\t   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n   387\t4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n   388\t   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\n   389\t   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\n   390\t   position get two numbers.\n   391\t5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n   392\t   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n   393\t   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\n   394\t\n   395\tWhat is given up: the procedure does not number every state. D5 names\n   396\tthe refused class exactly (§1 F4); a later bounded search over that\n   397\tresidue would not change the numbering of any state D1–D4 number today,\n   398\tso the refusal can be narrowed without churning ids.\n   399\t\n   400\t## 4. Amendments\n   401\t\n   402\t**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\n   403\twith §2 above (vocabulary, the body label, the spelling, D1–D5, totality,\n   404\twork). Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend\n   405\t\"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering\n   406\tis defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is\n   407\ta function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\n   408\tenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\n   409\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\n   410\tminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. A state in which two\n   411\tequal-keyed elements of one guest set or map are linked by shared\n   412\tunnumbered cursor cells at a fixpoint is refused (D5), not searched; the\n   413\trefusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and can be narrowed\n   414\tlater without changing any numbering assigned today.\" State in step 2's\n   415\treplacement that `[:n k]` is an exact CBOR integer and that `[:child]`\n   416\treplaces `[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").\n   417\t\n   418\t**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\n   419\tsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\n   420\tthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\n   421\tto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\n   422\tthereby accepted. The procedure reads the canonical traversal order of\n   423\tD1, which is a function of the state; it never reads the host's\n   424\tenumeration order of a set, map or census, and a receiver never decides\n   425\tvalidity by its own enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural\n   426\trefusal, not a lift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is\n   427\texceeded before the procedure completes refuses with the structured\n   428\tresource refusal before any attachment, never labels the state\n   429\t`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never substitutes host enumeration order,\n   430\tand never accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches D5\n   431\trefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"\n   432\t\n   433\t**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6\n   434\tare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\n   435\tof an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\n   436\tused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\n   437\tan emitted row\".\n   438\t\n   439\t**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound and\n   440\tcompare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`;\n   441\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached,\n   442\ttranslated from the recomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only;\n   443\tor the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded, which\n   444\tpropagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics paragraph after step 12,\n   445\tadd `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds named,\n   446\tand change \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set:\n   447\t`:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds this carrier adds to\n   448\tUCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`, `:metadata` and\n   449\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\". In §11, \"two lift kinds and two\n   450\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" becomes \"three lift kinds and three\n   451\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\".\n   452\t\n   453\t**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\n   454\trefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\n   455\tis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\n   456\ton decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\n   457\tand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\n   458\ttraversal depth, and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work`\n   459\tunit counts positions and copied literal bytes, so spellings are paid\n   460\tfor before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls back to encounter\n   461\torder\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for the canonical\n   462\ttraversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\" → \"as defined\n   463\tby discovery order, and the D5 refusal class\".\n   464\t\n   465\t**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\n   466\tcanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\n   467\ttraversal)\".\n   468\t\n   469\t**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6\n   470\tprocedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n   471\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n   472\t\n   473\t**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is\n   474\tinvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\n   475\tan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\n   476\tthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n   477\t(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\n   478\tin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n   479\t**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\n   480\tother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\n   481\tbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\n   482\tas amended) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers\n   483\t(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\n   484\tsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\n   485\thost traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\n   486\torder of their elements' spellings, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the\n   487\tparked table in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 D1),\n   488\tnever in host enumeration order\". (iii) Leave \"never derived from\n   489\ttraversal order\" but qualify it: \"never derived from *host* traversal\n   490\torder (the canonical traversal of draft §6 D1 is a function of the\n   491\tstate and is what the procedure reads)\".\n   492\t\n   493\t**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–21\n   494\tof §8 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\n   495\t\n   496\t**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within\n   497\tone lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.\n   498\t\n   499\t## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\n   500\t\n   501\tThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\n   502\tcreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\n   503\tmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\n   504\t(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\n   505\tcounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\n   506\tas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\n   507\texecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\n   508\t`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\n   509\tsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\n   510\tcounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\n   511\tstructure:\n   512\t\n   513\t- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\n   514\t  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\n   515\t  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\n   516\t  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\n   517\t  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\n   518\t  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\n   519\t  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\n   520\t  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\n   521\t  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\n   522\t  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\n   523\t- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\n   524\t  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\n   525\t  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\n   526\t  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\n   527\t- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\n   528\t  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\n   529\t  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\n   530\t  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\n   531\t  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\n   532\t  relative to its state.\n   533\t\n   534\tDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\n   535\tidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\n   536\tn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\n   537\tposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\n   538\tids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\n   539\tonly kind proposed.\n   540\t\n   541\t## 6. Prototype\n   542\t\n   543\t`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\n   544\tscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D5 as\n   545\tone schema-driven run procedure (root run and local runs share the code;\n   546\ta local run differs only in its number tag and in spelling residues as\n   547\t`[:tie]`), memoized as §2 describes, with the totality assertion, an\n   548\temitter that numbers install children by the same procedure, a decode\n   549\tpath that runs 6a's `decode*` with its numbering check off and then\n   550\trecomputes, compares and translates only `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, and\n   551\ta permissive diagnostic mode that replaces D5 by host-order entry to\n   552\ttest whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable. Run from the\n   553\tprobe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private helpers):\n   554\t\n   555\t```\n   556\tclojure -M -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n   557\t```\n   558\t\n   559\tResults of 2026-10-12 (revised prototype; 6a tests pass in the same\n   560\tproject, 21 tests, 324 assertions; full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`):\n   561\t\n   562\t| State | Discovery order | 6a |\n   563\t|---|---|---|\n   564\t| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same root |\n   565\t| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | root `2d9873a8…` |\n   566\t| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same root |\n   567\t| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same roots |\n   568\t| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | `71543fe2…` / same |\n   569\t| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest for this numbering) | `387f294a…` |\n   570\t| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | `be62ba43…` |\n   571\t| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same root |\n   572\t| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same root |\n   573\t| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | `a850fce6…` |\n   574\t| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | `e61d3709…` |\n   575\t| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (the first element has two cursor cells, the second four); root `b35b8e12…` | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n   576\t| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells` on `{A, B}` | root `55ff168e…` |\n   577\t| 2-cycle, 3-cycle | refused; permissive mode gives one root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | numbered |\n   578\t| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | numbered |\n   579\t| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive mode gives 4 roots (not interchangeable) | refused `:resource-limit [:label-bytes]` |\n   580\t| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same root |\n   581\t| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3` (the wait's pending); decodes | different root (6a numbers by its own search) |\n   582\t| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1` (slot 8 precedes frames, slot 13), `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | different root |\n   583\t| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2` by discovery order; one child row shared; decodes | — |\n   584\t| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (15 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 10 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation) | — |\n   585\t| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget | — |\n   586\t| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget | — |\n   587\t\n   588\tHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\n   589\tfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\n   590\treview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\n   591\tFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\n   592\tkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\n   593\t(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\n   594\t`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) sorts before\n   595\t`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\n   596\tcounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 b]` twice) and is\n   597\tdeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\n   598\tagainst `[:u 0 b]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 b] [u0 b]]`\n   599\tand `[:vec [u0 b] [u1 b]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\n   600\tis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\n   601\tentries `[:entry [u0 b] [u0 b]]` and `[:entry [u0 b] [u1 b]]` likewise.\n   602\tReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 b] [u1 b]]` (each local run\n   603\tresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\n   604\tthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\n   605\tcomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\n   606\teither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\n   607\tand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\n   608\tequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 b] 2 [u0 b]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\n   609\tin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\n   610\tmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. Loop plus\n   611\t2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 b] [u0 b]`, the two others as `[u0 b] [u1 b]`,\n   612\tso `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}` ties, the fixpoint changes\n   613\tnothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}` holds two elements of one\n   614\tgroup: D5. The example state of `collab/example-suspended-state.clj`\n   615\thas one cursor cell, first met in the act's environment under `c`, so\n   616\t`cc-1`; a one-cell state has the same rows under both procedures.\n   617\t\n   618\tPins that moved from the first draft: none of the first draft's pins\n   619\tchange (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`, `a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n   620\t`49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, the ten-cell root); the first\n   621\tdraft refused `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` (now `92747c0f…`), numbered review A and\n   622\tB inconsistently (now `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`) and left review C\n   623\tunnumbered (now `18b70de6…`); `7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…` and `b35b8e12…`\n   624\tare new pins.\n   625\t\n   626\t## 7. Recommendation\n   627\t\n   628\t**(a) Adopt discovery order now.** 6b has not started and 6a is\n   629\tunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\n   630\ttests plus the amendments of §4: delete `check-label-bytes!`\n   631\t(`rows.cljc:212-230`), `positions` (`:285-325`), `classes` (`:326-330`),\n   632\t`refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`) and the label seeding\n   633\t(`:252-255`); replace `ce` (`:257-284`) by the spelling of §2; add the\n   634\trun procedure (traversal, worklist, fixpoint, components, D5, totality\n   635\tassertion, memo) and the decode translation (the prototype's procedure\n   636\tis about 230 lines with the schema helpers; 150–200 in place, portable\n   637\t`.cljc`: no `@#'` reflection, `mapv`/`into`/`reduce` over long sequences,\n   638\t`contains?` for membership, continuation §12); delete the `:search` and\n   639\t`:label-bytes` budget keys (`:200-202`) and charge `:work` per position\n   640\tand per copied literal byte. Unchanged: `emit`, `emit-numbered`,\n   641\t`canonical-under`, the census and its numeric order (`:140-156`),\n   642\t`decode*`'s recompute-and-compare (`:570-575`) apart from the\n   643\ttranslation, numbering before `validate-frames` (`:576-578`; test\n   644\t`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`), the `:bytes`,\n   645\t`:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells` and `:work` bounds. Tests: see §8.\n   646\tCost: one implementation round and one review round, and the review can\n   647\tcheck §3 by hand. Risk: a canonicality bug, mitigated by §8 item 18's\n   648\tgenerator in CI (it caught the prototype's memo bug), by the decode round\n   649\ttrip in that generator, and by the proof; and the D5 class, documented\n   650\texactly and narrowable later without id churn.\n   651\t\n   652\t**(b) Keep the 6a design.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine\n   653\tharness and every reviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search,\n   654\tlabels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte, and a\n   655\tdefinition that is \"whatever the procedure outputs\". Not recommended.\n   656\t\n   657\t**(c) Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the\n   658\tharness (§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\n   659\t\n   660\t## 8. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n   661\t\n   662\t12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\n   663\t    the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]` and the\n   664\t    two frame fixtures of §6, `canonical-numbering` returns exactly the\n   665\t    maps of §6.\n   666\t13. `rows-test/review-counterexample-resolves-by-fixpoint`: `'a #{X Y}`,\n   667\t    `'b X` numbers `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; the task renamed and with the set\n   668\t    reversed gives the same root; the root is pinned `a3713a5e…`.\n   669\t14. `rows-test/component-rule`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\n   670\t    `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` number without refusal;\n   671\t    element reversal and renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`,\n   672\t    `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`.\n   673\t15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n   674\t    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n   675\t    pinned `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`; `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}`\n   676\t    numbers and is pinned `b35b8e12…`.\n   677\t16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the loop plus 2-cycle,\n   678\t    the 2-cycle, the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell state refuse\n   679\t    at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\n   680\t    a path naming the set and the component's cursor cells; a carrier of\n   681\t    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n   682\t    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a\n   683\t    carrier of the loop plus 2-cycle with a `{:work 1}` budget refuses\n   684\t    `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`, not `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\n   685\t17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n   686\t    unchanged; `a3713a5e…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, `7dc3d5c4…`,\n   687\t    `6ea03f9b…` added; `55ff168e…` removed.\n   688\t18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (one to six\n   689\t    cursor cells over two bodies; nested `:vec`, `:set`, `:map` with\n   690\t    cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including `[X X]`, sets of\n   691\t    sets, under keyed bindings and the result; unique elements and keys\n   692\t    under decoded equality) over at least 2000 states, plus at least 200\n   693\t    frame-bearing states (cursor cells in an act window, a K-frame\n   694\t    window, environments matched to the free environment, and a parked\n   695\t    state at a park site) and at least 100 states with install children:\n   696\t    every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its root, and\n   697\t    renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and census\n   698\t    gives an equal root id; refusals are counted and reported.\n   699\t19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a task whose census declares a\n   700\t    cursor cell the traversal does not reach raises the totality defect\n   701\t    (not a refusal) before any row is emitted.\n   702\t20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n   703\t    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n   704\t    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n   705\t    and `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n   706\t    large-input regression of `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n   707\t    is kept as `large-literals-do-not-spend-work-beyond-their-bytes`\n   708\t    without its label-budget expectation.\n   709\t21. Budgets and docs: the `:search` and `:label-bytes` keys are gone;\n   710\t    `{:work 1}` still refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`; A1–A9\n   711\t    applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node\n   712\t    log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n   713\t\n   714\t## 9. Owner questions\n   715\t\n   716\t1. Is the D5 class, as stated in §1 F4 (exact predicate, no rarity\n   717\t   claim, unmeasured on real states until 6b lifts one), acceptable as a\n   718\t   documented limitation of body version 3 (recommended: yes; it can be\n   719\t   narrowed later by a bounded search over the residue without changing\n   720\t   any id numbered today)?\n   721\t2. Should the refusal kind be `:unresolved-cursor-cells` as proposed,\n   722\t   or the orchestrator's `:resource-limit` (recommended against: it is\n   723\t   not a budget, and carrier §10.10 says a resource refusal never\n   724\t   stands in for a canonicality verdict)?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
