{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '455,605p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '25,195p' collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '910,940p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1660,1688p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; nl -ba collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc | sed -n '560,582p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '455,605p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '25,195p' collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '910,940p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1660,1688p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; nl -ba collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc | sed -n '560,582p'\"","aggregated_output":"\nCursor-cell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\ntwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cursor cell's number must depend on\neverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\nelsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\nholding cursor cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\nlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\ncan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\ntopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\nwhich any algorithm is checked.\n\n**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\nis defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\nof the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\ncursor cell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cursor cells of\nthe input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\nminimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\ndefinition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\nit (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\ncursor cells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\nequal-body cursor cells leaves all seven cursor cells in one class, and no\nautomorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\nprocedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\npruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\nprocedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\nsymmetry argument.\n\n**The procedure.** Its input is the abstract task: the structure of\n§5 with cursor cells as identities, before any numbering. Every encoding below\nis canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) of a *synthetic vector\nstructure*; vectors are used throughout, never host maps or sets, so\nthat elements or keys that become equal after class substitution keep\ntheir multiplicity. All byte comparisons are bytewise on those CBOR\nbytes. Let the cursor cells be `n` in number; if `n = 0` the procedure returns\nthe empty numbering and performs no search.\n\n1. **Class labels** are CBOR byte strings. Wherever a label is embedded\n   in a larger encoding it is embedded as a CBOR byte string (major\n   type 2), never re-parsed. A cursor cell's initial label is the CBOR of\n   `[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`.\n2. **Class encoding** `CE(x)` of any encoded value or row, structural\n   and hash-free. **Every identity-bearing position encodes the cursor cell's\n   current label, never its incoming id**; the identity positions are\n   exactly: `[:cursor-cell C]` values, the bare `cursor-cell-C` and `response-cursor-cell-C`\n   slots of `:pending` rows, and the keys of the `:cursor-cells` census.\n   - `[:lit d]` → `[:lit d]`.\n   - `[:cursor-cell C]`, and a bare `C` slot → `[:cursor-cell <label of C>]` (full\n     tag retained, §10.9).\n   - `[:row B]` → `[:row <CE(row B)>]`, the row expanded in place; the\n     expansion is finite because the structural edge family is acyclic\n     (§8 step 3).\n   - A row `[kind s₁ … sₖ]` → `[kind CE(s₁) … CE(sₖ)]` with each slot\n     encoded as: a structural datum (`A`, `M`, `P`, `S`, `N`, `D`, a\n     reason, a phase, a version, a role, a kind) as itself; a `C` as\n     above; a `V` as above; a cref as the expanded row; a crefs vector\n     as the vector of expansions.\n   - For `:set` rows, and for the key-value pairs of `:map`, `:store`\n     and `:bindings` rows, the entries of `:parked-table`, and the\n     installs and module-stores slots of the root: encode each element\n     (or each `[key value]` pair as a two-element vector), then **sort\n     the resulting vector of encodings bytewise, retaining duplicates**.\n     Ordered rows (`:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) and the ordered\n     `frames` slot keep their order.\n   - The `:cursor-cells` census is **not expanded**: in `CE(root)` the\n     `cursor-cells-cref` slot is the constant `[:cursor-cells]`. The census is a\n     derived index of the cursor cells the task reaches, so it carries no\n     additional information beyond the reached cursor cell identities and\n     their bodies (which already seed the initial labels), and\n     (consistently with\n     §8 step 3, which excludes census declaration edges from\n     reachability) **census declaration occurrences contribute no\n     positions** to any cursor cell.\n   - Context lookups (`P` in `:parked-ref` and `parked-id`, `M` in\n     `store-of`) are not expanded; they appear as the plain datum.\n   - An install child's `:task` row is **not expanded**: children are\n     numbered first, as their own tasks with their own cursor cells (task\n     boundary; this runs bottom-up inside §8 step 6 and needs no\n     restoration), and a child cref appears in the parent's encodings\n     as `[:child <child root id>]`, the child's already-canonical root\n     id as a byte string.\n3. **Positions.** A position is the CBOR of the path from the task's\n   root to one identity-bearing occurrence of a cursor cell: a vector of\n   steps, built by descending from the root and appending exactly one\n   step per slot category crossed, with the occurrence itself\n   contributing no step. The categories, and their one spelling each:\n   - *Fixed slot* (any tabled slot that is not one of the categories\n     below, including a `:pending` row's `cursor-cell-C` slots):\n     `[kind slot-index]`, the slot's 0-based index in the row table.\n   - *Ordered repeated entry* (`:vec`, `:list` elements; `:k`\n     frames; the root's `frames`): `[kind i]` with `i` the 0-based\n     position; for `frames` the step is `[:task :frame i]`.\n   - *Unordered element* (`:set`): `[kind :elem CE(e)]`; never an index.\n   - *Keyed pair* (`:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, `:parked-table`, and\n     the root's installs and module-stores slots, keyed by module name\n     and `M`): `[kind :key CE(k)]` when descending into the key,\n     `[kind :val CE(k)]` when descending into the value; never an\n     index. For `:bindings`, `:parked-table`, installs and module\n     stores the key is a structural datum and `CE(k)` is the datum.\n   - *Task boundary*: a path never crosses into an install child; a\n     child's cursor cells have paths rooted at the child's own root.\n   Equal occurrences yield equal positions, one per occurrence, and\n   positions never contain a source enumeration index of an unordered\n   container.\n4. **Signature and refinement.** A cursor cell's signature is the CBOR of\n   `[:sig <old label> <sorted vector of its positions, duplicates\n   retained>]`; its new label is that byte string. Repeat until no\n   class splits. Keeping the old label makes refinement monotone, so\n   the partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from\n   `k` initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n5. **Search.** Order classes bytewise by label. If every class is a\n   singleton, assign `1 … n` in that order and stop. Otherwise take the\n   first non-singleton class and, **for each of its members in turn**,\n   give that member the label that is the CBOR byte string of\n   `[:ind <depth> <old label>]`, where `depth` is 0 at the top-level\n   search and increases by one per level of recursion (distinct from\n   every refinement label by its tag; no ordering relative to other\n   labels is needed), refine to stability, and\n   recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete leaf yields a\n   numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the **root body's\n   canonical CBOR bytes** (the digest preimage, not the digest) as the\n   leaf's key. The procedure's output is the row set of the leaf with\n   the bytewise least key. Two leaves with equal keys have emitted\n   identical rows: they differ only in which source cursor cell received\n   which number, which the transported representation cannot observe.\n\n**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\na source-cursor-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\nnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\nprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\ncarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\ncommitment is thereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever\ndecides validity. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with a structured resource refusal before\nany attachment and never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never\nfalls back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n\nUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\ndifferent states sharing a closure over the same cursor cell may number it\ndifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\ncross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\nnever affected rows below a cursor cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\nprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cursor-cell C]`\nmaps to that entry; two references to one cursor cell share it and one\nreference per cursor cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n\n## 7. Equality and the content address\n\n\n\n(def pending-table\n  {:next [:C]\n   :put [[:ref :stream] :V :D]\n   :ffi [:C :D :D]\n   :ffi-request [:D :V [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :D]\n   :link-request [:D :S [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :D :D]\n   :link-response [:D :S [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C]\n   :install [:S]})\n\n\n(def row-table\n  \"Slot descriptors, excluding id and kind. :repeat/:pairs are variadic;\n   :refs is an ordered vector of structural references. :pending is\n   discriminated by pending-table. Nothing in this table is executable.\"\n  {:vec [:repeat :V] :list [:repeat :V] :set [:repeat :V]\n   :map [:pairs :V :V] :bindings [:pairs :S :V] :store [:pairs :D :V]\n   :env [[:ref :bindings] :optional-D]\n   :closure [:A :N :syms [:ref :bindings] :optional-D]\n   :prim [:S] :stream [:D :D] :reified [[:ref :act]] :parked-ref [:D]\n   :act [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k] :N]\n   :tail [:A :N [:ref :k]]\n   :window [:pairs :N :V] :k [:repeat [:ref :kframe]]\n   :kframe [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] :N]\n   :wait [[:ref :act :tail] [:ref :pending]] :pending :pending\n   :module-store [:D [:ref :store]]\n   :parked-table [:pairs :D [:ref :act :tail]]\n   :install [:S :D :D :D [:ref :task]]\n   :cursor-cells [:pairs :C [:ref :cursor-cell-body]] :cursor-cell-body [[:ref :stream] :D]\n   :task [:N :D :D :D :N [:ref :bindings] [:ref :store]\n          [:refs :module-store] [:ref :parked-table] [:ref :cursor-cells]\n          :D :D [:refs :install] [:refs :wait] :optional-D :optional-V :optional-D]})\n\n\n(defn node\n  [kind slots]\n  {:kind kind :slots (if (= :window kind)\n                       (vec (mapcat identity (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n                       (vec slots))})\n\n\n(defn- refuse!\n  [status kind path]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Row codec refused data\"\n                  {:yin.k/status status :yin.k/kind kind :yin.k/path path})))\n\n\n(defn- bad!\n  [kind path]\n  (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable kind path))\n\n\n(defn- marker-shaped?\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x) (= 1 (count x)) (or (contains? x :yin.k/row) (contains? x :yin.k/cursor-cell))))\n\n\n(defn- check-literal-markers!\n  [x]\n  (when (marker-shaped? x) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :marker []))\n  (when (coll? x)\n    (doseq [v (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) x)] (check-literal-markers! v)))\n  (when (meta x) (check-literal-markers! (meta x))))\n\n\n(defn- bytes-key\n  [bs]\n  ;; Unsigned bytewise order, without a length-first comparison.\n  (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq bs)))\n\n\n(defn- compare-bytes\n  [a b]\n  (loop [i 0]\n    (cond\n      (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n      (= i (count b)) 1\n      (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n      :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n\n\n(defn- byte-compare\n  [a b]\n  (compare-bytes (bytes-key (cbor/encode a)) (bytes-key (cbor/encode b))))\n\n\n(defn- sorted-bytes\n  [xs]\n  (vec (sort byte-compare xs)))\n\n\n(defn- descriptors\n  [kind slots]\n  (let [schema (get row-table kind)]\n    (cond\n      (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get pending-table (first slots)))\n      (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n      (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n      :else schema)))\n\n\n(defn- ref?\n  [d]\n  (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n\n\n(defn- refs?\n  [d]\n  (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n\n\n(def ^:private census-key #\"cc-([1-9][0-9]*)\")\n\n\n(defn- census-order\n  ;; Numeric order of cc-N without reading wire data: fewer digits first,\n  ;; then the digits. Other keys (a partial emit-numbered) sort last.\n  [k]\n  (let [digits (when (keyword? k) (second (re-matches census-key (name k))))]\n    (if digits [0 (count digits) digits] [1 0 (str k)])))\n\n\n(defn- ordered-slots\n  [kind slots]\n  (cond\n    (= kind :window) (vec (mapcat identity (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n    (= kind :cursor-cells) (vec (mapcat identity (sort-by #(census-order (first %)) (partition 2 slots))))\n    (contains? #{:map :bindings :store :parked-table} kind)\n    (vec (mapcat identity (sort #(byte-compare (first %1) (first %2)) (partition 2 slots))))\n    (= kind :set) (sorted-bytes slots)\n    :else slots))\n\n\n(declare emit-node canonical-numbering validate-frames)\n\n\n(defn- emit-slot\n  [d x numbering rows]\n  (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\n    (check-literal-markers! (if (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d)\n                              (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x)))\n  (cond\n    (ref? d) (emit-node x numbering rows)\n    (refs? d) (reduce (fn [[out rs] n]\n                        (let [[id rs'] (emit-node n numbering rs)] [(conj out id) rs']))\n                      [[] rows] x)\n    (= d :C) [(get numbering x x) rows]\n    (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d)\n    (case (first x)\n      :row (let [[id rs] (emit-node (second x) numbering rows)] [[:row id] rs])\n      :cursor-cell [[:cursor-cell (get numbering (second x) (second x))] rows]\n      [x rows])\n    :else [x rows]))\n\n\n(defn- emit-node\n  [n numbering rows]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n\n        ;; A child is canonicalized in its own identity scope.\n        [slots rows] (reduce (fn [[out rs] [d x]]\n                               (if (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x)))\n                                 (let [[id child-rows] (emit-node x (canonical-numbering x) {})]\n                                   [(conj out id) (merge rs child-rows)])\n                                 (let [[x' rs'] (emit-slot d x numbering rs)] [(conj out x') rs'])))\n                             [[] rows] (map vector (descriptors kind slots) slots))\n        slots (if (= kind :task)\n                (-> slots\n                    (update 7 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %)))\n                    (update 12 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %))))\n                (ordered-slots kind slots))\nharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\ntheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\nlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\nlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\npair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\ntransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\ntraversal order** (the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\ntakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\nthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\nabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\nand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\ndoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\ncursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\nand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial\nlabel (`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step\n1) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\nlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\nidentities. Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`,\n`:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by sorting their elements'\nencodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order, so\na permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\nthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n\n### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n\n**None.** The reviewer accepted the engine-less profile and rejected the\nmerged-`E` free environment; both are now settled in the text. Nothing\nremaining is owner-level.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n\n1. **Nil-valued window keys (Dart, Node).** The window is built with\n   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\n   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\n   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\n   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\n   `(get W r)`.\n2. **`for` over large seqs (Dart).** Row sets of the C4 modules run to\n   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\n3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\n   Clojure data in `.cljc`, never EDN strings.\n4. **Duplicate protocol params (Dart).** `gc-children [_ _x]`\n   (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:386`); any new protocol method\n   in `layout` likewise.\n5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\n   `{}` (`handoff.cljc:1455-1457` records the trap).\n6. **Reader conditionals.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`\n   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\n7. **Private var access (Dart).** Every function named in §8 is public;\n   no `#'`.\n8. **Sorted keys.** Window and bindings rows are sorted by Jing\n   canonical bytes of the key, never by host `sort` on mixed types.\n   560\t               (bad! :duplicate-key [id])))))\n   561\t       (letfn [(expand-slot\n   562\t                 [d x]\n   563\t                 (cond (ref? d) (expand x) (refs? d) (mapv expand x)\n   564\t                       (and (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d) (= :row (first x))) [:row (expand (second x))]\n   565\t                       :else x))\n   566\t               (expand\n   567\t                 [id]\n   568\t                 (let [[_ kind & slots] (get index id)]\n   569\t                   (node kind (mapv expand-slot (descriptors kind slots) slots))))]\n   570\t         (let [task (expand root)\n   571\t               _ (check-abstract! task)\n   572\t               canonical (if (= :task (:kind task)) (emit task budget)\n   573\t                             (let [[id _] (emit-node task {} {})] {:yin.k/root id}))]\n   574\t           (when (and (not= false (:check-numbering budget)) (not= root (:yin.k/root canonical)))\n   575\t             (bad! :noncanonical-cursor-cells [root]))\n   576\t           (when (= :task (:kind task))\n   577\t             (when-let [d (validate-frames task)]\n   578\t               (throw (ex-info \"Frame validation failed\" (update d :yin.k/path #(into [root] %))))))\n   579\t           task))))))\n   580\t\n   581\t\n   582\t(defn decode\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Lemma|lemma|Proof|proof' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md collab/*numbering*; nl -ba collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc | sed -n '20,50p;155,180p;200,215p;307,343p;390,425p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '225,231p;311,324p;557,568p;2312,2318p;2595,2602p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Lemma|lemma|Proof|proof' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md collab/*numbering*; nl -ba collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc | sed -n '20,50p;155,180p;200,215p;307,343p;390,425p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '225,231p;311,324p;557,568p;2312,2318p;2595,2602p'\"","aggregated_output":"collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:2:I found a gap in the orbit lemma: equal content encodings erase aliasing within an element, so `[X X]` can tie with `[Y Z]` even though they aren’t interchangeable. I’m testing that case and nested ties. The Clojure launcher tried to write its classpath cache; I’ll use an existing classpath to keep the review read-only.\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:63:**Counterexample A — D4’s orbit lemma**\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:122:**3. Proof**\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:124:The proof fails at the orbit lemma’s claim that equal `CE` yields “the same internal pattern.” Body erasure removes exactly the alias information needed to establish that claim.\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:208:The direction is compatible with phase 5’s pure image derivations and scanners. Phase 6’s engine-independent frames and sharing requirements remain unchanged, but its equality laws require the repaired canonicality proof.\ncollab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log:5:- **Both blocking findings reproduced and are fixed.** The content encoding now numbers cursor cells inside an element provisionally in the element's own discovery order (de Bruijn style), and an element's sort key is the output of a full local run of the same procedure, which is what makes the key carry alias topology even through nested containers. The orbit rule became a component rule: tied elements sharing an unnumbered cursor cell form a component, a component holding two elements of one group is the D5 refusal, and otherwise components are encoded, sorted, and the least run entered. One worklist per run resolves nested ties. The proof is rewritten as an isomorphism-invariance theorem with four lemmas.\ncollab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.report.md:10:(about 420 lines: findings F1-F7, the rule D1-D5, a proof, amendments\ncollab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.report.md:40:   one element, enter in any order (lemma: rows are order-independent).\ncollab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.report.md:51:   order, plus the orbit lemma; terminating in ≤ n fixpoint rounds of\ncollab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.prompt.md:10:1. BLOCKING, D4's orbit lemma is unsound: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` (X, Y, Z identical bodies, no other occurrences) passes D4 but different set enumerations give different roots. Equal `CE` of two elements does not imply interchangeable, because unnumbered cursor cells are spelled by body only, which cannot tell the alias pattern `(X X)` from `(Y Z)`. Orchestrator's suggested direction (evaluate, do not assume): make the content encoding of an element spell each UNNUMBERED cursor cell by its body PLUS a local first-occurrence index within that element (de Bruijn style inside the element: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`), so alias topology inside an element is part of the sort key, and only elements with equal such encodings are candidates for the orbit rule; then state exactly when the orbit rule is sound (prove it) and refuse otherwise. If you find a better repair, give it with proof.\ncollab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log:9:- The residue is a tie group whose cells occur only in tie groups. If each cell's occurrences lie inside one element, any entry order gives identical rows (proved as a lemma). Otherwise the lift refuses with a dedicated kind, `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, not `:resource-limit`. The loop plus 2-cycle fixture and the seven-cell fixture fall in that class. The 6a procedure already refuses the seven-cell state by its label-byte budget.\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.prompt.md:12:2. The rule D1-D5: traversal order, tie groups, fixpoint, the orbit lemma (D4), the refusal (D5). Try to BREAK it: construct states where discovery order plus content-first sorting gives two different numberings for the same structure (renaming or permuting an unordered container), or where the orbit lemma admits a non-interchangeable choice. Try nested unordered containers, a cell appearing in two tie groups, a cell that appears both in an ordered slot and in a set, install children, cells referenced from a guest map's keys and values. Run or extend the prototype to test them; report counterexamples with exact inputs.\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.prompt.md:13:3. The proof (section 3): induction on renaming invariance, permutation invariance, termination (at most n fixpoint rounds): is each step actually justified?\ncollab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.prompt.md:9:2. TRY TO BREAK THE NEW RULE: the component rule, local runs, memoization and totality are new and unproved by you. Build adversarial states: elements sharing cells across several groups; a cell in an ordered slot and in tied elements; nested components; map keys holding aliased cells; tied elements whose local-run encodings are equal but which are NOT isomorphic given their sharing with other elements; memoization poisoning (the author found one such bug; are there more?); install children inside tied elements; two renamings of the same state (rename every cell, permute every unordered container, permute census order) giving two roots. Run at least 5000 random states of your own design (different generator from the author's) and report failures with exact inputs. Check lemmas A-D in section 3 line by line.\ncollab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.prompt.md:10:3. THE SIMPLICITY AUDIT (new question): the first draft promised 60 to 80 added lines and deleting about 150 lines of refinement/search code. The repaired scheme now has components, local runs, memoization, a total-numbering assertion and a work bound of O(P (d+1)(n+1) log m). Is it still materially simpler and cheaper to review than the 6a refinement-and-search design it replaces? Estimate the implementation size in `rows.cljc` terms (lines added/deleted), compare the proof obligations (three lemmas of 6a's own vs four here), the refusal class (what each design refuses), and the failure modes. Then give a plain RECOMMENDATION: adopt the revised discovery-order design, or keep 6a's refinement design with its documented limitation, or a third option. If you recommend keeping 6a, say what in the revised note is still worth keeping (for example the vocabulary-free parts, the decode translation, the position inventory).\ncollab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md:32:as lemma C). The proof is an isomorphism-invariance theorem with four\ncollab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md:33:lemmas (§3 of the note).\ncollab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md:37:**1. BLOCKING, orbit lemma unsound.** Changed: the spelling (note §2\ncollab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md:39:(theorem, lemmas A–D). I evaluated the orchestrator's direction and\ncollab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short; git rev-parse HEAD; cat collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; cat collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-Z3PZFHZs' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 03:35:37.869 xcodebuild[89062:57440368]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 03:35:38.102 xcodebuild[89062:57440367] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-PrUzw0LT' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 03:35:38.651 xcodebuild[89066:57440386]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 03:35:38.823 xcodebuild[89066:57440385] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.report.md\\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md\\n?? collab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.prompt.md\\n?? collab/example-suspended-state.clj\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/deps.edn\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/run.log\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame_test.cljc\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-hJAcdm9p' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.044 xcodebuild[89073:57440446]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.218 xcodebuild[89073:57440445] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-qIuPIPR1' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.733 xcodebuild[89077:57440462]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 03:35:40.917 xcodebuild[89077:57440461] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697\\nI’ll read the architecture rules, note, and carrier draft, then challenge the numbering rule with the prototype and check its claims against the phase 6a code. I’ll keep the worktree read-only.\\nI found a gap in the orbit lemma: equal content encodings erase aliasing within an element, so `[X X]` can tie with `[Y Z]` even though they aren’t interchangeable. I’m testing that case and nested ties. The Clojure launcher tried to write its classpath cache; I’ll use an existing classpath to keep the review read-only.\\nThe prototype produces different BLAKE3 roots for the same set when I reverse `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`. The same failure occurs for map entries `{X X, Y Z}`. Nested sets also return an empty numbering, leaving source ids in the carrier. The reported fixtures and all 21 phase 6a row tests reproduce, but those tests miss these cases.\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 19:42:34 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 02:42:34 Asia/Bangkok\\n\\nDiscovery-order numbering remains a viable direction, but **this note is not sound as written**. D4 admits choices that change the root id, and nested ties can leave cursor cells unnumbered.\\n\\nReview was read-only at HEAD `7a6d7cc9`; phase 6a was inspected through Git at `d77da9f0`. No files were edited, staged, or committed. Completion timestamps use the available clock, which precedes the brief’s Created-GMT.\\n\\nFindings below use `note` for `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`, `probe` for `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, and `rows` for phase 6a’s `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`.\\n\\n| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **Blocking** | note:153, 183 | D4’s occurrence-locality condition does not preserve alias topology **within** an element. Equal `CE` does not imply interchangeability. `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` passes D4 and produces different roots after element reversal. | Require a proven alias-preserving correspondence between tied elements, including their internal traversal. Conservatively refuse uncertified cases. Equal bodies and disjoint identities alone are insufficient. |\\n| **Blocking** | note:153; probe:146–162 | Entering D4 elements can discover nested tie groups. The prototype processes only the old residue, then returns success. `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` returns an empty numbering and emits source ids. | Specify and implement processing of newly discovered deferred groups, including further fixpoint/orbit checks. Require total numbering before emission. |\\n| **High** | note:70–88, 219 | The stated refusal class is too narrow. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` refuses even though X and Y are interchangeable and there are no guest pairs or map keys. | Describe D5 by its actual predicate. Separate “not certified by this procedure” from “non-interchangeable,” and remove the unsupported rarity claim. |\\n| **High** | note:352–354; rows:570–575 | Decode invokes `emit`. A newly introduced lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` exception will propagate unless explicitly translated. Recompute-and-compare alone does not implement the proposed decode taxonomy. | Translate only `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to `:yin.k/undecodable` at recomputation, preserving resource refusals and validator precedence. |\\n| **Medium** | probe:48, 111–113, 131–139; note:299 | The prototype differs from D1–D5: numbered `CE` uses keyword `:yin.k/cc-n`, not integer `n`; parked-table values are incorrectly visited as `V`, although they are structural refs; the orbit walker omits `:refs`. Install children still use 6a numbering. | Make the prototype schema-driven and match the specified encoding. Test parked states, K frames, and children under discovery numbering in every scope. |\\n| **Medium** | note:18, 124, 192–196 | “Linear work” is not established. `CE` sorts unordered containers; repeated encoding and nested expansion add work. Acyclic DAGs can have large expanded traversals. | State a bound in terms of expanded positions, sorting, rounds, and encoded bytes. Account for that work before allocation under the retained budgets. |\\n| **Medium** | note:223–262 | Amendments leave stale wording: A2 leaves “no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity”; A5 leaves §10.10’s “never falls back to encounter order”; A8 leaves the old byte-string body-label explanation and step-1 reference. | Update those passages explicitly. Distinguish canonical traversal from arbitrary host enumeration. |\\n| **Low** | note:274–289 | The portable-id example changes the carried `id-counter`, so the two complete tasks are already structurally different. Some engine line references are inaccurate. | Hold every carried field, including the counter, equal in the example; correct the citations. |\\n\\n**1. Position inventory**\\n\\nF1 is substantially correct: I found **no omitted identity-bearing position** in the carrier §5 tables or phase 6a’s row schema.\\n\\nThe ordered positions cover:\\n\\n- Root free environment, store, module stores, parked table, waits, and result.\\n- Act environments, windows, K frames; tail K frames; captured acts.\\n- Closure bindings and module-store contents.\\n- Pending bare cursor-cell slots and guest value/envelope slots.\\n- Install children, each with an independent task-local numbering scope.\\n\\nOnly guest set elements and guest map entries lack a structural order. Census declaration edges are correctly excluded.\\n\\nOne wording correction: D1 should explicitly retain **numeric ascending register order** for windows, as the carrier specifies, rather than grouping windows indiscriminately under byte ordering. The prototype’s parked-table omission is an implementation defect, not an inventory omission.\\n\\n**2. Attempts to break D1–D5**\\n\\nI ran the prototype using `java -cp` with an existing classpath because the requested Clojure launcher attempted a prohibited cache write. All custom inputs were evaluated in memory.\\n\\nThe following constructor fixes the surrounding task exactly:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(require '[probe.discovery :as p]\\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows :as r]\\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as t])\\n\\n(def b (t/cursor-cell-body :s 0))\\n(def c #(vector :cursor-cell %))\\n(def row #(vector :row %))\\n(def v #(row (r/node :vec (mapv c %))))\\n(def s #(row (r/node :set %)))\\n(def task\\n  (fn [ids result]\\n    (t/task-node (mapv #(vector % b) ids) result)))\\n```\\n\\nAll named cursor cells below have the same body.\\n\\n**Counterexample A — D4’s orbit lemma**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])]))\\n(task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:y :z]) (v [:x :x])]))\\n```\\n\\nThese differ only in set enumeration. Both pass D4.\\n\\n| Enumeration | Numbering | BLAKE3 root digest |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `[X X]`, `[Y Z]` | X=1, Y=2, Z=3 | `65ba4530bd8908e734a8be4fcc594d2989c5a8a7d1017779ef8f62991df38dab` |\\n| `[Y Z]`, `[X X]` | Y=1, Z=2, X=3 | `387f294a2f1963f2f2241e8527d8698dedff5d50de4f46bb762b456a01375090` |\\n\\nThe emitted multisets contain respectively `[1 1], [2 3]` and `[1 2], [3 3]`. There is no alias-preserving automorphism exchanging the two elements. Both abstract inputs successfully emit and decode under existing 6a.\\n\\n**Counterexample B — guest map keys and values**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(task [:x :y :z]\\n      (row (r/node :map [(c :x) (c :x) (c :y) (c :z)])))\\n\\n(task [:x :y :z]\\n      (row (r/node :map [(c :y) (c :z) (c :x) (c :x)])))\\n```\\n\\nThe roots differ:\\n\\n```text\\n409548a71f080cae6f3f66911409370d83a5df9e928135d95c497e9b770d9328\\nbe62ba43d9d07dc7b5e29375e195ab839ab37b721074af816e540615f6167ee9\\n```\\n\\nThis is the same missing alias distinction across an entry’s key and value.\\n\\n**Counterexample C — nested unordered containers**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(task [:x :y :z :w]\\n      (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)])\\n          (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]))\\n```\\n\\nThe prototype returns `{:numbering {}}`. Renaming X/Y/Z/W to Q/R/S/T changes the emitted root:\\n\\n```text\\nfddde4522dbebc4ee6fa2d78a1bd1d2691faccc26c4ca14ee17c620ecdcddf0b\\nabb32996b0ac30b720f17c6cfacf0323dcb16a47d58cc8e54c33571ce5d58080\\n```\\n\\nThe outer orbit step discovers inner ties but never processes them.\\n\\nOther requested challenges:\\n\\n- `[#{X Y} X]` correctly resolves to X=1, Y=2 through D3.\\n- `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` refuses because identities cross tie groups.\\n- A parked act containing an ordered X reference is ignored by the prototype, causing an unnecessary refusal.\\n- An install child using the review fixture gets its old 6a numbering. Switching all scopes to discovery changes the parent root, confirming that current child results do not validate the proposed rule.\\n\\n**3. Proof**\\n\\nThe proof fails at the orbit lemma’s claim that equal `CE` yields “the same internal pattern.” Body erasure removes exactly the alias information needed to establish that claim.\\n\\nThe renaming induction is reasonable for D1–D3 with a complete schema walk and fixed encoding. “No source id is read” is insufficient for permutation invariance: listed order remains an input to D4.\\n\\nD3’s monotone numbering supports a finite bound on productive rounds. It does not prove that D4 finishes nested discovery or that every reached cursor cell receives a number. Consequently, the asserted bijection, determinism, and cross-engine conclusion are not established.\\n\\nSorting and repeated expansion also invalidate the unqualified linear-work claim.\\n\\n**4. Refusal taxonomy and practical class**\\n\\nThe proposed families are appropriate:\\n\\n- Lift: `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\\n- Decode recomputation: `:yin.k/undecodable`, same kind.\\n- Actual budget exhaustion: retain qualified unsupported/resource refusal.\\n\\nA deliberate representational restriction is not a resource exhaustion. Run it at carrier validation step 6, after structural/canonical-form checks and before frame validation. Implement the decode translation explicitly.\\n\\nThe remaining class is **not demonstrated rare**. The two-group example above is a simple reusable collection, not an exotic graph. Moreover, repairing D4 conservatively will enlarge the refused class beyond what F4 currently describes. That limitation needs an accurate owner-facing description before adoption.\\n\\n**5. Impact on 6a and pins**\\n\\nThe proposed deletions are broadly correct: refinement, positions, class construction, exhaustive search, growing-label checks, and search/label-byte budget keys.\\n\\nKeep the carrier schema, BLAKE3 addressing, census numeric order, final row ordering, recompute-and-compare, and numbering-before-frame-validation order. Add a complete traversal, deferred processing, certified orbit rule, total-numbering assertion, refusal translation, and meaningful work accounting.\\n\\nI recomputed these pins:\\n\\n| Fixture | BLAKE3 digest | Result |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Two-cell fixture | `fab223d9b3540fdce0ccf2132f8a4041917a158a32295470a4936754742d3ac4` | Unchanged |\\n| Worked frame | `c4a7c7e6de3b44400efec5930bc3d85d86b29153b4b2d548acba630bfbc3da7d` | Unchanged |\\n| Review counterexample | `a3713a5e1d1a051d1c0aab255bdc48349993fbf21d68ba947e74fc4408c68c28` | Matches proposed pin |\\n| `[X X Y]` | `4993b5b192de7d903a90f7bc87dde8c7d5ac007c2b013ef62b998dc988282576` | Matches proposed pin |\\n| `[X Y Y]` | `536c810b07c7cab1dd812fcb1bbf0c7433dc41af4e39037c5f278e82abf1e032` | Matches; unchanged from 6a |\\n\\nThe symmetric-pair, repeated-pair, map, and ten-cursor-cell pins also reproduced. The prototype reproduced the reported 400-state statistics. Existing 6a row tests passed: **21 tests, 324 assertions**.\\n\\nConsumers include `emit`, child emission, `decode*`, and `canonical-under`. Continuation §5.4’s round-trip and cross-engine laws depend on canonical re-emission. `canonical-under` still recomputes through `emit`; its mathematical guarantee therefore inherits this defect.\\n\\nAcceptance items 12–20 need the three counterexamples above, multi-group cases, parked/K coverage, discovery-numbered children, total-numbering checks, and decode round trips. Random testing alone missed both blocking defects. Keep a large-input regression even when deleting its obsolete label-budget expectation.\\n\\n**6. Portable ids**\\n\\nThe rejection is sound **for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged** under the owner’s structure-only root law. Local counters, execution sequence, occurrence/counter hashes, and random names cannot replace structural canonicalization.\\n\\nCorrect the example by making both tasks’ carried counters equal—for example, consume an unrelated fresh name in the second history. Do not claim every imaginable portable naming scheme is impossible: ids recomputed from snapshot structure are effectively the canonicalization problem again.\\n\\nThe note calls this F7; F6 is prototype evidence.\\n\\n**7. Grounding, amendments, and portability**\\n\\nI spot-checked more than twelve cited locations against branch code:\\n\\n| Reference | Check |\\n|---|---|\\n| rows:37–57 | Row schema and identity-bearing positions |\\n| rows:140–145 | Numeric census order |\\n| rows:148–156 | Emitted collection ordering |\\n| rows:185–189 | Independent child numbering |\\n| rows:200–202 | Budget keys |\\n| rows:212–230 | Label-byte checking |\\n| rows:252–255 | Initial labels |\\n| rows:257–284 | Content encoding |\\n| rows:285–325 | Positions |\\n| rows:331–354 | Refinement and search |\\n| rows:570–578 | Recompute, compare, then frames |\\n| rows_test:28 | Two-cell fixture |\\n| rows_test:159 | Alias fixtures |\\n| rows_test:171 | Symmetric pair |\\n| rows_test:206 | Numbering precedes frame defect |\\n| rows_test:307–338 | Pins and numeric census |\\n| rows_test:392–421 | Seven-cursor-cell and large-input tests |\\n\\nThe scratch rows implementation and tests matched their branch versions by checksum. Engine citations need correction: `issue-ref` starts at 311, `gensym` at 2312, and unminted cursor creation is principally 557–568.\\n\\nAmendments should also:\\n\\n- State that `[:child]` deliberately replaces the existing `[:child <child root id>]`; “one change; nothing else changes” is inaccurate.\\n- Define `[:n k]` unambiguously as an integer and align the prototype.\\n- Remove the stale body-label explanation in continuation §5.5.\\n- Update §10.10’s encounter-order wording.\\n- Change the diagnostics’ “two kinds” count when adding a third lift refusal.\\n\\nThe direction is compatible with phase 5’s pure image derivations and scanners. Phase 6’s engine-independent frames and sharing requirements remain unchanged, but its equality laws require the repaired canonicality proof.\\n\\nRetain continuation §12’s portability rules: nil-valued keys remain present; membership uses `contains?`; large sequences use `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`; pinned fixtures are `.cljc` data rather than whitespace-sensitive EDN strings on Dart. These were inspected, not validated by new Node or Dart runs.\\n\\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 20:34:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 03:34:00 Asia/Bangkok\\n\\n(Host clock as read by `date -u` at completion; as in the previous\\nreport, this host's clock precedes the brief's Created-GMT.)\\n\\n# Architect report: revision of the discovery-order numbering note after the codex review\\n\\nEdited: `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\\n(rewritten, 724 lines) and the prototype\\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (rewritten, 751 lines;\\nrun log at `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`). Nothing staged or\\ncommitted. The 6a code was read from branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`)\\nthrough the probe project's copies, as before.\\n\\n## The repair in one paragraph\\n\\nThe content encoding now numbers the cursor cells *inside* an element\\nprovisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to the\\nenclosing numbering (de Bruijn style: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`,\\n`[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`), and the encoding of an element is the\\nspelling produced by a *local run* of the same procedure over it (nested\\ncontainers sorted, nested ties deferred and re-examined, what it cannot\\nresolve spelled `[:tie …]`). There is one worklist per run, so a tie\\ndiscovered inside an entered element joins the root worklist and is\\nre-examined. The orbit rule is replaced by a **component rule**: at a\\nfixpoint, tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell form a\\ncomponent; a component holding two elements of one group is the D5\\nrefusal; otherwise components are encoded by a local run over their\\nelements in group order, sorted, and the least run is entered (equal\\nencodings are isomorphic, so host order among them is harmless, proved\\nas lemma C). The proof is an isomorphism-invariance theorem with four\\nlemmas (§3 of the note).\\n\\n## Per item\\n\\n**1. BLOCKING, orbit lemma unsound.** Changed: the spelling (note §2\\n\\\"Spelling\\\"), D4 (components, component encodings, least-run entry), §3\\n(theorem, lemmas A–D). I evaluated the orchestrator's direction and\\nadopted it, with one addition it needs to be sound: local first-occurrence\\nindices inside an element are not enough when the element itself holds\\nnested unordered containers or when tied elements share cells across\\ngroups, so the element's key is the output of a full local run (which\\nresolves nested ties by the same rules and spells unresolved ones as\\n`[:tie]`), and the orbit candidates are components, not elements. The\\nnote shows why the weaker version fails: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and\\n`[#{P Q} #{R S}]` would key equal under single-pass local indices with\\n`[:tie]` parts, yet they are not isomorphic, and entering them in either\\norder gives different rows; the local run with the component rule keys\\nthem differently (prototype fixture `b35b8e12…`). Verified: review A\\n`#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and B `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations\\nand after renaming plus shuffling; the roots `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`\\nequal the digests codex computed for the `X=1, Y=2, Z=3` numbering, an\\nindependent cross-check of the emission. Hand derivations in note §6.\\n\\n**2. BLOCKING, nested tie groups.** Changed: D3 states that entering an\\nelement inside a group runs D1 on it, so newly discovered groups join\\nthe one worklist of the run and are re-examined next round; D4 and D5\\nrecurse through component local runs; a \\\"Totality\\\" paragraph requires a\\nbijection onto `1…n` before emission, asserted by the implementation as\\na defect (never a refusal, never a partial carrier). Prototype:\\n`discovery-numbering` throws \\\"numbering is not total\\\" with the missing\\nand extra cells; it fired once during revision on a malformed fixture of\\nmine (a census missing the worked frame's pending cursor cell), which is\\nthe behaviour wanted. Verified: review C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` numbers all\\nfour cells, root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming (and 6a\\nreaches the same root); `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` numbers `X cc-1` first and\\nresolves the inner sets by the fixpoint and the component rule, root\\n`6ea03f9b…`.\\n\\n**3. HIGH, the refusal class.** Changed: F4 states the predicate in one\\nsentence (two elements of one guest set or map with equal keys, linked by\\na chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells at a fixpoint), separates \\\"not\\ncertified by this procedure\\\" from \\\"non-interchangeable\\\" with examples\\non each side, withdraws the rarity claim, and says the real-state rate\\nis unmeasured because the repository has no version-3 task corpus (Stage\\nE scenarios and the v2 handoff census tests hold cursor cells but no v3\\nlift exists before 6b). `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` now numbers (root `92747c0f…`,\\nequal to 6a's). Verified by a permissive diagnostic mode in the\\nprototype (D5 replaced by host-order entry, 24 shuffles): the loop plus\\n2-cycle, the 2-cycle and the 3-cycle give one root (interchangeable,\\nrefused anyway); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` gives 2 roots and the\\nseven-cell state 4 roots (genuinely need a tie-break). Fixture census:\\nof the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\\nD5. Random census under a tie-biased generator: 25 of 2000 value states\\n(15 witnessed non-interchangeable, 10 not distinguished by 12 shuffles),\\n8 of 200 frame-bearing, 2 of 100 with children; the note reports these\\nas rates under that distribution, not as a claim about real states.\\n\\n**4. HIGH, decode.** Changed: §2 \\\"Validation and decode\\\" specifies that\\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\\nraised by the recomputation is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable` of the\\nsame kind (path prefixed by the root id), resource refusals propagate as\\nthemselves, no other non-portable kind can arise there because step 5\\nran, and the validator order (after step 5, before `validate-frames`)\\nstays; A4 carries it into carrier §8 step 6 and the diagnostics\\nparagraph. Prototype `decode*` implements the translation and the\\ncomparison. Unverified: the prototype's recompute runs after 6a's\\n`decode*` returns (so after `validate-frames`), because 6a's `decode*` is\\nprivate and monolithic; the note states the required order and the\\nacceptance item 16 tests it.\\n\\n**5. MEDIUM, prototype follows the spec.** Changed: the prototype is\\nschema-driven (one `enter-node!`/`spell-node` pair over `row-table`\\ndescriptors plus two synthetic kinds, `:entry` and `:component`);\\nnumbered cells spell `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]` with `k` an integer and\\nlocal ones `[:cursor-cell [:u j body]]`; parked-table values are entered\\nthrough their `[:ref :act :tail]` descriptor (the old `:V` bug is gone);\\ninstall children are numbered by the same procedure in the prototype's\\nown emitter; `:window` is entered in numeric register order. New\\nfixtures: act window, K-frame window, environment matched to the free\\nenvironment, and a parked act at a park site (code from the frame tests'\\n`:park` segment), all decodable. Search: 2000 random value states with\\nnested sets, sets of pairs including `[X X]`, cursor-cell map keys and\\ncells aliased across containers, plus 200 frame-bearing and 100\\nwith-children states; renaming every cursor cell and shuffling every\\nset, map and census: **0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures**,\\n2033 decode round trips ok, 232 not decodable through 6a's own\\nrecompute under its default budget (the probe decodes through 6a's\\n`decode*`, which still runs 6a's numbering; a probe limitation, not a\\nnumbering outcome), 35 refusals. The decode round trip caught one real\\nprototype bug during revision: a memoized local run returned the whole\\nnumbering of another element's context, so an unrelated cursor cell was\\nadopted and shifted the provisional indices (seed 1016 gave two roots\\nover 40 shuffles). The memo now returns the numbering restricted to the\\nitem's own cells; the note's \\\"Memoization\\\" paragraph records the\\nrequirement. Also fixed in the generator during revision: reference-free\\nvectors, and set elements equal under decoded equality, both refused by\\ndecode and both generator artifacts.\\n\\n**6. MEDIUM, \\\"linear work\\\".** Changed: §2 \\\"Work\\\" states\\n`W = O(P · (d + 1) · (n + 1) · log m)` with memoized local runs (`P`\\nexpanded positions, counted per reference so DAG expansion is bounded by\\n`P` rather than the row count; `d` container depth; `n` cursor cells;\\n`m` largest container) and `O(P · (n + 1)^(d+1) · log m)` without, and\\nmakes `:work` charge one unit per position visited or spelled and one\\nper literal byte copied, so every spelling byte is paid for before\\nallocation; `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`, `:rows` and `:bytes` stay and\\ntogether bound what a spelling can copy; `:search` and `:label-bytes`\\ngo. The recommendation line now says \\\"work bounded and accounted\\\" instead\\nof \\\"linear work\\\". A5 updates carrier §10.10's wording accordingly.\\nUnverified: the bound is argued, not measured; the prototype does not\\ncharge budgets.\\n\\n**7. MEDIUM, stale wording.** Changed: §0 defines canonical traversal\\norder versus host enumeration order; A2 now replaces the whole tail of\\nthe Validation paragraph including \\\"no encounter-order tie-break ever\\ndecides validity\\\" and \\\"never falls back to encounter order\\\"; A5 replaces\\n§10.10's \\\"never falls back to encounter order\\\" and answer 9's\\n\\\"refinement-and-search\\\"; A8 gives three explicit replacements for\\ncontinuation §5.5 (the body-label sentence with its \\\"step 1\\\" reference,\\nthe \\\"sorting their elements' encodings (step 2)\\\" sentence, and a\\nqualification of \\\"never derived from traversal order\\\" to *host*\\ntraversal order); A4 corrects the \\\"two kinds\\\" counts in carrier §8 and\\n§11 and states the `[:child]` and integer `[:n k]` changes in A1.\\n\\n**8. LOW.** Changed: the portable-id example holds the id counter equal\\n(task B consumes one unrelated fresh name), and the paragraph concedes\\nthe review's point that an id recomputed from structure is the\\ncanonicalization problem again; engine citations are given against\\nbranch `srvm-phase6a` (`gen-id` 227–230, `issue-ref` 311–323,\\n`handle-cursor-unminted` 557–568, `gensym` 2312–2318, the `:stream/cursor`\\neffect site 2595–2602, read with `git show` on the branch); D1 and the\\nF1 table say `:window` is in numeric ascending register order as the\\ncarrier specifies.\\n\\n## Pinned ids, old → new\\n\\n- Unchanged from the first draft and from 6a: `fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\\n  `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `536c810b…`, ten-cell root\\n  `6e7c7a6b…`.\\n- Unchanged from the first draft (differ from 6a): `a3713a5e…`,\\n  `4993b5b1…`.\\n- First draft gave two roots depending on enumeration → one root:\\n  review A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` `65ba4530…`; review B `{X X, Y Z}` `409548a7…`.\\n- First draft refused → numbered: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` `92747c0f…`.\\n- First draft left unnumbered (emitted source ids) → numbered: review C\\n  `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` `18b70de6…`.\\n- New pins: `[#{X Y} X]` `7dc3d5c4…`; `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` `6ea03f9b…`;\\n  `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` `b35b8e12…`.\\n- Still refused: loop plus 2-cycle (6a's `55ff168e…` stays removed; `L`\\n  now gets `cc-1` before the refusal), seven-cell.\\n- Every numbering was derived by hand in note §6 and the digest taken\\n  from the codec under that numbering; A, B, C and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`\\n  agree with an independent computation (codex's, or 6a's).\\n\\n## Unverified or disputed\\n\\n- No codex counterexample is disputed; all three reproduced on the first\\n  prototype and are fixed.\\n- The prototype's decode check runs after 6a's frame validation\\n  (item 4); the real codec must place it at step 6.\\n- Three-host runs, kondo, budgets charging: not run or not implemented\\n  (documents and a JVM scratch prototype only).\\n- The real-state rate of D5 is unmeasured (no v3 task corpus; item 3).\\n- The work bound is argued, not measured (item 6).\\n- Uncommitted changes in the p6a worktree, if any, remain invisible.\\n\\n## Owner questions\\n\\nUnchanged from the first report, restated in note §9: accept the D5\\nclass as documented (recommended yes); refusal kind\\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` rather than `:resource-limit` (recommended).\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tStatus: **Proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-12 (Architect,\\n     4\\tclaude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \\\"the problem is not\\n     5\\tthat the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity is local\\\", and\\n     6\\tthe orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by **discovery order**\\n     7\\tin a canonical traversal. **Revised 2026-10-12** after the independent\\n     8\\treview (gpt-6.1-sol, `collab/1792140000000-…findings.md`), which broke\\n     9\\tthe first draft in two places: the orbit lemma was unsound (equal\\n    10\\tcontent encodings did not imply interchangeable elements: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`)\\n    11\\tand nested tie groups were never processed (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). The\\n    12\\trule, the proof, the refusal class, the decode path, the work bound, the\\n    13\\tamendments and the prototype are rewritten below; every pinned id that\\n    14\\tmoved is listed in §6. Subordinate to\\n    15\\t[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\\n    16\\t(the carrier, body version 3) and\\n    17\\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\\n    18\\tvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\\\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\\n    19\\tcell\\\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\\n    20\\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\\n    21\\t(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\\n    22\\tcode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\t**Recommendation in one line:** adopt discovery-order numbering now (§7,\\n    25\\toption a): a function of structure, renaming- and enumeration-invariant,\\n    26\\tprovable by hand (§3), with work bounded and accounted before allocation\\n    27\\t(§2 \\\"Work\\\"); it deletes carrier §6's refinement and search, keeps every\\n    28\\tpinned root id except the one state only the search could number, and\\n    29\\trefuses a precisely described class (D5) that it cannot certify.\\n    30\\t\\n    31\\t## 0. Vocabulary used below\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\t- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\\n    34\\t  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \\\"listed\\n    35\\t  order\\\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\\n    36\\t  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\\n    37\\t  accident of the host and the procedure must never let it decide a\\n    38\\t  number. The first draft's amendments blurred the two; §4 keeps them\\n    39\\t  apart.\\n    40\\t- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\\n    41\\t  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\\n    42\\t- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\\n    43\\t  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\\n    44\\t  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\\n    45\\t- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\\n    46\\t  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\\n    47\\t  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\\n    48\\t  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one element (or\\n    49\\t  one component, D4) from the enclosing run's current numbering; it\\n    50\\t  exists only to compute a sort key and is discarded.\\n    51\\t\\n    52\\t## 1. Findings\\n    53\\t\\n    54\\t**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\\n    55\\t(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\\n    56\\tcursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\\n    57\\t\\n    58\\t| Position | Order | Canonical index |\\n    59\\t|---|---|---|\\n    60\\t| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\\n    61\\t| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\\n    62\\t| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\\n    63\\t| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\\n    64\\t| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \\\"sorted\\\") |\\n    65\\t| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\\n    66\\t| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\\n    67\\t| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\\n    68\\t| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\\n    69\\t\\n    70\\tSo the environment's key order is canonical (bytes of the symbol as\\n    71\\tspelled, the order `:bindings` rows are emitted in), and the only\\n    72\\tunordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest `:map` entries.\\n    73\\tThe review confirmed the inventory complete. The example state in\\n    74\\t`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one cursor cell\\n    75\\t(`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's environment under\\n    76\\t`c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next` pending's\\n    77\\t`cursor-cell-C` slot.\\n    78\\t\\n    79\\t**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\\n    80\\tstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\\n    81\\tit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\\n    82\\tfixed order, so \\\"the n-th cursor cell met\\\" already encodes the first\\n    83\\tposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\\n    84\\tnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\\n    85\\texisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\\n    86\\torder is a total order by construction.\\n    87\\t\\n    88\\t**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\\n    89\\tcarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\\n    90\\tcell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike and the\\n    91\\treview's counterexample followed. The repaired encoding (§2 \\\"Spelling\\\")\\n    92\\tnumbers the cursor cells *inside* an element provisionally, in the\\n    93\\telement's own discovery order, relative to the enclosing numbering: `[X X]`\\n    94\\tspells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology\\n    95\\tinside an element is part of its sort key, as a de Bruijn index is part\\n    96\\tof a term. The key of an element is the spelling produced by a *local\\n    97\\trun* over it, which applies the same rules recursively (nested\\n    98\\tcontainers sorted, nested ties deferred and re-examined, D4 inside) and\\n    99\\tspells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`. Equal keys form a **tie\\n   100\\tgroup**, deferred to the run's worklist and re-examined to a fixpoint\\n   101\\t(D3) as other positions assign numbers. No key ever grows beyond the\\n   102\\tsize of its element's expansion.\\n   103\\t\\n   104\\t**F4. The residue, the component rule, and the refusal predicate.** What\\n   105\\tsurvives the fixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor\\n   106\\tcells occur only inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they\\n   107\\tshare an unnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\\n   108\\t**component**. Two cases:\\n   109\\t- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\\n   110\\t  component is then encoded by a local run over its elements in group\\n   111\\t  order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, spells its\\n   112\\t  shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by that\\n   113\\t  encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\\n   114\\t  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components, and the\\n   115\\t  entered state is the same up to isomorphism whichever order the host\\n   116\\t  lists them in (§3, lemma C). `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\\n   117\\t  `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\\n   118\\t- *Not certified* (D5): some component holds two elements of one tie\\n   119\\t  group, at some fixpoint of some run. Then entering either element first\\n   120\\t  is a choice this procedure cannot show to be harmless, and it refuses.\\n   121\\t  This is **\\\"not certified by this procedure\\\"**, not \\\"non-interchangeable\\\":\\n   122\\t  `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` are refused\\n   123\\t  although every entering order gives the same rows (the swap and the\\n   124\\t  rotation are automorphisms); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the\\n   125\\t  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused\\n   126\\t  and genuinely need a tie-break (§6 shows two orders with different\\n   127\\t  roots). The loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\\n   128\\t  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) now numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and\\n   129\\t  refuses on `{A, B}`.\\n   130\\t  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused iff, at a\\n   131\\t  fixpoint, two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are\\n   132\\t  connected by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied\\n   133\\t  elements.* Such a state needs two or more cursor cells with equal bodies\\n   134\\t  that are held *only* through a guest set or map whose elements mention\\n   135\\t  more than one of them each. How often real states do that is **not\\n   136\\t  measured**: the repository has no version-3 task corpus yet (the Stage E\\n   137\\t  scenarios and the v2 handoff census tests hold cursor cells but no v3\\n   138\\t  lift exists before 6b), so §6 reports only the fixture inventory (two\\n   139\\t  of the repository's fixtures hit D5, both built to) and the random\\n   140\\t  generator's rate under a tie-biased distribution. The first draft's\\n   141\\t  rarity claim is withdrawn. D5 can be narrowed later by a bounded search\\n   142\\t  over the residue without changing the numbering of any state D1–D4\\n   143\\t  number today, because D1–D4's decisions precede it.\\n   144\\t\\n   145\\t**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\\n   146\\trefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\\n   147\\tof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\\n   148\\tcensus), terminating, with work bounded by §2 \\\"Work\\\".\\n   149\\t\\n   150\\t**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\\n   151\\tcursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\\n   152\\tmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\\n   153\\tsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\\n   154\\tframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\\n   155\\tstate) and 100 states with install children: the prototype gave equal\\n   156\\troot ids after renaming every cursor cell and shuffling every set, map\\n   157\\tand census, with no failure; every numbered carrier decoded through 6a's\\n   158\\tdecoder and recomputed to its own root; it refused 35 of 2300. The\\n   159\\treview's three counterexamples number canonically; the pinned fixture\\n   160\\troot `fab223…` and the ten-cell census root are unchanged; the new pins\\n   161\\tfor the counterexamples equal the digests the review computed\\n   162\\tindependently for the same numbering.\\n   163\\t\\n   164\\t**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\\n   165\\t\\n   166\\t## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \\\"The procedure\\\")\\n   167\\t\\n   168\\tInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\\n   169\\tcells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\\n   170\\ttask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\\n   171\\trefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\\n   172\\trule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\\n   173\\t\\n   174\\t**Body label.** `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, the\\n   175\\tcursor cell's census body as plain data.\\n   176\\t\\n   177\\t**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\\n   178\\ttraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\\n   179\\tnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\\n   180\\trun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\\n   181\\tenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j <body label>]]`\\n   182\\tand a cursor cell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it.\\n   183\\tNothing else changes from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself,\\n   184\\t`[:row B]` expanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\\n   185\\t`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (this deliberately\\n   186\\treplaces step 2's `[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a\\n   187\\tname-keyed install entry, never inside an unordered container, so its\\n   188\\tcontent never decides an order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed\\n   189\\tstructural rows are spelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in\\n   190\\tnumeric register order; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise\\n   191\\tsorted vector of its elements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`),\\n   192\\tduplicates retained, where a tie group the run could not resolve is\\n   193\\tspelled in place as `[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element).\\n   194\\tAll comparisons are bytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic\\n   195\\tvectors. `CE` is hash-free and linear in the expansion of its argument.\\n   196\\t\\n   197\\t**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\\n   198\\trow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\\n   199\\t(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\\n   200\\tkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\\n   201\\t`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\\n   202\\t§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\\n   203\\tspecifies (the same as byte order for `N`, but the rule is numeric). The\\n   204\\tcensus is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is not entered. A\\n   205\\t`:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a **key**: the\\n   206\\tspelling of a local run over the element under the numbering at the\\n   207\\tmoment the container is entered (every element keyed from that one\\n   208\\tnumbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\\n   209\\tequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\\n   210\\torder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\\n   211\\tcontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\\n   212\\tan entry's key before its value.\\n   213\\t\\n   214\\t**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\\n   215\\t`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\\n   216\\tcell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\\n   217\\tcounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\\n   218\\tlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\\n   219\\tenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\\n   220\\tno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\\n   221\\t`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\\n   222\\tcontents table, so content rows keep sharing by Merkle id whether or not\\n   223\\tthey hold a cursor cell's first occurrence.\\n   224\\t\\n   225\\t**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\\n   226\\tre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\\n   227\\tthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\\n   228\\tre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\\n   229\\tcell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\\n   230\\tit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (the review's\\n   231\\t`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`) joins the same worklist and is re-examined in the\\n   232\\tnext round; there is one worklist per run, and the root run's worklist\\n   233\\tcollects deferred groups from every depth.\\n   234\\t\\n   235\\t**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\\n   236\\tlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\\n   237\\tcell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\\n   238\\tfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\\n   239\\tso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) If no component holds\\n   240\\ttwo elements of one group: encode each component by a local run over the\\n   241\\tsynthetic ordered row `[:component g₁ e₁ g₂ e₂ …]` of its elements in\\n   242\\tgroup order (`gᵢ` the group's deferral sequence number, a datum), require\\n   243\\tthat run to be complete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling, else D5), sort the\\n   244\\tcomponents by their encodings, and **enter the run of least encoding**:\\n   245\\teach of its components has its local run's cursor cells numbered, in that\\n   246\\trun's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's numbering;\\n   247\\tcomponents of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3, lemma C:\\n   248\\tequal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same). Remove the\\n   249\\tentered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group whose\\n   250\\telements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\\n   251\\tcarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\\n   252\\thost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\\n   253\\t\\n   254\\t**D5. Refusal.** If at any fixpoint of any run a component holds two\\n   255\\telements of one group (directly, or inside a component's local run), the\\n   256\\tlift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\\n   257\\t`:yin.k/path` the row path of the container holding that group, and a\\n   258\\t`:yin.k/cursor-cells` list of the component's unnumbered cursor cells.\\n   259\\tA receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing refuses `:yin.k/undecodable`\\n   260\\tkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is a resource refusal and neither\\n   261\\tis `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\\n   262\\t\\n   263\\t**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\\n   264\\tBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\\n   265\\tbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\\n   266\\tdefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\\n   267\\trefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\\n   268\\t\\n   269\\t**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\\n   270\\tlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\\n   271\\trestricted to the item's cursor cells, and of the enclosing local\\n   272\\tcounter; an implementation may memoize on that key and must return the\\n   273\\tnumbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the prototype's one\\n   274\\tbug during revision was returning the whole numbering of another\\n   275\\telement's context, which the decode round trip caught).\\n   276\\t\\n   277\\t**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\\n   278\\tthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\\n   279\\ton mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\\n   280\\t(`rows.cljc:570-575`); it must be wrapped so that **only** a lift-side\\n   281\\t`:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` raised by the\\n   282\\trecomputation is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\\n   283\\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the root id;\\n   284\\ta resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\\n   285\\titself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\\n   286\\talready checked literals and markers. The validator order stays: step 6\\n   287\\t(numbering) after step 5 and before `validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`;\\n   288\\ttest `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`).\\n   289\\t\\n   290\\t**Work.** Let `P` be the number of expanded positions of the task (the\\n   291\\ttree after expanding every `[:row B]` and structural reference in place;\\n   292\\ta shared row counts once per reference, so `P` can exceed the row count\\n   293\\tand the implementation must bound it, not the row count), `d` the\\n   294\\tcontainer nesting depth, `n` the number of cursor cells, `m` the largest\\n   295\\telement count of one container. Then, with memoized local runs, the\\n   296\\tprocedure visits each position at most `(n + 1)` times per nesting level\\n   297\\t(a local run over an element is recomputed only when the numbering\\n   298\\trestricted to that element's cursor cells changes, at most `n` times),\\n   299\\tevery encoding is linear in its element's expansion, and each container\\n   300\\tsort costs `O(B · log m)` compare work for `B` key bytes; so\\n   301\\t`W = O(P · (d + 1) · (n + 1) · log m)` positions-and-bytes, and the bytes\\n   302\\tmaterialized at any time are `O(W)` plus the memo, itself `O(P · (d + 1)\\n   303\\t· (n + 1))`. Without memoization the bound is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d+1) · log m)`.\\n   304\\tBoth are accounted before allocation by the budgets that stay: `:work`\\n   305\\tis charged one unit per position visited or spelled **and** one unit per\\n   306\\tliteral byte copied into a spelling (so every allocated spelling byte\\n   307\\thas been paid for), `:depth` bounds `d` and the structural depth as\\n   308\\ttoday, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` bounds the carried rows and\\n   309\\t`:bytes` the carried bytes, which together bound every literal a spelling\\n   310\\tcan copy. The `:search` and `:label-bytes` budgets disappear with the\\n   311\\tsearch and the growing labels. Exceeding a budget is the resource refusal\\n   312\\tof carrier §10.10, never a canonicality verdict.\\n   313\\t\\n   314\\t## 3. Proof of canonicality\\n   315\\t\\n   316\\tCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\\n   317\\t**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\\n   318\\tother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\\n   319\\torder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\\n   320\\t(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\\n   321\\tLet `S` be an abstract task.\\n   322\\t\\n   323\\t**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\\n   324\\tof a local run over it, and a local run reads only structure, body\\n   325\\tlabels and numbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order\\n   326\\tinside nested containers, which it never uses except through keys, by\\n   327\\tinduction on nesting depth, and (ii) the host order of components of\\n   328\\tequal encoding in its D4 step, covered by lemma C. So isomorphic items\\n   329\\tunder corresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted\\n   330\\torder of a container's elements is a function of the configuration up\\n   331\\tto isomorphism.\\n   332\\t\\n   333\\t**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\\n   334\\tconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\\n   335\\t\\n   336\\t**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\\n   337\\tof one run of equal encodings, none holding two elements of one group,\\n   338\\tand let `U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are\\n   339\\tpairwise disjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of\\n   340\\tan unnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\\n   341\\tEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ e₁ …]` rows are a faithful\\n   342\\tspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\\n   343\\tindex and body, groups by sequence number), so they exhibit, for each\\n   344\\tpair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a bijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the\\n   345\\tsame structure group by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives\\n   346\\tthe cells of `C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index;\\n   347\\tin order `σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The\\n   348\\tblock-swap bijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an\\n   349\\tisomorphism of the two resulting configurations: numbers agree by\\n   350\\tconstruction, structures agree because the swapped components are\\n   351\\tisomorphic and nothing outside them mentions their cells, and the\\n   352\\tworklists agree because the same groups remain, with the same elements\\n   353\\tremoved. ∎\\n   354\\t\\n   355\\t**Lemma D (refusal is invariant).** Whether a component holds two\\n   356\\telements of one group depends only on which tied elements share\\n   357\\tunnumbered cursor cells, which is a function of the configuration up to\\n   358\\tisomorphism; and whether a component's local run is complete is, by\\n   359\\tlemma A applied to that run, likewise invariant.\\n   360\\t\\n   361\\t**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\\n   362\\tsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\\n   363\\trows. *Proof.* By induction on the number of steps. D1 and D2 decisions\\n   364\\tare functions of the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only\\n   365\\trepeats D1 on deferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the\\n   366\\tleast run is canonical (lemma A on component encodings) and its only\\n   367\\tlisted-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant (lemma D).\\n   368\\tEmission spells cursor cells by number only and sorts every emitted\\n   369\\tunordered slot by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5),\\n   370\\tso isomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\\n   371\\t\\n   372\\tConsequences:\\n   373\\t\\n   374\\t1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\\n   375\\t   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\\n   376\\t   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\\n   377\\t   point lemma C covers.\\n   378\\t2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\\n   379\\t   3), so every traversal is finite. In the root run each D3 round that\\n   380\\t   is not the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least\\n   381\\t   one cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; local runs are finite\\n   382\\t   by the same argument one level down and there are finitely many\\n   383\\t   nesting levels. So the procedure ends after at most `n` numbering\\n   384\\t   events per run plus one terminal round, within the bound of §2 \\\"Work\\\".\\n   385\\t3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\\n   386\\t   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\\n   387\\t4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\\n   388\\t   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\\n   389\\t   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\\n   390\\t   position get two numbers.\\n   391\\t5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\\n   392\\t   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\\n   393\\t   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\\n   394\\t\\n   395\\tWhat is given up: the procedure does not number every state. D5 names\\n   396\\tthe refused class exactly (§1 F4); a later bounded search over that\\n   397\\tresidue would not change the numbering of any state D1–D4 number today,\\n   398\\tso the refusal can be narrowed without churning ids.\\n   399\\t\\n   400\\t## 4. Amendments\\n   401\\t\\n   402\\t**A1 — carrier §6, from \\\"The procedure.\\\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\\n   403\\twith §2 above (vocabulary, the body label, the spelling, D1–D5, totality,\\n   404\\twork). Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend\\n   405\\t\\\"What is defined, and what is not claimed\\\" to: \\\"The canonical numbering\\n   406\\tis defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is\\n   407\\ta function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\\n   408\\tenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\\n   409\\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\\n   410\\tminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. A state in which two\\n   411\\tequal-keyed elements of one guest set or map are linked by shared\\n   412\\tunnumbered cursor cells at a fixpoint is refused (D5), not searched; the\\n   413\\trefusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and can be narrowed\\n   414\\tlater without changing any numbering assigned today.\\\" State in step 2's\\n   415\\treplacement that `[:n k]` is an exact CBOR integer and that `[:child]`\\n   416\\treplaces `[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \\\"Spelling\\\").\\n   417\\t\\n   418\\t**A2 — carrier §6 \\\"Validation\\\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\\n   419\\tsentences, from \\\"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\\n   420\\tthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\\\"\\n   421\\tto the end, with: \\\"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\\n   422\\tthereby accepted. The procedure reads the canonical traversal order of\\n   423\\tD1, which is a function of the state; it never reads the host's\\n   424\\tenumeration order of a set, map or census, and a receiver never decides\\n   425\\tvalidity by its own enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural\\n   426\\trefusal, not a lift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is\\n   427\\texceeded before the procedure completes refuses with the structured\\n   428\\tresource refusal before any attachment, never labels the state\\n   429\\t`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never substitutes host enumeration order,\\n   430\\tand never accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches D5\\n   431\\trefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\\\"\\n   432\\t\\n   433\\t**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \\\"The class-erased skeletons of §6\\n   434\\tare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\\n   435\\tof an emitted row\\\" with \\\"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\\n   436\\tused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\\n   437\\tan emitted row\\\".\\n   438\\t\\n   439\\t**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \\\"Recompute §6 under the work bound and\\n   440\\tcompare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`;\\n   441\\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached,\\n   442\\ttranslated from the recomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only;\\n   443\\tor the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded, which\\n   444\\tpropagates untranslated).\\\" In the diagnostics paragraph after step 12,\\n   445\\tadd `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds named,\\n   446\\tand change \\\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set:\\n   447\\t`:byte-string` and `:metadata`\\\" to \\\"the three kinds this carrier adds to\\n   448\\tUCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`, `:metadata` and\\n   449\\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\\\". In §11, \\\"two lift kinds and two\\n   450\\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\\\" becomes \\\"three lift kinds and three\\n   451\\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\\\".\\n   452\\t\\n   453\\t**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \\\"a renaming-invariant\\n   454\\trefinement-and-search procedure\\\" → \\\"the discovery-order procedure, which\\n   455\\tis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\\\". Answer 10: \\\"a bound\\n   456\\ton decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\\n   457\\tand search nodes for §6\\\" → \\\"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\\n   458\\ttraversal depth, and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work`\\n   459\\tunit counts positions and copied literal bytes, so spellings are paid\\n   460\\tfor before they are allocated)\\\"; and \\\"never falls back to encounter\\n   461\\torder\\\" → \\\"never substitutes host enumeration order for the canonical\\n   462\\ttraversal\\\". Proposals list: \\\"as defined by its procedure\\\" → \\\"as defined\\n   463\\tby discovery order, and the D5 refusal class\\\".\\n   464\\t\\n   465\\t**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \\\"minted by the lift under §6's\\n   466\\tcanonical numbering\\\" add \\\"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\\n   467\\ttraversal)\\\".\\n   468\\t\\n   469\\t**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \\\"numbered by the draft's §6\\n   470\\tprocedure\\\" with \\\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\\n   471\\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\\\".\\n   472\\t\\n   473\\t**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \\\"Draft §6 is\\n   474\\tinvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\\n   475\\tan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\\n   476\\tthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\\n   477\\t(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\\n   478\\tin cursor-cell bodies\\\" → \\\"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\\n   479\\t**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\\n   480\\tother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\\n   481\\tbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\\n   482\\tas amended) and in cursor-cell bodies\\\". (ii) \\\"Unordered containers\\n   483\\t(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\\n   484\\tsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\\n   485\\thost traversal order\\\" → \\\"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\\n   486\\torder of their elements' spellings, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the\\n   487\\tparked table in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 D1),\\n   488\\tnever in host enumeration order\\\". (iii) Leave \\\"never derived from\\n   489\\ttraversal order\\\" but qualify it: \\\"never derived from *host* traversal\\n   490\\torder (the canonical traversal of draft §6 D1 is a function of the\\n   491\\tstate and is what the procedure reads)\\\".\\n   492\\t\\n   493\\t**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–21\\n   494\\tof §8 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\\n   495\\t\\n   496\\t**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \\\"minted by the emitter (stable within\\n   497\\tone lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\\\" remains true.\\n   498\\t\\n   499\\t## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\\n   500\\t\\n   501\\tThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\\n   502\\tcreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\\n   503\\tmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\\n   504\\t(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\\n   505\\tcounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\\n   506\\tas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\\n   507\\texecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\\n   508\\t`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\\n   509\\tsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\\n   510\\tcounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\\n   511\\tstructure:\\n   512\\t\\n   513\\t- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\\n   514\\t  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\\n   515\\t  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\\n   516\\t  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\\n   517\\t  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\\n   518\\t  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\\n   519\\t  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\\n   520\\t  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\\n   521\\t  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\\n   522\\t  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\\n   523\\t- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\\n   524\\t  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\\n   525\\t  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\\n   526\\t  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\\n   527\\t- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\\n   528\\t  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\\n   529\\t  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\\n   530\\t  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\\n   531\\t  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\\n   532\\t  relative to its state.\\n   533\\t\\n   534\\tDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\\n   535\\tidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\\n   536\\tn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\\n   537\\tposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\\n   538\\tids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\\n   539\\tonly kind proposed.\\n   540\\t\\n   541\\t## 6. Prototype\\n   542\\t\\n   543\\t`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\\n   544\\tscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D5 as\\n   545\\tone schema-driven run procedure (root run and local runs share the code;\\n   546\\ta local run differs only in its number tag and in spelling residues as\\n   547\\t`[:tie]`), memoized as §2 describes, with the totality assertion, an\\n   548\\temitter that numbers install children by the same procedure, a decode\\n   549\\tpath that runs 6a's `decode*` with its numbering check off and then\\n   550\\trecomputes, compares and translates only `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, and\\n   551\\ta permissive diagnostic mode that replaces D5 by host-order entry to\\n   552\\ttest whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable. Run from the\\n   553\\tprobe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private helpers):\\n   554\\t\\n   555\\t```\\n   556\\tclojure -M -e \\\"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\\\"\\n   557\\t```\\n   558\\t\\n   559\\tResults of 2026-10-12 (revised prototype; 6a tests pass in the same\\n   560\\tproject, 21 tests, 324 assertions; full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`):\\n   561\\t\\n   562\\t| State | Discovery order | 6a |\\n   563\\t|---|---|---|\\n   564\\t| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same root |\\n   565\\t| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | root `2d9873a8…` |\\n   566\\t| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same root |\\n   567\\t| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same roots |\\n   568\\t| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | `71543fe2…` / same |\\n   569\\t| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest for this numbering) | `387f294a…` |\\n   570\\t| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | `be62ba43…` |\\n   571\\t| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same root |\\n   572\\t| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same root |\\n   573\\t| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | `a850fce6…` |\\n   574\\t| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | `e61d3709…` |\\n   575\\t| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (the first element has two cursor cells, the second four); root `b35b8e12…` | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\\n   576\\t| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells` on `{A, B}` | root `55ff168e…` |\\n   577\\t| 2-cycle, 3-cycle | refused; permissive mode gives one root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | numbered |\\n   578\\t| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | numbered |\\n   579\\t| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive mode gives 4 roots (not interchangeable) | refused `:resource-limit [:label-bytes]` |\\n   580\\t| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same root |\\n   581\\t| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3` (the wait's pending); decodes | different root (6a numbers by its own search) |\\n   582\\t| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1` (slot 8 precedes frames, slot 13), `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | different root |\\n   583\\t| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2` by discovery order; one child row shared; decodes | — |\\n   584\\t| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (15 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 10 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation) | — |\\n   585\\t| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget | — |\\n   586\\t| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget | — |\\n   587\\t\\n   588\\tHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\\n   589\\tfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\\n   590\\treview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\\n   591\\tFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\\n   592\\tkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\\n   593\\t(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\\n   594\\t`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) sorts before\\n   595\\t`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\\n   596\\tcounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 b]` twice) and is\\n   597\\tdeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\\n   598\\tagainst `[:u 0 b]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 b] [u0 b]]`\\n   599\\tand `[:vec [u0 b] [u1 b]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\\n   600\\tis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\\n   601\\tentries `[:entry [u0 b] [u0 b]]` and `[:entry [u0 b] [u1 b]]` likewise.\\n   602\\tReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 b] [u1 b]]` (each local run\\n   603\\tresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\\n   604\\tthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\\n   605\\tcomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\\n   606\\teither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\\n   607\\tand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\\n   608\\tequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 b] 2 [u0 b]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\\n   609\\tin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\\n   610\\tmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. Loop plus\\n   611\\t2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 b] [u0 b]`, the two others as `[u0 b] [u1 b]`,\\n   612\\tso `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}` ties, the fixpoint changes\\n   613\\tnothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}` holds two elements of one\\n   614\\tgroup: D5. The example state of `collab/example-suspended-state.clj`\\n   615\\thas one cursor cell, first met in the act's environment under `c`, so\\n   616\\t`cc-1`; a one-cell state has the same rows under both procedures.\\n   617\\t\\n   618\\tPins that moved from the first draft: none of the first draft's pins\\n   619\\tchange (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`, `a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\\n   620\\t`49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, the ten-cell root); the first\\n   621\\tdraft refused `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` (now `92747c0f…`), numbered review A and\\n   622\\tB inconsistently (now `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`) and left review C\\n   623\\tunnumbered (now `18b70de6…`); `7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…` and `b35b8e12…`\\n   624\\tare new pins.\\n   625\\t\\n   626\\t## 7. Recommendation\\n   627\\t\\n   628\\t**(a) Adopt discovery order now.** 6b has not started and 6a is\\n   629\\tunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\\n   630\\ttests plus the amendments of §4: delete `check-label-bytes!`\\n   631\\t(`rows.cljc:212-230`), `positions` (`:285-325`), `classes` (`:326-330`),\\n   632\\t`refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`) and the label seeding\\n   633\\t(`:252-255`); replace `ce` (`:257-284`) by the spelling of §2; add the\\n   634\\trun procedure (traversal, worklist, fixpoint, components, D5, totality\\n   635\\tassertion, memo) and the decode translation (the prototype's procedure\\n   636\\tis about 230 lines with the schema helpers; 150–200 in place, portable\\n   637\\t`.cljc`: no `@#'` reflection, `mapv`/`into`/`reduce` over long sequences,\\n   638\\t`contains?` for membership, continuation §12); delete the `:search` and\\n   639\\t`:label-bytes` budget keys (`:200-202`) and charge `:work` per position\\n   640\\tand per copied literal byte. Unchanged: `emit`, `emit-numbered`,\\n   641\\t`canonical-under`, the census and its numeric order (`:140-156`),\\n   642\\t`decode*`'s recompute-and-compare (`:570-575`) apart from the\\n   643\\ttranslation, numbering before `validate-frames` (`:576-578`; test\\n   644\\t`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`), the `:bytes`,\\n   645\\t`:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells` and `:work` bounds. Tests: see §8.\\n   646\\tCost: one implementation round and one review round, and the review can\\n   647\\tcheck §3 by hand. Risk: a canonicality bug, mitigated by §8 item 18's\\n   648\\tgenerator in CI (it caught the prototype's memo bug), by the decode round\\n   649\\ttrip in that generator, and by the proof; and the D5 class, documented\\n   650\\texactly and narrowable later without id churn.\\n   651\\t\\n   652\\t**(b) Keep the 6a design.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine\\n   653\\tharness and every reviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search,\\n   654\\tlabels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte, and a\\n   655\\tdefinition that is \\\"whatever the procedure outputs\\\". Not recommended.\\n   656\\t\\n   657\\t**(c) Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the\\n   658\\tharness (§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\\n   659\\t\\n   660\\t## 8. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\\n   661\\t\\n   662\\t12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\\n   663\\t    the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]` and the\\n   664\\t    two frame fixtures of §6, `canonical-numbering` returns exactly the\\n   665\\t    maps of §6.\\n   666\\t13. `rows-test/review-counterexample-resolves-by-fixpoint`: `'a #{X Y}`,\\n   667\\t    `'b X` numbers `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; the task renamed and with the set\\n   668\\t    reversed gives the same root; the root is pinned `a3713a5e…`.\\n   669\\t14. `rows-test/component-rule`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\\n   670\\t    `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` number without refusal;\\n   671\\t    element reversal and renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`,\\n   672\\t    `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`.\\n   673\\t15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\\n   674\\t    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\\n   675\\t    pinned `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`; `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}`\\n   676\\t    numbers and is pinned `b35b8e12…`.\\n   677\\t16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the loop plus 2-cycle,\\n   678\\t    the 2-cycle, the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell state refuse\\n   679\\t    at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\\n   680\\t    a path naming the set and the component's cursor cells; a carrier of\\n   681\\t    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\\n   682\\t    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a\\n   683\\t    carrier of the loop plus 2-cycle with a `{:work 1}` budget refuses\\n   684\\t    `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`, not `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\\n   685\\t17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\\n   686\\t    unchanged; `a3713a5e…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, `7dc3d5c4…`,\\n   687\\t    `6ea03f9b…` added; `55ff168e…` removed.\\n   688\\t18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (one to six\\n   689\\t    cursor cells over two bodies; nested `:vec`, `:set`, `:map` with\\n   690\\t    cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including `[X X]`, sets of\\n   691\\t    sets, under keyed bindings and the result; unique elements and keys\\n   692\\t    under decoded equality) over at least 2000 states, plus at least 200\\n   693\\t    frame-bearing states (cursor cells in an act window, a K-frame\\n   694\\t    window, environments matched to the free environment, and a parked\\n   695\\t    state at a park site) and at least 100 states with install children:\\n   696\\t    every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its root, and\\n   697\\t    renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and census\\n   698\\t    gives an equal root id; refusals are counted and reported.\\n   699\\t19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a task whose census declares a\\n   700\\t    cursor cell the traversal does not reach raises the totality defect\\n   701\\t    (not a refusal) before any row is emitted.\\n   702\\t20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\\n   703\\t    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\\n   704\\t    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\\n   705\\t    and `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\\n   706\\t    large-input regression of `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\\n   707\\t    is kept as `large-literals-do-not-spend-work-beyond-their-bytes`\\n   708\\t    without its label-budget expectation.\\n   709\\t21. Budgets and docs: the `:search` and `:label-bytes` keys are gone;\\n   710\\t    `{:work 1}` still refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`; A1–A9\\n   711\\t    applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node\\n   712\\t    log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\\n   713\\t\\n   714\\t## 9. Owner questions\\n   715\\t\\n   716\\t1. Is the D5 class, as stated in §1 F4 (exact predicate, no rarity\\n   717\\t   claim, unmeasured on real states until 6b lifts one), acceptable as a\\n   718\\t   documented limitation of body version 3 (recommended: yes; it can be\\n   719\\t   narrowed later by a bounded search over the residue without changing\\n   720\\t   any id numbered today)?\\n   721\\t2. Should the refusal kind be `:unresolved-cursor-cells` as proposed,\\n   722\\t   or the orchestrator's `:resource-limit` (recommended against: it is\\n   723\\t   not a budget, and carrier §10.10 says a resource refusal never\\n   724\\t   stands in for a canonicality verdict)?\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1792170000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:12:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/numbering-probe/deps.edn; nl -ba collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"{:deps {datomworld/root {:local/root \\\"../..\\\"}}\\n :paths [\\\"src\\\" \\\"../../test\\\"]}\\n     1\\t(ns probe.discovery\\n     2\\t  \\\"Prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (note D1-D5 as revised\\n     3\\t   2026-10-12 after the codex review) over the 6a abstract task representation\\n     4\\t   (yin.vm.ucf.rows). Not repo code; a scratch project under collab/.\\n     5\\t\\n     6\\t   Vocabulary: UCF 7.5.3. A cursor cell is the VM box the carrier serializes;\\n     7\\t   its source id is the lift's local name; the procedure assigns :yin.k/cc-n.\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\t   Shape of the procedure (one algorithm, two uses):\\n    10\\t   - a RUN traverses an item (D1), numbers cursor cells on first meeting (D2),\\n    11\\t     sorts each :set / :map by the local keys of its elements and defers equal\\n    12\\t     runs to the run's worklist (D1), re-examines the worklist to a fixpoint\\n    13\\t     (D3), groups the surviving tied elements into components by shared\\n    14\\t     unnumbered cursor cells, refuses a component holding two elements of one\\n    15\\t     group (D5), encodes each component by a nested local run, and adopts the\\n    16\\t     least encoding's components (D4); repeat until the worklist is empty.\\n    17\\t   - the root run assigns [:n k], k = 1, 2, ...; a LOCAL run (used only to\\n    18\\t     compute the key of an element or of a component) starts from the\\n    19\\t     enclosing run's numbering and assigns [:u j] from the enclosing run's\\n    20\\t     local counter; its spelling with surviving ties spelled [:tie K ...] is\\n    21\\t     the key. Local runs are memoized on (item, numbering restricted to the\\n    22\\t     item's cursor cells, local counter).\\\"\\n    23\\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\\n    24\\t            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\\n    25\\t            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\\n    26\\t            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]\\n    27\\t            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]))\\n    28\\t\\n    29\\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schema\\n    30\\t\\n    31\\t(def synthetic-table\\n    32\\t  \\\"Rows that exist only inside the procedure: a :map entry as an ordered pair,\\n    33\\t   and a component as the ordered list of its (group-id element) pairs.\\\"\\n    34\\t  {:entry [:V :V] :component [:pairs :D :V]})\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\t(defn descriptors [kind slots]\\n    37\\t  (let [schema (or (get rows/row-table kind) (get synthetic-table kind))]\\n    38\\t    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\\n    39\\t          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\\n    40\\t          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\\n    41\\t          :else schema)))\\n    42\\t\\n    43\\t(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\\n    44\\t(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\\n    45\\t(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\\n    46\\t\\n    47\\t(defn bytes-key [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\\n    48\\t\\n    49\\t(defn compare-bytes [a b]\\n    50\\t  (loop [i 0]\\n    51\\t    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\\n    52\\t          (= i (count b)) 1\\n    53\\t          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\\n    54\\t          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\\n    55\\t\\n    56\\t(defn by-key-bytes [f xs] (sort #(compare-bytes (f %1) (f %2)) xs))\\n    57\\t\\n    58\\t(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table})\\n    59\\t\\n    60\\t(defn elem-item\\n    61\\t  \\\"The item an element is entered and keyed as: a :set element is its V; a\\n    62\\t   :map entry [k v] is the synthetic ordered row [:entry k v], so that the\\n    63\\t   alias pattern across key and value is part of the key.\\\"\\n    64\\t  [kind e]\\n    65\\t  (if (= :map kind) [:row {:kind :entry :slots (vec e)}] e))\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\t(defn child-task? [ctx n] (and (= :task (:kind n)) (not (identical? n (:task ctx)))))\\n    68\\t\\n    69\\t(defn item-cells\\n    70\\t  \\\"Cursor cells occurring in slot (d x); census and install children excluded.\\n    71\\t   Memoized per procedure on the slot value.\\\"\\n    72\\t  [ctx d x]\\n    73\\t  (or (get @(:cells-memo ctx) [d x])\\n    74\\t      (let [acc (atom #{})]\\n    75\\t        (letfn [(slot [d x]\\n    76\\t                  (cond (ref? d) (node x)\\n    77\\t                        (refs? d) (run! node x)\\n    78\\t                        (= :C d) (swap! acc conj x)\\n    79\\t                        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\\n    80\\t                                         :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second x))\\n    81\\t                                         :row (node (second x))\\n    82\\t                                         nil))\\n    83\\t                        :else nil))\\n    84\\t                (node [n]\\n    85\\t                  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\\n    86\\t                    (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells kind) (child-task? ctx n))\\n    87\\t                      (dorun (map slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\\n    88\\t          (slot d x)\\n    89\\t          (swap! (:cells-memo ctx) assoc [d x] @acc)\\n    90\\t          @acc))))\\n    91\\t\\n    92\\t(defn body-label [ctx c]\\n    93\\t  (let [[stream position] (:slots (get (:bodies ctx) c))]\\n    94\\t    [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\\n    95\\t\\n    96\\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ runs\\n    97\\t\\n    98\\t(def ^:dynamic *trace* false)\\n    99\\t\\n   100\\t(defn- new-run [ctx num tag next]\\n   101\\t  {:ctx ctx :num (atom num) :tag tag :next (atom next)\\n   102\\t   :groups (atom []) :gid (atom 0) :refused (atom nil)})\\n   103\\t\\n   104\\t(defn- meet!\\n   105\\t  \\\"D2. A cursor cell met for the first time receives the run's next number.\\\"\\n   106\\t  [run c]\\n   107\\t  (when-not (contains? @(:num run) c)\\n   108\\t    (let [k @(:next run)]\\n   109\\t      (swap! (:num run) assoc c [(:tag run) k])\\n   110\\t      (swap! (:next run) inc)\\n   111\\t      true)))\\n   112\\t\\n   113\\t(declare enter-node! enter-slot! unordered! local-run resolve! spell-slot spell-node)\\n   114\\t\\n   115\\t(defn- enter-slot! [run d x path]\\n   116\\t  (cond (ref? d) (enter-node! run x path)\\n   117\\t        (refs? d) (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i n] (enter-node! run n (conj path i))) x))\\n   118\\t        (= :C d) (meet! run x)\\n   119\\t        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\\n   120\\t                         :cursor-cell (meet! run (second x))\\n   121\\t                         :row (enter-node! run (second x) path)\\n   122\\t                         nil))\\n   123\\t        :else nil))\\n   124\\t\\n   125\\t(defn- enter-node!\\n   126\\t  \\\"D1. Canonical traversal: table order for slots; ordered repeated slots in\\n   127\\t   order; keyed structural rows in the Jing byte order of the key; :window in\\n   128\\t   ascending register order; installs and module stores by name; :set and\\n   129\\t   :map by local keys with ties deferred; census and children not entered.\\\"\\n   130\\t  [run n path]\\n   131\\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\\n   132\\t    (cond\\n   133\\t      (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\\n   134\\t      (child-task? ctx n) nil\\n   135\\t      (= :task kind)\\n   136\\t      (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\\n   137\\t        (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\\n   138\\t          (doseq [child (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first (:slots %))) x)]\\n   139\\t            (enter-node! run child (conj path i (first (:slots child)))))\\n   140\\t          (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))))\\n   141\\t      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\\n   142\\t      (doseq [[k v] (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %)) (partition 2 slots))]\\n   143\\t        (enter-slot! run (second ds) v (conj path k)))\\n   144\\t      (= :window kind)\\n   145\\t      (doseq [[r v] (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))] (enter-slot! run :V v (conj path r)))\\n   146\\t      (= :set kind) (unordered! run :set (vec slots) path)\\n   147\\t      (= :map kind) (unordered! run :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)) path)\\n   148\\t      :else (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\\n   149\\t\\n   150\\t(defn- local-key [run item]\\n   151\\t  (let [r (local-run run item)] [(bytes-key (:spell r)) (:spell r)]))\\n   152\\t\\n   153\\t(defn- unordered!\\n   154\\t  \\\"D1 for an unordered container: key every element by a local run under the\\n   155\\t   numbering at this moment; enter singletons in key order; defer equal runs\\n   156\\t   as one tie group (deferral order = canonical order).\\\"\\n   157\\t  [run kind elems path]\\n   158\\t  (let [keyed (mapv (fn [[i e]] [(local-key run (elem-item kind e)) e i]) (map-indexed vector elems))\\n   159\\t        groups (partition-by (comp first first) (sort #(compare-bytes (first (first %1)) (first (first %2))) keyed))]\\n   160\\t    (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\\n   161\\t      (println \\\"  [sort]\\\" path \\\"num\\\" @(:num run))\\n   162\\t      (doseq [g groups] (println \\\"    \\\" (if (= 1 (count g)) \\\"enter\\\" \\\"DEFER\\\") (mapv (fn [[[_ sp] e _]] [e sp]) g))))\\n   163\\t    (doseq [g groups]\\n   164\\t      (if (= 1 (count g))\\n   165\\t        (let [[_ e i] (first g)] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))\\n   166\\t        (swap! (:groups run) conj {:id (swap! (:gid run) inc) :kind kind :path path\\n   167\\t                                   :key (second (first (first g))) :elems (mapv second g)})))))\\n   168\\t\\n   169\\t(defn- fixpoint!\\n   170\\t  \\\"D3. Re-examine every deferred group in deferral order under the current\\n   171\\t   numbering; repeat while a round numbers a cursor cell and groups remain.\\\"\\n   172\\t  [run]\\n   173\\t  (loop []\\n   174\\t    (let [before (count @(:num run)) groups @(:groups run)]\\n   175\\t      (reset! (:groups run) [])\\n   176\\t      (doseq [{:keys [kind elems path]} groups] (unordered! run kind elems path))\\n   177\\t      (when (and (seq @(:groups run)) (> (count @(:num run)) before)) (recur)))))\\n   178\\t\\n   179\\t(defn- components\\n   180\\t  \\\"D4. Tied elements of all surviving groups, linked by shared unnumbered\\n   181\\t   cursor cells (union-find). A component is :conflict when it holds two\\n   182\\t   elements of one group.\\\"\\n   183\\t  [run]\\n   184\\t  (let [num @(:num run) ctx (:ctx run)\\n   185\\t        elems (vec (for [g @(:groups run) [i e] (map-indexed vector (:elems g))]\\n   186\\t                     {:group (:id g) :kind (:kind g) :elem e :idx i\\n   187\\t                      :cells (set (remove #(contains? num %) (item-cells ctx :V (elem-item (:kind g) e))))}))\\n   188\\t        parent (atom (vec (range (count elems))))\\n   189\\t        find (fn find [i] (let [p (@parent i)] (if (= p i) i (let [r (find p)] (swap! parent assoc i r) r))))\\n   190\\t        union (fn [i j] (let [ri (find i) rj (find j)] (when (not= ri rj) (swap! parent assoc ri rj))))\\n   191\\t        by-cell (reduce (fn [m [i el]] (reduce (fn [m c] (update m c (fnil conj []) i)) m (:cells el)))\\n   192\\t                        {} (map-indexed vector elems))]\\n   193\\t    (doseq [[_ is] by-cell] (doseq [j (rest is)] (union (first is) j)))\\n   194\\t    (->> (group-by find (range (count elems)))\\n   195\\t         (sort-by first)\\n   196\\t         (mapv (fn [[_ is]]\\n   197\\t                 (let [members (vec (sort-by (juxt :group :idx) (map elems is)))\\n   198\\t                       groups (map :group members)]\\n   199\\t                   {:members members\\n   200\\t                    :conflict (not= (count groups) (count (distinct groups)))\\n   201\\t                    :group (first groups)\\n   202\\t                    :cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat :cells members))))}))))))\\n   203\\t\\n   204\\t(defn- component-item [comp]\\n   205\\t  [:row {:kind :component\\n   206\\t         :slots (vec (mapcat (fn [m] [(:group m) (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m))]) (:members comp)))}])\\n   207\\t\\n   208\\t(defn- adopt!\\n   209\\t  \\\"D4. Enter a component by adopting its local run: its fresh cursor cells\\n   210\\t   receive the enclosing run's next numbers in local-index order.\\\"\\n   211\\t  [run comp]\\n   212\\t  (let [fresh (->> (:num (:ce comp))\\n   213\\t                   (remove (fn [[c _]] (contains? @(:num run) c)))\\n   214\\t                   (sort-by (comp second val)))]\\n   215\\t    (doseq [[c _] fresh] (meet! run c))))\\n   216\\t\\n   217\\t(defn- remove-elements! [run comps]\\n   218\\t  (let [gone (set (for [c comps m (:members c)] [(:group m) (:idx m)]))]\\n   219\\t    (swap! (:groups run)\\n   220\\t           (fn [gs] (vec (keep (fn [g]\\n   221\\t                                 (let [es (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (gone [(:id g) i]) e)) (:elems g)))]\\n   222\\t                                   (when (seq es) (assoc g :elems es))))\\n   223\\t                               gs))))))\\n   224\\t\\n   225\\t(defn- resolve!\\n   226\\t  \\\"D3-D5 to completion. Leaves :refused set (D5) with the worklist as of the\\n   227\\t   last fixpoint, or the worklist empty.\\\"\\n   228\\t  [run]\\n   229\\t  (loop []\\n   230\\t    (fixpoint! run)\\n   231\\t    (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\\n   232\\t      (println \\\"  [resolve] num\\\" @(:num run) \\\"groups\\\" (mapv (fn [g] [(:id g) (:path g) (mapv (fn [e] (if (= :cursor-cell (first e)) (second e) e)) (:elems g))]) @(:groups run))))\\n   233\\t    (when (seq @(:groups run))\\n   234\\t      (let [comps (components run)\\n   235\\t            conflict (first (filter :conflict comps))]\\n   236\\t        (if conflict\\n   237\\t          (if (:permissive (:ctx run))\\n   238\\t            ;; Diagnostic only: enter the conflicting component in listed order.\\n   239\\t            (do (doseq [m (:members conflict)] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m)) [:permissive]))\\n   240\\t                (remove-elements! run [conflict])\\n   241\\t                (recur))\\n   242\\t            (reset! (:refused run) {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\\n   243\\t                                    :path (:path (first (filter #(= (:group conflict) (:id %)) @(:groups run))))\\n   244\\t                                    :cursor-cells (:cells conflict)}))\\n   245\\t          (let [comps (mapv #(assoc % :ce (local-run run (component-item %))) comps)]\\n   246\\t            (if-let [bad (first (filter #(not (:complete? (:ce %))) comps))]\\n   247\\t              (reset! (:refused run) (assoc (:refused (:ce bad)) :kind :unresolved-cursor-cells :nested true))\\n   248\\t              (let [sorted (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %1))) (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %2)))) comps)\\n   249\\t                    least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce (first sorted))))\\n   250\\t                    least-run (take-while #(= least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %)))) sorted)]\\n   251\\t                (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\\n   252\\t                  (doseq [c sorted] (println \\\"  [component]\\\" (mapv (fn [m] [(:group m) (:cells m)]) (:members c)) \\\"CE\\\" (:spell (:ce c))))\\n   253\\t                  (println \\\"  [adopt]\\\" (count least-run)))\\n   254\\t                (doseq [c least-run] (adopt! run c))\\n   255\\t                (remove-elements! run least-run)\\n   256\\t                (recur)))))))))\\n   257\\t\\n   258\\t(defn- local-run\\n   259\\t  \\\"A nested run on one item from the enclosing run's numbering, tag :u. Its\\n   260\\t   result is a key: the spelling under its final numbering, surviving ties\\n   261\\t   spelled [:tie K ...]. Memoized.\\\"\\n   262\\t  [run item]\\n   263\\t  (let [ctx (:ctx run)\\n   264\\t        cells (item-cells ctx :V item)\\n   265\\t        num @(:num run)\\n   266\\t        start (if (= :u (:tag run)) @(:next run) 0)\\n   267\\t        mk [item (select-keys num cells) start]]\\n   268\\t    (or (get @(:memo ctx) mk)\\n   269\\t        (let [sub (new-run ctx num :u start)]\\n   270\\t          (enter-slot! sub :V item [])\\n   271\\t          (resolve! sub)\\n   272\\t          (let [r {:spell (spell-slot sub :V item [])\\n   273\\t                   ;; Restricted to the item's own cursor cells: the result is a\\n   274\\t                   ;; function of the memo key, never of the enclosing context.\\n   275\\t                   :num (select-keys @(:num sub) cells)\\n   276\\t                   :complete? (and (nil? @(:refused sub)) (empty? @(:groups sub)))\\n   277\\t                   :refused @(:refused sub)}]\\n   278\\t            (swap! (:memo ctx) assoc mk r)\\n   279\\t            r)))))\\n   280\\t\\n   281\\t;; -------------------------------------------------------------- spelling\\n   282\\t\\n   283\\t(defn- spell-cell [run c]\\n   284\\t  (let [[t k] (get @(:num run) c)]\\n   285\\t    (case t\\n   286\\t      :n [:cursor-cell [:n k]]\\n   287\\t      :u [:cursor-cell [:u k (body-label (:ctx run) c)]]\\n   288\\t      (throw (ex-info \\\"spelling an unnumbered cursor cell\\\" {:cursor-cell c})))))\\n   289\\t\\n   290\\t(defn- spell-slot [run d x path]\\n   291\\t  (cond (ref? d) (spell-node run x path)\\n   292\\t        (refs? d) (vec (map-indexed (fn [i n] (spell-node run n (conj path i))) x))\\n   293\\t        (= :C d) (spell-cell run x)\\n   294\\t        (v? d) (if (nil? x) x\\n   295\\t                   (case (first x)\\n   296\\t                     :cursor-cell (spell-cell run (second x))\\n   297\\t                     :row [:row (spell-node run (second x) path)]\\n   298\\t                     x))\\n   299\\t        :else x))\\n   300\\t\\n   301\\t(defn- spell-node\\n   302\\t  \\\"The content encoding CE of a row under the run's final numbering.\\\"\\n   303\\t  [run n path]\\n   304\\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\\n   305\\t    (cond\\n   306\\t      (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\\n   307\\t      (child-task? ctx n) [:child]\\n   308\\t      (= :task kind)\\n   309\\t      (into [kind]\\n   310\\t            (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]]\\n   311\\t                           (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\\n   312\\t                             (vec (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (nth % 1))\\n   313\\t                                                (map #(spell-node run % (conj path i (first (:slots %)))) x)))\\n   314\\t                             (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))))\\n   315\\t                         (map vector ds slots)))\\n   316\\t      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\\n   317\\t      (into [kind] (mapcat identity (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %))\\n   318\\t                                                  (map (fn [[k v]] [k (spell-slot run (second ds) v (conj path k))])\\n   319\\t                                                       (partition 2 slots)))))\\n   320\\t      (= :window kind)\\n   321\\t      (into [kind] (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [r (spell-slot run :V v (conj path r))]) (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\\n   322\\t      (contains? #{:set :map} kind)\\n   323\\t      (let [elems (if (= :set kind) (vec slots) (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\\n   324\\t            tied (filter #(= path (:path %)) @(:groups run))\\n   325\\t            tied-elems (set (mapcat :elems tied))\\n   326\\t            plain (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (tied-elems e) (spell-slot run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))) elems)\\n   327\\t            ties (map #(into [:tie] (repeat (count (:elems %)) (:key %))) tied)]\\n   328\\t        (into [kind] (by-key-bytes bytes-key (concat plain ties))))\\n   329\\t      :else (into [kind] (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\\n   330\\t\\n   331\\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------- procedure\\n   332\\t\\n   333\\t(defn discovery-numbering\\n   334\\t  \\\"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n}} or {:refused {...}}. Throws when\\n   335\\t   the numbering is not total (a defect, never a refusal).\\\"\\n   336\\t  ([task] (discovery-numbering task {}))\\n   337\\t  ([task opts]\\n   338\\t   (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\\n   339\\t         ctx {:task task\\n   340\\t              :bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\\n   341\\t              :memo (atom {}) :cells-memo (atom {})\\n   342\\t              :permissive (:permissive opts)}\\n   343\\t         run (new-run ctx {} :n 1)]\\n   344\\t     (enter-node! run task [])\\n   345\\t     (resolve! run)\\n   346\\t     (if-let [r @(:refused run)]\\n   347\\t       {:refused r}\\n   348\\t       (let [numbered (set (keys @(:num run))) declared (set (keys (:bodies ctx)))]\\n   349\\t         (when (seq @(:groups run)) (throw (ex-info \\\"residue after resolve\\\" {})))\\n   350\\t         (when (not= numbered declared)\\n   351\\t           (throw (ex-info \\\"numbering is not total\\\" {:missing (vec (remove numbered declared))\\n   352\\t                                                      :extra (vec (remove declared numbered))})))\\n   353\\t         {:numbering (into {} (map (fn [[c [_ k]]] [c (keyword \\\"yin.k\\\" (str \\\"cc-\\\" k))])) @(:num run))})))))\\n   354\\t\\n   355\\t;; --------------------------------------------------------------- emission\\n   356\\t;; 6a's emit-node with install children numbered by discovery order.\\n   357\\t\\n   358\\t(def ^:private ordered-slots @#'rows/ordered-slots)\\n   359\\t(def ^:private check-literal-markers! @#'rows/check-literal-markers!)\\n   360\\t(def ^:private byte-compare @#'rows/byte-compare)\\n   361\\t\\n   362\\t(declare emit-node*)\\n   363\\t\\n   364\\t(def ^:dynamic *numbering-opts* {})\\n   365\\t\\n   366\\t(defn- refuse-unresolved! [r path]\\n   367\\t  (throw (ex-info \\\"unresolved cursor cells\\\"\\n   368\\t                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\\n   369\\t                   :yin.k/path (into path (:path r)) :yin.k/cursor-cells (:cursor-cells r)})))\\n   370\\t\\n   371\\t(defn- emit-slot* [d x numbering rows]\\n   372\\t  (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\\n   373\\t    (check-literal-markers! (if (v? d) (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x)))\\n   374\\t  (cond\\n   375\\t    (ref? d) (emit-node* x numbering rows)\\n   376\\t    (refs? d) (reduce (fn [[out rs] n] (let [[id rs'] (emit-node* n numbering rs)] [(conj out id) rs'])) [[] rows] x)\\n   377\\t    (= d :C) [(get numbering x x) rows]\\n   378\\t    (v? d) (case (first x)\\n   379\\t             :row (let [[id rs] (emit-node* (second x) numbering rows)] [[:row id] rs])\\n   380\\t             :cursor-cell [[:cursor-cell (get numbering (second x) (second x))] rows]\\n   381\\t             [x rows])\\n   382\\t    :else [x rows]))\\n   383\\t\\n   384\\t(defn- emit-node* [n numbering rows]\\n   385\\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n\\n   386\\t        [slots rows] (reduce (fn [[out rs] [d x]]\\n   387\\t                               (if (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x)))\\n   388\\t                                 (let [r (discovery-numbering x *numbering-opts*)\\n   389\\t                                       _ (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) [:child]))\\n   390\\t                                       [id child-rows] (emit-node* x (:numbering r) {})]\\n   391\\t                                   [(conj out id) (merge rs child-rows)])\\n   392\\t                                 (let [[x' rs'] (emit-slot* d x numbering rs)] [(conj out x') rs'])))\\n   393\\t                             [[] rows] (map vector (descriptors kind slots) slots))\\n   394\\t        slots (if (= kind :task)\\n   395\\t                (-> slots\\n   396\\t                    (update 7 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %)))\\n   397\\t                    (update 12 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %))))\\n   398\\t                (ordered-slots kind slots))\\n   399\\t        body (into [kind] slots) id (jing/segment-key body)]\\n   400\\t    [id (assoc rows id (into [id] body))]))\\n   401\\t\\n   402\\t(defn emit*\\n   403\\t  \\\"Lift side: number by discovery order, emit; refuses :yin.k/non-portable\\n   404\\t   :unresolved-cursor-cells (D5).\\\"\\n   405\\t  [task]\\n   406\\t  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\\n   407\\t    (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) []))\\n   408\\t    (let [[id rows] (emit-node* task (:numbering r) {})]\\n   409\\t      {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))})))\\n   410\\t\\n   411\\t(defn decode*\\n   412\\t  \\\"Receiver side: 6a's decode* with its own numbering check off (it still runs\\n   413\\t   6a's recompute, under a large budget), then the discovery recompute and\\n   414\\t   compare. Only D5 translates to :yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells;\\n   415\\t   a resource refusal propagates as itself. In the real codec this check sits\\n   416\\t   at carrier step 6, before validate-frames; here it runs after decode*\\n   417\\t   returns (a probe limitation, documented in the note).\\\"\\n   418\\t  [wire]\\n   419\\t  (let [task (@#'rows/decode* wire (assoc rows/default-budget :check-numbering false))\\n   420\\t        r (discovery-numbering task)]\\n   421\\t    (when (:refused r)\\n   422\\t      (throw (ex-info \\\"unresolved cursor cells\\\"\\n   423\\t                      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\\n   424\\t                       :yin.k/path (into [(:yin.k/root wire)] (:path (:refused r)))})))\\n   425\\t    (let [[id _] (emit-node* task (:numbering r) {})]\\n   426\\t      (when (not= id (:yin.k/root wire))\\n   427\\t        (throw (ex-info \\\"noncanonical\\\" {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :noncanonical-cursor-cells\\n   428\\t                                        :yin.k/path [(:yin.k/root wire)]}))))\\n   429\\t    task))\\n   430\\t\\n   431\\t(defn error [f]\\n   432\\t  (try (f) nil (catch Throwable e (or (ex-data e) {:throwable (str e)}))))\\n   433\\t\\n   434\\t(defn do-root [task]\\n   435\\t  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\\n   436\\t    (if (:refused r)\\n   437\\t      r\\n   438\\t      (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\\n   439\\t           (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\\n   440\\t             (if (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind (ex-data e))) {:refused (ex-data e)} (throw e)))))))\\n   441\\t\\n   442\\t(defn safe-6a-root [task]\\n   443\\t  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\\n   444\\t       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\\n   445\\t\\n   446\\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\\n   447\\t\\n   448\\t(defn rename-and-shuffle\\n   449\\t  \\\"Rename every cursor cell by `bij` and shuffle every :set, :map and the\\n   450\\t   census in listed order (shuffle is rebound by callers for determinism).\\\"\\n   451\\t  [n renaming]\\n   452\\t  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\\n   453\\t                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\\n   454\\t                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\\n   455\\t                       v))\\n   456\\t          (bij [x] (get renaming x x))\\n   457\\t          (go [n]\\n   458\\t            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\\n   459\\t              (case kind\\n   460\\t                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))\\n   461\\t                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\\n   462\\t                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\\n   463\\t                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\\n   464\\t                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\\n   465\\t                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\\n   466\\t                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\\n   467\\t                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\\n   468\\t                                              :else x))\\n   469\\t                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\\n   470\\t    (go n)))\\n   471\\t\\n   472\\t(defn all-census-cells\\n   473\\t  \\\"Source ids of every census in the tree (root and install children).\\\"\\n   474\\t  [task]\\n   475\\t  (let [acc (atom [])]\\n   476\\t    (letfn [(node [n] (when (map? n)\\n   477\\t                        (when (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (swap! acc into (take-nth 2 (:slots n))))\\n   478\\t                        (doseq [[d x] (map vector (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))]\\n   479\\t                          (cond (ref? d) (node x) (refs? d) (run! node x)\\n   480\\t                                (v? d) (when (and x (= :row (first x))) (node (second x)))))))]\\n   481\\t      (node task) (vec @acc))))\\n   482\\t\\n   483\\t(defn seeded-shuffle [^java.util.Random r]\\n   484\\t  (fn [xs] (let [a (java.util.ArrayList. ^java.util.Collection (vec xs))]\\n   485\\t             (java.util.Collections/shuffle a r)\\n   486\\t             (vec a))))\\n   487\\t\\n   488\\t(defn decoded\\n   489\\t  \\\"Portable decoded equality of an abstract value (check-abstract!'s rule):\\n   490\\t   sets as sets, maps as maps, cursor cells by identity.\\\"\\n   491\\t  [v]\\n   492\\t  (case (first v)\\n   493\\t    :lit [:lit (second v)]\\n   494\\t    :cursor-cell [:identity (second v)]\\n   495\\t    :row (let [n (second v) ss (:slots n)]\\n   496\\t           (case (:kind n)\\n   497\\t             :vec [:seq (mapv decoded ss)]\\n   498\\t             :set [:set (set (map decoded ss))]\\n   499\\t             :map [:map (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [(decoded k) (decoded x)])) (partition 2 ss))]\\n   500\\t             [:row n]))))\\n   501\\t\\n   502\\t(defn distinct-decoded [vs] (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [e] [(decoded e) e])) vs))))\\n   503\\t\\n   504\\t(defn gen-value\\n   505\\t  \\\"Random V over `cells`, depth-bounded. Shapes: literals, cursor cells,\\n   506\\t   vectors, sets, maps (cursor cells as keys too), sets of cursor-cell pairs\\n   507\\t   (edges, aliases such as [X X] included), sets of sets.\\\"\\n   508\\t  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\\n   509\\t  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\\n   510\\t        cc (fn [] [:cursor-cell (pick cells)])\\n   511\\t        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 9 3))]\\n   512\\t    (case k\\n   513\\t      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \\\"s\\\" nil])]\\n   514\\t      1 (cc)\\n   515\\t      2 (cc)\\n   516\\t      3 (let [elems (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\\n   517\\t          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :vec elems)] [:lit (mapv second elems)]))\\n   518\\t      4 (let [elems (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\\n   519\\t          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\\n   520\\t      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p]))\\n   521\\t                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\\n   522\\t          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\\n   523\\t            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\\n   524\\t            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)]))\\n   525\\t      (6 7) (let [edges (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector :row (rows/node :vec [(cc) (cc)]))))]\\n   526\\t              [:row (rows/node :set edges)])\\n   527\\t      8 (let [inner (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 2))\\n   528\\t                                                  #(let [es (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) cc))]\\n   529\\t                                                     [:row (rows/node :set es)])))]\\n   530\\t          [:row (rows/node :set inner)]))))\\n   531\\t\\n   532\\t(defn reached [v]\\n   533\\t  (let [acc (atom #{})]\\n   534\\t    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\\n   535\\t                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\\n   536\\t                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\\n   537\\t                     nil))]\\n   538\\t      (w v) @acc)))\\n   539\\t\\n   540\\t(defn gen-task\\n   541\\t  \\\"A :halted task: 1-6 cursor cells over two bodies (ties common), 1-3 store\\n   542\\t   bindings and the result slot hold random values.\\\"\\n   543\\t  [seed]\\n   544\\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\\n   545\\t        k (inc (.nextInt r 6))\\n   546\\t        cells (mapv #(keyword \\\"src\\\" (str \\\"c\\\" %)) (range k))\\n   547\\t        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body :s (if (zero? (.nextInt r 3)) 1 0))) cells)\\n   548\\t        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\\n   549\\t        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\\n   550\\t        result (gen-value r cells 2)\\n   551\\t        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\\n   552\\t        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\\n   553\\t    (if (empty? used)\\n   554\\t      (recur (inc seed))\\n   555\\t      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\\n   556\\t        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\\n   557\\t\\n   558\\t(defn worked-act []\\n   559\\t  (first (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame)))))\\n   560\\t\\n   561\\t(def