e; **the one row the earlier design would have failed** | yes |\\n850:| 22 | nil-valued live register | `(let1 pair (fn [p q] (cat (cat [] p) q)) (pair nil (next-of c)))`: the `nil` operand is evaluated **before** the read and is live across it (`(cat nil (next-of c))` would give `(conj nil \\\"B\\\")`, the list `(\\\"B\\\")`, so a vector-building `pair` is used; there is no `vector` primitive, `vm.cljc:380-410`, and `pair` builds through the segment's `cat`) | append `\\\"B\\\"` | act, `:next`; `W` has a present key with `[:lit nil]` | `[nil \\\"B\\\"]` | yes |\\n864:the `[:lit nil]` of row 22 dropped → `:window-missing`; a `:reified`\\n916:transformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\\n927:and compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\\n933:identities. Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`,\\n941:1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\\n963:   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\\n1098:   source on the JVM lane, `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`).\\n1115:4. `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member`: row 22's frame\\n1116:   validates with the nil key present; the same frame with the key\\n1131:   are identities on every frame of item 2, nil-valued keys included.\\n1318:  positional values are nil-filled; the law is on canonical frames);\\n1347:`[r₁ V₁ r₂ V₂ …]` sorted by `r` in which a nil-valued register is a\\n1348:present `[:lit nil]`; `:kframe` is `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\\n1597:version-0 reader breaches both\\\"), §7.7.4's `nil`-header version-0 lift\\n1669:   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\\n1670:   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\\n1671:   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\\n1672:   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\\n1675:   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\\n1676:3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\\n1681:5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\\n1684:   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:624:   shuffles is not a proof.\"\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short; git rev-parse HEAD; cat docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md; cat collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.report.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-qm05gqcD' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:39:04.607 xcodebuild[75225:57370926]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:39:05.045 xcodebuild[75225:57370925] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-Hvw7TFjr' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:39:05.862 xcodebuild[75237:57370993]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:39:06.175 xcodebuild[75237:57370992] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1792120000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering.report.md\\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.prompt.md\\n?? collab/example-suspended-state.clj\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/deps.edn\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame_test.cljc\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\\n?? collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-NDYJ1jXT' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:39:07.830 xcodebuild[75246:57371041]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:39:08.113 xcodebuild[75246:57371040] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-cnPvegir' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:39:08.791 xcodebuild[75258:57371098]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:39:09.076 xcodebuild[75258:57371094] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n7a6d7cc9554aab801cafc260b11d93e0a7e09c48\\n# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\\n\\nStatus: **Proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-12 (Architect,\\nclaude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \\\"the problem is not\\nthat the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity is local\\\", and\\nthe orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by **discovery order**\\nin a canonical traversal. Subordinate to\\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\\n(the carrier, body version 3) and\\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\\\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\\ncell\\\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\\n(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a read-only prototype run\\nagainst that code (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\\n\\n**Recommendation in one line:** adopt discovery-order numbering now (§7,\\noption a): a function of structure, renaming-invariant, linear work,\\nprovable by hand; it deletes carrier §6's refinement and search and\\nkeeps every pinned root id except the one state only the search could\\nnumber.\\n\\n## 1. Findings\\n\\n**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\\n(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\\ncursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\\n\\n| Position | Order | Canonical index |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\\n| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\\n| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\\n| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending |\\n| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \\\"sorted\\\") |\\n| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key |\\n| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\\n| `:map` keys and values | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\\n| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\\n\\nSo the environment's key order is canonical (bytes of the symbol as\\nspelled, the order `:bindings` rows are emitted in), and the only\\nunordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest `:map` entries.\\nThe example state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one\\ncursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's\\nenvironment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`\\npending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.\\n\\n**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\\nstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\\nit occurs. A canonical traversal (§2, D1) visits every ordered position\\nin a fixed order, so \\\"the n-th cursor cell met\\\" already encodes the\\nfirst position of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an\\nassigned number and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and\\nsearch (step 5) existed only to turn position multisets into a total\\norder; discovery order is a total order by construction.\\n\\n**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by content, then by numbers\\nalready assigned.** A `:set` or `:map` is entered in the bytewise order\\nof a *content encoding* of its elements in which a numbered cursor cell\\nis spelled by its number and an unnumbered one by its body (stream\\nidentity, descriptor, position). Elements with equal encodings form a\\n*tie group*. A tie group can be split by numbers assigned later through\\nordered positions or through other containers (the review's\\ncounterexample: `'a` bound to `#{X Y}` with equal bodies and `'b` bound\\nto `X`; `'b` numbers `X`, after which `#{X Y}` is no longer tied), so tie\\ngroups are deferred and re-examined to a fixpoint (D3). No label ever\\ngrows: the encoding of an element is linear in the element.\\n\\n**F4. The residual tie class and the orbit rule.** What survives the\\nfixpoint is a tie group whose unnumbered cursor cells have equal bodies\\nand occur only inside tie groups. Two cases:\\n- *Orbit case*: every such cursor cell occurs only inside one element of\\n  the group. Then the elements are interchangeable and the group may be\\n  entered in any order: the emitted rows do not depend on the order\\n  (§3, lemma). `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` and `{X 1, Y 1}` are this case.\\n- *Residue*: a cursor cell of the group occurs in two elements, or\\n  twice across groups. Examples: `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}` (the 6a pinned\\n  loop plus 2-cycle, `rows_test.cljc:312-329`) and the seven-cell\\n  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`). Here the choice of\\n  which element to enter first can change the rows (a triangle vertex\\n  numbered 1 versus a square vertex), so discovery order alone cannot\\n  decide. The honest answer is a refusal (D5): `:yin.k/non-portable`\\n  kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` at lift, naming the container's path\\n  and the cursor cells. Such a state needs two or more cursor cells on\\n  one stream at one position held only through symmetric guest\\n  collections of pairs; 6a refuses the seven-cell state too, by its\\n  label-byte budget (`rows_test.cljc:400-404`).\\n\\n**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: a function of structure\\nalone, renaming-invariant, invariant under every permutation of\\nunordered containers, terminating in at most `n` rounds of linear work.\\n\\n**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** Over 400 random states (one to five\\ncursor cells, bodies drawn from four values so equal bodies are common,\\nnested vectors, sets and maps under keyed bindings and the result slot)\\nthe prototype gave equal root ids after renaming every cursor cell and\\nshuffling every set and map, with no failure; it refused one state, the\\n6a procedure two. The pinned two-cell fixture root\\n`fab223…` and the ten-cell census root are unchanged; the review\\ncounterexample and the alias fixtures receive new, hand-derived ids.\\n\\n**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\\n\\n## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \\\"The procedure\\\")\\n\\nInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\\ncells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\\ntask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\\nrefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\\nrule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\\n\\n**Body label.** `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, the\\ncursor cell's census body as plain data.\\n\\n**Content encoding `CE(x)`** under the numbering assigned so far:\\ncarrier §6 step 2 with one change. An identity position encodes a\\nnumbered cursor cell as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]` and an unnumbered one as\\n`[:cursor-cell <body label>]`; nothing else changes: `[:lit d]` as\\nitself, `[:row B]` expanded in place, structural data as itself, the\\ncensus as `[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]`, and the\\nelements of `:set` rows and the `[key value]` pairs of `:map`, `:store`,\\n`:bindings` and `:parked-table` rows sorted bytewise, duplicates\\nretained. `CE` is hash-free, label-free and linear in its argument.\\n\\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the root `:task` row.\\nA row's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, `:window`, the root's\\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\\nkey, the order §5 emits. The census is not entered; an install child's\\n`:task` row is not entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries\\n(each entry the pair `[key value]`) are sorted bytewise by `CE` under\\nthe numbering at the moment the container is entered; a run of equal\\nencodings is a **tie group** and is deferred; the remaining elements are\\nentered in sorted order, an entry's key before its value.\\n\\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `1 + (count of numbered\\ncursor cells)`. An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\\ncontents table, so content rows keep sharing by Merkle id whether or\\nnot they hold a cursor cell's first occurrence.\\n\\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the deferred tie groups are\\nre-examined in deferral order: each is re-sorted by `CE` under the\\ncurrent numbering; elements now alone in their encoding class are\\nentered (D1, D2 apply inside them); equal runs are deferred again.\\nRepeat while a round numbers at least one cursor cell.\\n\\n**D4. Orbit rule.** For each surviving tie group let `U` be the\\nunnumbered cursor cells occurring in its elements. If every cursor cell\\nof `U` has all its occurrences in the task inside one element of this\\ngroup, enter the elements in the container's listed order. (By §3's\\nlemma the emitted rows are the same for every order.)\\n\\n**D5. Refusal.** Otherwise the lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the container's row path, and\\na `:yin.k/cursor-cells` list of the undecided cursor cells. A receiver\\nthat reaches D5 while recomputing refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is a resource refusal and neither\\nis `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\\n\\n**Validation** is unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs D1-D5,\\nre-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells` on\\nmismatch). The work bound (carrier §10.10) keeps rows, cursor cells,\\ndepth and work; the search and label-byte bounds disappear.\\n\\n## 3. Proof of canonicality\\n\\nLet `S` be an abstract task and `ρ` a renaming of its cursor cells.\\n\\n1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant.* By induction over the\\n   procedure's steps: each step reads only structural data, body labels\\n   and numbers assigned by earlier steps; no step reads a source id. So\\n   the run on `ρ(S)` makes the same decisions as the run on `S` and\\n   assigns `N∘ρ⁻¹`; the emitted rows, which spell cursor cells by number\\n   only, are byte-identical.\\n2. *Invariant under permutation of unordered containers.* A `:set` or\\n   `:map` is read in `CE` order, never in listed order (D1, D3), except\\n   in D4, where the lemma applies. **Lemma (orbit).** Let a surviving tie\\n   group have elements `e₁ … eₘ` with equal `CE`, and let every cursor\\n   cell of `U` occur only inside one `eᵢ`. Equal `CE` means the elements\\n   are identical once `U` is erased to body labels, with equal bodies at\\n   corresponding places. Entering `e_{σ(1)} … e_{σ(m)}` assigns the\\n   `j`-th block of fresh numbers to `e_{σ(j)}` in the same internal\\n   pattern for every `j`; the emitted container is the same multiset of\\n   elements for every `σ`, the census pairs the same numbers with equal\\n   bodies, and no other row mentions a cursor cell of `U`. ∎\\n3. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8\\n   step 3), so D1 is finite; D3 runs at most `n` rounds because each\\n   round numbers a cursor cell or stops; each round is linear in the\\n   number of positions times the cost of `CE`, which is linear in the\\n   element. No label grows.\\n4. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\\n   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\\n5. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\\n   cells (D2 never renumbers), every occurrence of one cursor cell spells\\n   one number, and two cursor cells at one position get two numbers.\\n6. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\\n   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\\n   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by 1, equal rows.\\n\\nWhat is given up: the procedure does not number every state. D5 names\\nthe refused class; a later search over that residue would not change\\nthe numbering of any state D1-D4 number today, so the refusal can be\\nnarrowed without churning ids.\\n\\n## 4. Amendments\\n\\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \\\"The procedure.\\\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\\nwith §2 above (the body label, `CE`, D1-D5). Keep the section's first\\nparagraph (the counterexample) and amend \\\"What is defined, and what is\\nnot claimed\\\" to: \\\"The canonical numbering is defined as the output of\\nthe discovery-order procedure below, which is a function of the state\\nalone (proof in `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\\n§3). It is not the minimum of the root bytes over all bijections. A\\nstate whose cursor cells are distinguishable only by a symmetric guest\\ncollection of pairs is refused (D5), not searched.\\\"\\n\\n**A2 — carrier §6 \\\"Validation\\\".** Replace \\\"A receiver whose work bound\\n(§10.10) is exceeded before the procedure completes refuses with a\\nstructured resource refusal\\\" with \\\"A receiver whose work bound (§10.10)\\nis exceeded, or that reaches D5, refuses with the structured refusal\\nnamed there\\\". Delete \\\"never falls back to encounter order\\\" (the\\nprocedure *is* canonical encounter order; the clause now reads as a\\ncontradiction) and keep \\\"never accepts an unfinished check\\\".\\n\\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \\\"The class-erased skeletons of §6\\nare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\\nof an emitted row\\\" with \\\"The content encodings of §6 are used only\\ninside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted\\nrow\\\".\\n\\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \\\"Recompute §6 under the work bound and\\ncompare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`;\\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached;\\nor the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded).\\\" Add\\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 as\\na `:yin.k/undecodable` kind and a `:yin.k/non-portable` kind.\\n\\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \\\"refinement-and-search procedure\\\" →\\n\\\"discovery-order procedure\\\". Answer 10: \\\"refinement work and search\\nnodes for §6\\\" → \\\"and traversal work for §6\\\". Proposals list: \\\"as\\ndefined by its procedure\\\" → \\\"as defined by discovery order, and the D5\\nrefusal class\\\".\\n\\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \\\"minted by the lift under §6's\\ncanonical numbering\\\" add \\\"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's traversal)\\\".\\n\\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \\\"numbered by the draft's §6\\nprocedure\\\" with \\\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\\\".\\n\\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Replace \\\"(its labels never encode an\\nincoming cursor-cell id)\\\" with \\\"(it reads no incoming cursor-cell id)\\\"\\nand \\\"are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6\\nstep 2)\\\" with \\\"are entered in the bytewise order of their elements'\\ncontent encodings (draft §6 D1)\\\". \\\"Never derived from traversal order\\\"\\nstays true: the traversal is canonical, not the host's.\\n\\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12-19\\nof §8 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\\n\\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \\\"minted by the emitter (stable within\\none lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\\\" remains true.\\n\\n## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\\n\\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\\ncreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\\nmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\\nare: the task's id counter (`engine.cljc:227-230`, `gensym`\\n`:2317-2323`: local, and lower adopts `max(local, carried)`), a creation\\nsequence of `:stream-cursor` executions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s\\n`seq-n`, `engine.cljc:562-573`), a hash of (occurrence, counter), or a\\nrandom id. Every one of them names *history*, not structure:\\n\\n- *Same structure, same hash fails.* A task that minted cursor cells 1,\\n  2, 3 and dropped 2 is structurally equal to one that minted 1, 2; the\\n  carried ids differ, so the root ids differ.\\n- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\\n  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\\n  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\\n  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\\n- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\\n  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\\n  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\\n  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\\n  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\\n  relative to its state.\\n\\nDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\\nidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\\nn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\\nposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\\nids.\\n\\n## 6. Prototype\\n\\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\\nscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1-D5 over\\n6a's abstract task representation (the orbit rule in D4's general\\nform), emits through 6a's `rows/emit-numbered`, and compares with 6a's\\n`rows/emit`. Run from the probe directory:\\n\\n```\\nclojure -M -e \\\"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\\\"\\n```\\n\\nResults of 2026-10-12 (6a tests pass in the same project, 21 tests,\\n324 assertions):\\n\\n| State | Discovery order | 6a |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same root |\\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | root `2d9873a8…` |\\n| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | orbit rule, root `07eda35d…` | same root |\\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | orbit rule, roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after element reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | not compared |\\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | `71543fe2…` / same |\\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells` | root `55ff168e…` |\\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells` | refused `:resource-limit [:label-bytes]` |\\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same root |\\n| 400 random states, renamed and shuffled | 399 numbered, 1 refused, 0 invariance failures | 398 numbered, 2 refused, 0 failures; 188 equal roots, 210 different |\\n\\nHand derivation, verified against the codec above. Fixture\\n`:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by `CE` of its\\n`[key value]` pairs; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\\n(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\\n`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) sorts before\\n`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\\ncounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties and is deferred; `'b`\\nmeets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-sorts the set, `[:n 1]` against a body\\nlabel, and `Y = cc-2`. Alias `[x x y]`: `x = cc-1` at index 0, `y = cc-2`\\nat index 2. The example state of `collab/example-suspended-state.clj`\\nhas one cursor cell, first met in the act's environment under `c`, so\\n`cc-1`; a one-cell state has the same rows under both procedures.\\n\\n## 7. Recommendation\\n\\n**(a) Adopt discovery order now.** 6b has not started and 6a is\\nunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\\ntests plus the amendments of §4: delete `check-label-bytes!`\\n(`rows.cljc:212-230`), `positions` (`:285-325`), `classes` (`:326-330`),\\n`refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`) and the label seeding\\n(`:252-255`); keep `ce` (`:257-284`) with the numbered/unnumbered\\nspelling; add the traversal, fixpoint, orbit rule and refusal (the\\nprototype is 110 lines with helpers; 60-80 in place); delete the\\n`:search` and `:label-bytes` budget keys (`:200-202`). Unchanged:\\n`emit`, `emit-numbered`, `canonical-under`, the census and its numeric\\norder (`:140-156`), `decode*`'s recompute-and-compare (`:570-575`),\\nnumbering before `validate-frames` (`:576-578`; test\\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`), the `:bytes`,\\n`:rows`, `:depth` and `:work` bounds. Tests: `rows_test.cljc:171`\\nbecomes the orbit-rule test (no `{:search 1}`); `:307-329` keeps\\n`fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`, drops `55ff168e…`, `{:search 2}` and `cc-3`;\\n`:400-404` becomes the D5 refusal test; `:406-421` is deleted. Cost:\\none implementation round and one review round, and the review can check\\n§3 by hand. Risk: a canonicality bug, mitigated by §8 item 18's\\ngenerator in CI and by the proof; and the D5 class, documented and\\nnarrowable later without id churn.\\n\\n**(b) Keep the 6a design.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine\\nharness and every reviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search,\\nlabels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte, and a\\ndefinition that is \\\"whatever the procedure outputs\\\". Not recommended.\\n\\n**(c) Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the\\nharness (§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\\n\\n## 8. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\\n\\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\\n    the review counterexample, `[x x y]` and `[x y y]`,\\n    `canonical-numbering` returns exactly the maps of §6.\\n13. `rows-test/review-counterexample-resolves-by-fixpoint`: `'a #{X Y}`,\\n    `'b X` numbers `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; the task renamed and with the set\\n    reversed gives the same root; the root is pinned `a3713a5e…`.\\n14. `rows-test/orbit-rule`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` and `{X 1, Y 1}`\\n    number without refusal; element reversal and renaming give equal\\n    roots; `#{X Y}` keeps root `07eda35d…`; `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` is pinned\\n    `da69c679…` and `{X 1, Y 1}` is pinned `49d4478c…`.\\n15. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the loop plus 2-cycle and\\n    the seven-cell state refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`,\\n    kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, a path naming the set and the\\n    undecided cursor cells; a carrier of either built with\\n    `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\\n16. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\\n    unchanged; `a3713a5e…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…` added;\\n    `55ff168e…` removed.\\n17. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\\n    and `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged.\\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (one to six\\n    cursor cells, bodies from a four-value pool, nested `:vec`, `:set`,\\n    `:map` under keyed bindings and the result, unique set elements and\\n    map keys under decoded equality) over at least 400 states: every\\n    numbered state's carrier decodes, and renaming every cursor cell\\n    plus shuffling every set and map gives an equal root id.\\n19. Budgets: the `:search` and `:label-bytes` keys are gone;\\n    `{:work 1}` still refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`;\\n    `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` is deleted.\\n20. Docs: A1-A9 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three\\n    hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\\n\\n## 9. Owner questions\\n\\n1. Is the D5 refusal class acceptable as a documented limitation of\\n   body version 3 (recommended: yes; it can be narrowed later by a\\n   search over the residue without changing any id numbered today)?\\n2. Should the refusal kind be `:unresolved-cursor-cells` as proposed,\\n   or the orchestrator's `:resource-limit` (recommended against: it is\\n   not a budget, and carrier §10.10 says a resource refusal never\\n   stands in for a canonicality verdict)?\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 19:30:35 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 02:30:35 Asia/Bangkok\\n\\n(Host clock as read by `date -u` at completion; the brief's Created-GMT\\nof 2026-10-12 10:00 is later than this host's clock.)\\n\\n# Architect report: discovery-order numbering for cursor cells\\n\\nDeliverable: `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\\n(about 420 lines: findings F1-F7, the rule D1-D5, a proof, amendments\\nA1-A10, acceptance items 12-20, owner questions). Nothing staged or\\ncommitted. The 6a worktree was not edited; its code was read through the\\nshared git object store (`git show srvm-phase6a:…`, commit `d77da9f0`),\\nbecause the sandbox blocks the p6a path.\\n\\n## Answers in brief\\n\\n1. **Positions.** Every non-guest position is ordered: fixed slots\\n   (`:act`/`:tail`/`:kframe`/`:wait`/`:env`/`:closure`/`:reified`/\\n   `:pending` C and V slots, root slots 5/6/8/15), ordered vectors\\n   (`frames`, `:k`, `:vec`, `:list`) and keyed rows with structural keys\\n   (`:bindings` by symbol bytes, `:store` by key bytes, `:window` by\\n   register, `:parked-table` by P, installs by name, module stores by\\n   M). The only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest\\n   `:map` entries (keys are V). The environment's key order is canonical\\n   (Jing bytes of the symbol, the emitted order). Table in doc F1.\\n2. **Rule.** Depth-first pre-order traversal (D1), the n-th unnumbered\\n   cursor cell met gets `cc-n` (D2); no first-occurrence spelling, every\\n   occurrence stays `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census stays\\n   the contents table; install children are numbered as their own\\n   tasks (unchanged); the positions step is subsumed because discovery\\n   order already encodes each cell's first position.\\n3. **Unordered containers.** Entered in bytewise order of a content\\n   encoding `CE` that spells numbered cells by number and unnumbered by\\n   body (stream identity, descriptor, position). Equal runs are tie\\n   groups, deferred, re-examined to a fixpoint as other occurrences\\n   assign numbers (resolves the review's `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` case). The\\n   residue is a tie group whose unnumbered cells occur only in tie\\n   groups. Orbit rule (D4): if each such cell's occurrences all lie in\\n   one element, enter in any order (lemma: rows are order-independent).\\n   Otherwise refuse (D5). Recommended kind: `:yin.k/non-portable`\\n   `:unresolved-cursor-cells` at lift, `:yin.k/undecodable`\\n   `:unresolved-cursor-cells` at decode, **not** `:resource-limit`:\\n   it is not a budget and §10.10 forbids a resource refusal standing in\\n   for a canonicality verdict. The refused class (the loop plus 2-cycle\\n   and the seven-cell fixtures) is acceptable as a documented limitation\\n   and can be narrowed later without changing any id numbered today.\\n4. **Canonicality.** Proved in doc §3: structure-only and\\n   renaming-invariant by induction (no step reads a source id);\\n   permutation-invariant because unordered containers are read in `CE`\\n   order, plus the orbit lemma; terminating in ≤ n fixpoint rounds of\\n   linear work; no label growth. Prototype: 400 random states, renamed\\n   and shuffled, 0 invariance failures (6a also 0); 1 refusal vs 6a's 2.\\n5. **Impact.** Delete in `rows.cljc` (6a): `check-label-bytes!`\\n   (212-230), `positions` (285-325), `classes` (326-330), `refine`\\n   (331-337), `search` (338-354), label seeding (252-255), budget keys\\n   `:search`/`:label-bytes` (200-202). Keep `ce` (257-284) with the\\n   numbered/unnumbered spelling. Add traversal, fixpoint, orbit rule,\\n   refusal (60-80 lines). Unchanged: emit/emit-numbered/canonical-under,\\n   census order (140-156), decode's recompute-and-compare (570-575),\\n   numbering before frames (576-578), `:bytes`/`:rows`/`:depth`/`:work`.\\n   Pinned ids: `fab223…` (two-cell fixture) and `c4a7c7…` (frame)\\n   **unchanged**; the ten-cell census root unchanged; `55ff168e…` (loop\\n   plus 2-cycle) becomes a refusal; new pins `a3713a5e…` (review\\n   counterexample), `4993b5b1…` (`[x x y]`), `536c810b…` (`[x y y]`,\\n   same as 6a), `07eda35d…` (`#{X Y}`, same as 6a), `da69c679…`,\\n   `49d4478c…` (orbit fixtures). The seven-cell fixture goes from a\\n   `:label-bytes` refusal to a D5 refusal. Effort: one slice (code +\\n   tests + amendments A1-A9), one implementation round, one review.\\n6. **Portable ids in the VM.** Every candidate (id counter, creation\\n   sequence, hash of occurrence and counter, random) names history, not\\n   structure: structurally equal states carry different ids, so \\\"same\\n   structure, same hash\\\" fails; the cross-engine law would need engines\\n   to agree on minting history; lower must keep a second name beside\\n   its fresh resource entry and relift needs a collision-free global\\n   scheme. Recommend against. Discovery order gives the local identity a\\n   structural name instead (the n-th cell met, the de Bruijn move).\\n7. **Recommendation: (a)**, adopt now while 6a is unmerged and 6b\\n   unstarted. (b) costs nothing now but burdens 6b, the harness and\\n   every reviewer with an exhaustive search and growing labels; (c)\\n   needs VM, census, law and harness changes across three or more\\n   rounds.\\n\\n## Verified in code (file:line)\\n\\n- `rows.cljc:37-57` row table (position inventory); `:148-156`\\n  `ordered-slots` (keyed structural order); `:140-145` census numeric\\n  order; `:185-189` child numbered in its own scope; `:236-355`\\n  `canonical-numbering` (labels, `ce`, `positions`, `refine`, `search`);\\n  `:200-202` budget keys; `:212-230` label-byte bound; `:570-578`\\n  decode recompute-and-compare, then `validate-frames`.\\n- `rows_test.cljc:28` fixture; `:159` alias fixtures; `:171` symmetric\\n  pair with `{:search 1}`; `:206` numbering before frame defect;\\n  `:307-329` pinned roots incl. loop plus 2-cycle; `:331` ten cells;\\n  `:392-404` seven-cell fixture refused by `[:label-bytes]`; `:406-421`\\n  label-bytes test.\\n- `engine.cljc:227-230` `gen-id`, `:305-313` `cursor-cell?`, `:316-328`\\n  `issue-ref`, `:538-573` cursor minting with `seq-n`, `:2317-2323`\\n  `gensym` (for Q6).\\n- Example state run (`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` through the\\n  probe project): one cursor cell `:cursor-1` in env `c`, window\\n  register 2 and the `:next` pending; all ordered positions.\\n- 6a tests reproduced in the probe project: 21 tests, 324 assertions,\\n  0 failures. Prototype `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`\\n  (run: `clojure -M -e \\\"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\\\"`\\n  from `collab/numbering-probe`): outputs as tabulated in doc §6; hand\\n  derivations for the two-cell fixture, review counterexample and\\n  `[x x y]` match the codec.\\n\\n## Unverified\\n\\n- Uncommitted changes in the p6a worktree, if any, are invisible to me\\n  (read via the branch tip only).\\n- The prototype numbers install children through 6a's `emit-numbered`,\\n  which calls 6a's `canonical-numbering` for the child; the random\\n  generator uses no children. Child scope is unchanged by design.\\n- The random generator does not run `rows/decode` on its states (decode\\n  recomputes 6a's numbering and would mismatch by construction);\\n  acceptance item 18 requires decode in the real test.\\n- CBOR symbol order (length-first) was not inspected; the two-cell\\n  derivation holds under every candidate entry order (doc §6).\\n- Three-host runs, kondo: not run (documents only).\\n\\n## Owner questions\\n\\n1. Accept the D5 refusal class as a documented limitation of body\\n   version 3 (recommended yes)?\\n2. Refusal kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` (recommended) versus the\\n   orchestrator's `:resource-limit`?\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md | sed -n '65,215p'; cat collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    65\\tcounterexample: `'a` bound to `#{X Y}` with equal bodies and `'b` bound\\n    66\\tto `X`; `'b` numbers `X`, after which `#{X Y}` is no longer tied), so tie\\n    67\\tgroups are deferred and re-examined to a fixpoint (D3). No label ever\\n    68\\tgrows: the encoding of an element is linear in the element.\\n    69\\t\\n    70\\t**F4. The residual tie class and the orbit rule.** What survives the\\n    71\\tfixpoint is a tie group whose unnumbered cursor cells have equal bodies\\n    72\\tand occur only inside tie groups. Two cases:\\n    73\\t- *Orbit case*: every such cursor cell occurs only inside one element of\\n    74\\t  the group. Then the elements are interchangeable and the group may be\\n    75\\t  entered in any order: the emitted rows do not depend on the order\\n    76\\t  (§3, lemma). `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` and `{X 1, Y 1}` are this case.\\n    77\\t- *Residue*: a cursor cell of the group occurs in two elements, or\\n    78\\t  twice across groups. Examples: `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}` (the 6a pinned\\n    79\\t  loop plus 2-cycle, `rows_test.cljc:312-329`) and the seven-cell\\n    80\\t  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`). Here the choice of\\n    81\\t  which element to enter first can change the rows (a triangle vertex\\n    82\\t  numbered 1 versus a square vertex), so discovery order alone cannot\\n    83\\t  decide. The honest answer is a refusal (D5): `:yin.k/non-portable`\\n    84\\t  kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` at lift, naming the container's path\\n    85\\t  and the cursor cells. Such a state needs two or more cursor cells on\\n    86\\t  one stream at one position held only through symmetric guest\\n    87\\t  collections of pairs; 6a refuses the seven-cell state too, by its\\n    88\\t  label-byte budget (`rows_test.cljc:400-404`).\\n    89\\t\\n    90\\t**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: a function of structure\\n    91\\talone, renaming-invariant, invariant under every permutation of\\n    92\\tunordered containers, terminating in at most `n` rounds of linear work.\\n    93\\t\\n    94\\t**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** Over 400 random states (one to five\\n    95\\tcursor cells, bodies drawn from four values so equal bodies are common,\\n    96\\tnested vectors, sets and maps under keyed bindings and the result slot)\\n    97\\tthe prototype gave equal root ids after renaming every cursor cell and\\n    98\\tshuffling every set and map, with no failure; it refused one state, the\\n    99\\t6a procedure two. The pinned two-cell fixture root\\n   100\\t`fab223…` and the ten-cell census root are unchanged; the review\\n   101\\tcounterexample and the alias fixtures receive new, hand-derived ids.\\n   102\\t\\n   103\\t**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\\n   104\\t\\n   105\\t## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \\\"The procedure\\\")\\n   106\\t\\n   107\\tInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\\n   108\\tcells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\\n   109\\ttask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\\n   110\\trefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\\n   111\\trule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\\n   112\\t\\n   113\\t**Body label.** `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, the\\n   114\\tcursor cell's census body as plain data.\\n   115\\t\\n   116\\t**Content encoding `CE(x)`** under the numbering assigned so far:\\n   117\\tcarrier §6 step 2 with one change. An identity position encodes a\\n   118\\tnumbered cursor cell as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]` and an unnumbered one as\\n   119\\t`[:cursor-cell <body label>]`; nothing else changes: `[:lit d]` as\\n   120\\titself, `[:row B]` expanded in place, structural data as itself, the\\n   121\\tcensus as `[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]`, and the\\n   122\\telements of `:set` rows and the `[key value]` pairs of `:map`, `:store`,\\n   123\\t`:bindings` and `:parked-table` rows sorted bytewise, duplicates\\n   124\\tretained. `CE` is hash-free, label-free and linear in its argument.\\n   125\\t\\n   126\\t**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the root `:task` row.\\n   127\\tA row's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\\n   128\\t(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\\n   129\\tkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, `:window`, the root's\\n   130\\t`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\\n   131\\tkey, the order §5 emits. The census is not entered; an install child's\\n   132\\t`:task` row is not entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries\\n   133\\t(each entry the pair `[key value]`) are sorted bytewise by `CE` under\\n   134\\tthe numbering at the moment the container is entered; a run of equal\\n   135\\tencodings is a **tie group** and is deferred; the remaining elements are\\n   136\\tentered in sorted order, an entry's key before its value.\\n   137\\t\\n   138\\t**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\\n   139\\t`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\\n   140\\tcell has no number, the cursor cell receives `1 + (count of numbered\\n   141\\tcursor cells)`. An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\\n   142\\tno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\\n   143\\t`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\\n   144\\tcontents table, so content rows keep sharing by Merkle id whether or\\n   145\\tnot they hold a cursor cell's first occurrence.\\n   146\\t\\n   147\\t**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the deferred tie groups are\\n   148\\tre-examined in deferral order: each is re-sorted by `CE` under the\\n   149\\tcurrent numbering; elements now alone in their encoding class are\\n   150\\tentered (D1, D2 apply inside them); equal runs are deferred again.\\n   151\\tRepeat while a round numbers at least one cursor cell.\\n   152\\t\\n   153\\t**D4. Orbit rule.** For each surviving tie group let `U` be the\\n   154\\tunnumbered cursor cells occurring in its elements. If every cursor cell\\n   155\\tof `U` has all its occurrences in the task inside one element of this\\n   156\\tgroup, enter the elements in the container's listed order. (By §3's\\n   157\\tlemma the emitted rows are the same for every order.)\\n   158\\t\\n   159\\t**D5. Refusal.** Otherwise the lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\\n   160\\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the container's row path, and\\n   161\\ta `:yin.k/cursor-cells` list of the undecided cursor cells. A receiver\\n   162\\tthat reaches D5 while recomputing refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\\n   163\\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is a resource refusal and neither\\n   164\\tis `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\\n   165\\t\\n   166\\t**Validation** is unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs D1-D5,\\n   167\\tre-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells` on\\n   168\\tmismatch). The work bound (carrier §10.10) keeps rows, cursor cells,\\n   169\\tdepth and work; the search and label-byte bounds disappear.\\n   170\\t\\n   171\\t## 3. Proof of canonicality\\n   172\\t\\n   173\\tLet `S` be an abstract task and `ρ` a renaming of its cursor cells.\\n   174\\t\\n   175\\t1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant.* By induction over the\\n   176\\t   procedure's steps: each step reads only structural data, body labels\\n   177\\t   and numbers assigned by earlier steps; no step reads a source id. So\\n   178\\t   the run on `ρ(S)` makes the same decisions as the run on `S` and\\n   179\\t   assigns `N∘ρ⁻¹`; the emitted rows, which spell cursor cells by number\\n   180\\t   only, are byte-identical.\\n   181\\t2. *Invariant under permutation of unordered containers.* A `:set` or\\n   182\\t   `:map` is read in `CE` order, never in listed order (D1, D3), except\\n   183\\t   in D4, where the lemma applies. **Lemma (orbit).** Let a surviving tie\\n   184\\t   group have elements `e₁ … eₘ` with equal `CE`, and let every cursor\\n   185\\t   cell of `U` occur only inside one `eᵢ`. Equal `CE` means the elements\\n   186\\t   are identical once `U` is erased to body labels, with equal bodies at\\n   187\\t   corresponding places. Entering `e_{σ(1)} … e_{σ(m)}` assigns the\\n   188\\t   `j`-th block of fresh numbers to `e_{σ(j)}` in the same internal\\n   189\\t   pattern for every `j`; the emitted container is the same multiset of\\n   190\\t   elements for every `σ`, the census pairs the same numbers with equal\\n   191\\t   bodies, and no other row mentions a cursor cell of `U`. ∎\\n   192\\t3. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8\\n   193\\t   step 3), so D1 is finite; D3 runs at most `n` rounds because each\\n   194\\t   round numbers a cursor cell or stops; each round is linear in the\\n   195\\t   number of positions times the cost of `CE`, which is linear in the\\n   196\\t   element. No label grows.\\n   197\\t4. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\\n   198\\t   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\\n   199\\t5. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\\n   200\\t   cells (D2 never renumbers), every occurrence of one cursor cell spells\\n   201\\t   one number, and two cursor cells at one position get two numbers.\\n   202\\t6. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\\n   203\\t   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\\n   204\\t   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by 1, equal rows.\\n   205\\t\\n   206\\tWhat is given up: the procedure does not number every state. D5 names\\n   207\\tthe refused class; a later search over that residue would not change\\n   208\\tthe numbering of any state D1-D4 number today, so the refusal can be\\n   209\\tnarrowed without churning ids.\\n   210\\t\\n   211\\t## 4. Amendments\\n   212\\t\\n   213\\t**A1 — carrier §6, from \\\"The procedure.\\\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\\n   214\\twith §2 above (the body label, `CE`, D1-D5). Keep the section's first\\n   215\\tparagraph (the counterexample) and amend \\\"What is defined, and what is\\n(ns probe.discovery\\n  \\\"Read-only prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering over the\\n   6a abstract task representation (yin.vm.ucf.rows). Not repo code.\\\"\\n  (:require [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]))\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- helpers\\n\\n(defn descriptors [kind slots]\\n  (let [schema (get rows/row-table kind)]\\n    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\\n          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\\n          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\\n          :else schema)))\\n\\n(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\\n(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\\n(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\\n\\n(defn bytes-key [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\\n\\n(defn compare-bytes [a b]\\n  (loop [i 0]\\n    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\\n          (= i (count b)) 1\\n          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\\n          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\\n\\n(defn by-bytes [xs] (vec (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key %1) (bytes-key %2)) xs)))\\n\\n(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table :window})\\n\\n;; -------------------------------------------------------- discovery order\\n\\n(defn discovery-numbering\\n  \\\"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n}} or {:refused {...}}.\\n   D1 traversal, D2 first meeting, D3 deferred fixpoint, D4 orbit rule,\\n   D5 refusal.\\\"\\n  [task]\\n  (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\\n        bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\\n        label (fn [c] (let [[stream position] (:slots (get bodies c))]\\n                        [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\\n        numbering (atom {})\\n        deferred (atom [])                     ; vector of {:kind k :elems [...]}\\n        occurrences (atom {})                  ; src -> count, census excluded\\n        cell-ce (fn [c] (if-let [n (get @numbering c)] [:cursor-cell [:n n]] [:cursor-cell (label c)]))]\\n    (letfn [(ce-slot [d x]\\n              (cond (ref? d) (ce x)\\n                    (refs? d) (mapv ce x)\\n                    (= :C d) (cell-ce x)\\n                    (v? d) (case (first x)\\n                             :cursor-cell (cell-ce (second x))\\n                             :row [:row (ce (second x))]\\n                             x)\\n                    :else x))\\n            (ce [n]\\n              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\\n                (cond (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\\n                      (and (= :task kind) (not (identical? n task))) [:child]\\n                      :else (let [ss (mapv ce-slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)\\n                                  ss (cond (contains? #{:map :store :bindings :parked-table} kind)\\n                                           (by-bytes (mapv vec (partition 2 ss)))\\n                                           (= :set kind) (by-bytes ss)\\n                                           :else ss)]\\n                              (into [kind] ss)))))\\n            (count-occ-slot [d x]\\n              (cond (ref? d) (count-occ x)\\n                    (refs? d) (run! count-occ x)\\n                    (= :C d) (swap! occurrences update x (fnil inc 0))\\n                    (v? d) (case (first x)\\n                             :cursor-cell (swap! occurrences update (second x) (fnil inc 0))\\n                             :row (count-occ (second x))\\n                             nil)\\n                    :else nil))\\n            (count-occ [n]\\n              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\\n                (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells kind) (and (= :task kind) (not (identical? n task))))\\n                  (dorun (map count-occ-slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))\\n            (meet [c]\\n              (when-not (contains? @numbering c)\\n                (swap! numbering assoc c (keyword \\\"yin.k\\\" (str \\\"cc-\\\" (inc (count @numbering)))))))\\n            (visit-slot [d x]\\n              (cond (ref? d) (visit x)\\n                    (refs? d) (run! visit x)\\n                    (= :C d) (meet x)\\n                    (v? d) (case (first x)\\n                             :cursor-cell (meet (second x))\\n                             :row (visit (second x))\\n                             nil)\\n                    :else nil))\\n            (visit-unordered [kind elems]\\n              ;; elems: for :set the V elements; for :map the [k v] pairs\\n              (let [keyed (mapv (fn [e] [(bytes-key (if (= :map kind) [(ce-slot :V (first e)) (ce-slot :V (second e))] (ce-slot :V e))) e]) elems)\\n                    sorted (sort #(compare-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) keyed)\\n                    groups (partition-by first sorted)]\\n                (doseq [g groups]\\n                  (if (= 1 (count g))\\n                    (visit-elem kind (second (first g)))\\n                    (swap! deferred conj {:kind kind :elems (mapv second g)})))))\\n            (visit-elem [kind e]\\n              (if (= :map kind)\\n                (do (visit-slot :V (first e)) (visit-slot :V (second e)))\\n                (visit-slot :V e)))\\n            (visit [n]\\n              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ds (descriptors kind slots)]\\n                (cond\\n                  (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\\n                  (and (= :task kind) (not (identical? n task))) nil   ; child: own scope\\n                  (contains? keyed-structural kind)\\n                  (doseq [[_ v] (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (first %1)) (bytes-key (first %2))) (partition 2 slots))]\\n                    (visit-slot :V v))\\n                  (= :map kind) (visit-unordered :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\\n                  (= :set kind) (visit-unordered :set (vec slots))\\n                  (= :task kind)\\n                  (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\\n                    (cond (= i 7) (doseq [ms (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (first (:slots %1))) (bytes-key (first (:slots %2)))) x)] (visit ms))\\n                          (= i 12) (doseq [ins (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (first (:slots %1))) (bytes-key (first (:slots %2)))) x)] (visit ins))\\n                          :else (visit-slot d x)))\\n                  :else (dorun (map visit-slot ds slots)))))\\n            (fixpoint []\\n              (loop []\\n                (let [groups @deferred\\n                      before (count @numbering)]\\n                  (reset! deferred [])\\n                  (doseq [{:keys [kind elems]} groups] (visit-unordered kind elems))\\n                  (when (and (seq @deferred) (> (count @numbering) before)) (recur)))))\\n            (unnumbered-cells [kind e]\\n              (let [acc (atom [])]\\n                (letfn [(walk-slot [d x]\\n                          (cond (ref? d) (walk x)\\n                                (= :C d) (when-not (get @numbering x) (swap! acc conj x))\\n                                (v? d) (case (first x)\\n                                         :cursor-cell (when-not (get @numbering (second x)) (swap! acc conj (second x)))\\n                                         :row (walk (second x))\\n                                         nil)\\n                                :else nil))\\n                        (walk [n] (dorun (map walk-slot (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))))]\\n                  (if (= :map kind) (do (walk-slot :V (first e)) (walk-slot :V (second e))) (walk-slot :V e))\\n                  @acc)))]\\n      (count-occ task)\\n      (visit task)\\n      (fixpoint)\\n      ;; D4 orbit rule / D5 refusal on the residue\\n      (let [residue @deferred\\n            ;; General orbit rule: every unnumbered cell of the group has ALL its\\n            ;; occurrences in the task inside one element of this group.\\n            bad (some (fn [{:keys [kind elems]}]\\n                        (let [per-elem (mapv #(frequencies (unnumbered-cells kind %)) elems)\\n                              cells (distinct (mapcat keys per-elem))]\\n                          (when (some (fn [c]\\n                                        (let [in (filter #(contains? % c) per-elem)]\\n                                          (or (not= 1 (count in))\\n                                              (not= (get (first in) c) (get @occurrences c)))))\\n                                      cells)\\n                            {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells :container kind :cells (vec cells)})))\\n                      residue)]\\n        (if bad\\n          {:refused bad}\\n          (do (doseq [{:keys [kind elems]} residue] (doseq [e elems] (visit-elem kind e)))\\n              {:numbering @numbering}))))))\\n\\n(defn do-root [task]\\n  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\\n    (if (:refused r) r (:yin.k/root (rows/emit-numbered task (:numbering r))))))\\n\\n(defn safe-6a-root [task]\\n  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\\n       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\\n\\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\\n\\n(defn rename-and-shuffle\\n  \\\"Rename every cursor cell by `bij` and shuffle every :set and :map in source order.\\\"\\n  [n bij rnd]\\n  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\\n                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\\n                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\\n                       v))\\n          (go [n]\\n            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\\n              (case kind\\n                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))   ; shuffle uses global rnd; seeded below\\n                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\\n                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\\n                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\\n                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\\n                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\\n                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\\n                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\\n                                              :else x))\\n                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\\n    (go n)))\\n\\n(defn gen-value\\n  \\\"Random V over cells; depth-bounded. Returns V.\\\"\\n  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\\n  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\\n        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 6 3))]\\n    (case k\\n      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \\\"s\\\" nil])]\\n      1 [:cursor-cell (pick cells)]\\n      2 [:cursor-cell (pick cells)]\\n      3 [:row (rows/node :vec (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))]\\n      4 (let [elems (vec (distinct (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))]\\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\\n      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into {} (map (fn [p] [(pr-str (first p)) p]))\\n                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\\n          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\\n            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\\n            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)])))))\\n\\n(defn reached [v]\\n  (let [acc (atom #{})]\\n    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\\n                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\\n                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\\n                     nil))]\\n      (w v) @acc)))\\n\\n(defn gen-task\\n  [seed]\\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 5))\\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \\\"src\\\" (str \\\"c\\\" %)) (range k))\\n        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body (nth [:s :t] (.nextInt r 2)) (.nextInt r 2))) cells)\\n        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\\n        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\\n        result (gen-value r cells 2)\\n        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\\n        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\\n    (if (empty? used)\\n      (recur (inc seed))\\n      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\\n        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\\n\\n(defn fixture-review-counterexample\\n  \\\"'a bound to #{X Y} with equal bodies, 'b bound to X.\\\"\\n  []\\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\\n        t (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:lit nil])]\\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store ['a [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]\\n                                                             'b [:cursor-cell :x]])))))\\n\\n(defn report-fixture [name task]\\n  (let [d (discovery-numbering task)]\\n    (println name)\\n    (println \\\"  DO :\\\" (or (:refused d) (into (sorted-map) (:numbering d))))\\n    (println \\\"  DO root:\\\" (do-root task))\\n    (println \\\"  6a root:\\\" (safe-6a-root task))))\\n\\n(defn -main []\\n  (println \\\"== fixtures\\\")\\n  (report-fixture \\\"fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y (pinned fab223...)\\\" (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y))\\n  (report-fixture \\\"review counterexample 'a #{X Y}, 'b X\\\" (fixture-review-counterexample))\\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]\\n    (report-fixture \\\"symmetric pair #{X Y}\\\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]))\\n    (report-fixture \\\"alias a: [x x y]\\\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]))\\n    (report-fixture \\\"alias b: [x y y]\\\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y] [:cursor-cell :y]])]))\\n    (let [shape (fn [[l a b]] [[:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell l] [:cursor-cell l]])]\\n                               [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell a] [:cursor-cell b]])]\\n                               [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell b] [:cursor-cell a]])]])\\n          graph (fn [ids edges] (rt/task-node (mapv #(vector % body) ids) [:row (rows/node :set edges)]))]\\n      (report-fixture \\\"loop + 2-cycle (pinned 55ff16...)\\\" (graph [:l :a :b] (shape [:l :a :b])))))\\n  (report-fixture \\\"seven-cell triangle+square\\\" (rt/seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square))\\n  (println \\\"\\\\n== orbit rule, element reversal + renaming\\\")\\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\\n        orbit [[\\\"#{[X X] [Y Y]}\\\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]]\\n                                               [:row (rows/node :set [[:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :x]])]\\n                                                                      [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :y] [:cursor-cell :y]])]])])]\\n               [\\\"{X 1, Y 1}\\\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]]\\n                                           [:row (rows/node :map [[:cursor-cell :x] [:lit 1] [:cursor-cell :y] [:lit 1]])])]\\n               [\\\"#{X Y}\\\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])])]\\n               [\\\"review counterexample\\\" (fixture-review-counterexample)]]]\\n    (doseq [[name task] orbit]\\n      (let [rev (with-redefs [shuffle (comp vec reverse)] (rename-and-shuffle task identity nil)) ren (rename-and-shuffle task {:x :q/y :y :q/x} nil)]\\n        (println name \\\"DO:\\\" (do-root task) \\\"| reversed equal:\\\" (= (do-root task) (do-root rev))\\n                 \\\"| renamed+shuffled equal:\\\" (= (do-root task) (do-root ren))))))\\n  (let [ids (mapv #(keyword \\\"src\\\" (str \\\"x\\\" %)) (range 10))]\\n    (report-fixture \\\"ten cells in a vec\\\" (rt/task-node (mapv (fn [id i] [id (rt/cursor-cell-body :s i)]) ids (range))\\n                                                       [:row (rows/node :vec (mapv #(vector :cursor-cell %) ids))])))\\n  (println \\\"\\\\n== counterexample search\\\")\\n  (let [n 400\\n        stats (atom {:do-ok 0 :do-refused 0 :sixa-refused 0 :same-root 0 :diff-root 0 :do-invariance-fail 0 :sixa-invariance-fail 0})]\\n    (doseq [seed (range n)]\\n      (let [task (gen-task seed)\\n            cells (vec (take-nth 2 (:slots (nth (:slots task) 9))))\\n            fresh (zipmap cells (shuffle (mapv #(keyword \\\"other\\\" (str \\\"z\\\" %)) (range (count cells)))))\\n            task2 (rename-and-shuffle task fresh nil)\\n            d1 (do-root task) d2 (do-root task2)]\\n        (if (map? d1)\\n          (swap! stats update :do-refused inc)\\n          (do (swap! stats update :do-ok inc)\\n              (when (not= d1 d2) (swap! stats update :do-invariance-fail inc)\\n                    (println \\\"DO INVARIANCE FAIL seed\\\" seed))))\\n        (let [s1 (safe-6a-root task) s2 (safe-6a-root task2)]\\n          (cond (map? s1) (swap! stats update :sixa-refused inc)\\n                (not= s1 s2) (do (swap! stats update :sixa-invariance-fail inc) (println \\\"6a INVARIANCE FAIL seed\\\" seed))\\n                :else (if (= s1 d1) (swap! stats update :same-root inc) (swap! stats update :diff-root inc))))))\\n    (println n \\\"random states:\\\" @stats)))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:21:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I found a gap in the orbit lemma: equal content encodings erase aliasing within an element, so `[X X]` can tie with `[Y Z]` even though they aren’t interchangeable. I’m testing that case and nested ties. The Clojure launcher tried to write its classpath cache; I’ll use an existing classpath to keep the review read-only.\"}}\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:31:{\"type\