cs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.compilation.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\n   211\\t## 4. Amendments\\n   212\\t\\n   213\\t**A1 — carrier §6, from \\\"The procedure.\\\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\\n   214\\twith §2 above (the body label, `CE`, D1-D5). Keep the section's first\\n   215\\tparagraph (the counterexample) and amend \\\"What is defined, and what is\\n   216\\tnot claimed\\\" to: \\\"The canonical numbering is defined as the output of\\n   217\\tthe discovery-order procedure below, which is a function of the state\\n   218\\talone (proof in `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\\n   219\\t§3). It is not the minimum of the root bytes over all bijections. A\\n   220\\tstate whose cursor cells are distinguishable only by a symmetric guest\\n   221\\tcollection of pairs is refused (D5), not searched.\\\"\\n   222\\t\\n   223\\t**A2 — carrier §6 \\\"Validation\\\".** Replace \\\"A receiver whose work bound\\n   224\\t(§10.10) is exceeded before the procedure completes refuses with a\\n   225\\tstructured resource refusal\\\" with \\\"A receiver whose work bound (§10.10)\\n   226\\tis exceeded, or that reaches D5, refuses with the structured refusal\\n   227\\tnamed there\\\". Delete \\\"never falls back to encounter order\\\" (the\\n   228\\tprocedure *is* canonical encounter order; the clause now reads as a\\n   229\\tcontradiction) and keep \\\"never accepts an unfinished check\\\".\\n   230\\t\\n   231\\t**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \\\"The class-erased skeletons of §6\\n   232\\tare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\\n   233\\tof an emitted row\\\" with \\\"The content encodings of §6 are used only\\n   234\\tinside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted\\n   235\\trow\\\".\\n   236\\t\\n   237\\t**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \\\"Recompute §6 under the work bound and\\n   238\\tcompare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`;\\n   239\\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached;\\n   240\\tor the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded).\\\" Add\\n   241\\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 as\\n   242\\ta `:yin.k/undecodable` kind and a `:yin.k/non-portable` kind.\\n   243\\t\\n   244\\t**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \\\"refinement-and-search procedure\\\" →\\n   245\\t\\\"discovery-order procedure\\\". Answer 10: \\\"refinement work and search\\n   246\\tnodes for §6\\\" → \\\"and traversal work for §6\\\". Proposals list: \\\"as\\n   247\\tdefined by its procedure\\\" → \\\"as defined by discovery order, and the D5\\n   248\\trefusal class\\\".\\n   249\\t\\n   250\\t**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \\\"minted by the lift under §6's\\n   251\\tcanonical numbering\\\" add \\\"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's traversal)\\\".\\n   252\\t\\n   253\\t**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \\\"numbered by the draft's §6\\n   254\\tprocedure\\\" with \\\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\\n   255\\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\\\".\\n   256\\t\\n   257\\t**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Replace \\\"(its labels never encode an\\n   258\\tincoming cursor-cell id)\\\" with \\\"(it reads no incoming cursor-cell id)\\\"\\n   259\\tand \\\"are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6\\n   260\\tstep 2)\\\" with \\\"are entered in the bytewise order of their elements'\\n   261\\tcontent encodings (draft §6 D1)\\\". \\\"Never derived from traversal order\\\"\\n   262\\tstays true: the traversal is canonical, not the host's.\\n   263\\t\\n   264\\t**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12-19\\n   265\\tof §8 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\\n   266\\t\\n   267\\t**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \\\"minted by the emitter (stable within\\n   268\\tone lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\\\" remains true.\\n   269\\t\\n   270\\t## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\\n   kind admissible in that position; the structural edge family is\\n   acyclic (checked with an active-path walk, bounded by the row count);\\n   every row is reachable from the root through structural edges (an\\n   unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cursor-cell C]` names a\\n   census entry and every census entry is reached **through the task's\\n   own roots, the census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and\\n   per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked\\n   table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every\\n   `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child is a\\n   `:child` task. After this step every traversal below is finite and\\n   safe.\\n4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals `(jing/segment-key body)`\\n   under the algorithm version 3 mints with, and its address names that\\n   algorithm; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\\n   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\\n   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\\n   code key.\\n5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\\n   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\\n   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\\n   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\\n   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\\n   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\\n   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\\n   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\\n   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\\n   lose a binding on lower.\\n6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound and\\n   compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the\\n   resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded).\\n7. **Frames.** For each body (the root and every install child), first\\n   decode its task context (parked key, live FFI call ids, the root's\\n   free environment from `free-env-cref`) and judge it once by\\n   `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame/task-defect` (continuation §3.2 rule\\n   X1: the free environment is a sorted, unique symbol-keyed map with no\\n   reserved name); this runs for every body, a `:halted` body with no\\n   frames included, since no frame rule establishes X1. Then every\\n   `:act`, `:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is\\n   decoded to its keyed frame and judged by\\n   `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame/defect` with its context (`:wait`,\\n   `:captured`, `:parked`) and that task context; a defect of either\\n   function is `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path\\n   (continuation §3.3). This step runs after canonical numbering (step 6)\\n   and before pending validation (step 8).\\n8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\\n   agreement of §5.3 (cursor-cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\\n   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\\n   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\\n9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\\n   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, the initial free\\n   environment `free-env`, store, module stores, result, children; the\\n   free environment is a root because a value reachable only through it\\n   can carry closures, streams and primitives, continuation §7.1) and\\n   require the carried `requires` and `code` map to agree with it\\n   exactly: a declared segment not reached or a reached segment not\\n   declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`, and every reached segment has a\\n   non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry (UCF §7.6.1a). Then\\n   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\\n   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\\n   with cursor cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\\n   complete for every `store-of` reached.\\n10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\\n    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\\n    never adds missing header keys.\\n11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\\n    the receiving composition.\\n12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\\n    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\\n    private resource entry per cursor cell, the parked table, children first\\n    (one native task instance **per install entry**, even where two\\n    entries share a child row), then waits in order; adopt `max(local,\\n    carried)` for the id counter; publish the machine only after every\\n    child succeeds; run, poll, mint, append, close or initialize nothing\\n    during lower.\\n\\nDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\\n`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\\n(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\\n`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\\nthe two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-y40zHJ2d' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:40:34.296 xcodebuild[75620:57373111]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:40:34.503 xcodebuild[75620:57373110] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-jFHyLWng' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:40:35.078 xcodebuild[75637:57373239]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:40:35.320 xcodebuild[75637:57373238] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:159:(declare emit-node canonical-numbering validate-frames)\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:187:                                 (let [[id child-rows] (emit-node x (canonical-numbering x) {})]\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:233:(declare canonical-numbering)\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:236:(defn canonical-numbering\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:240:  ([task] (canonical-numbering task default-budget))\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:274:                   (let [[id _rows] (emit-node n (canonical-numbering n budget) {})]\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:371:   (let [[id rows] (emit-node task (canonical-numbering task budget) {})]\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:575:             (bad! :noncanonical-cursor-cells [root]))\\nsrvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:589:(defn canonical-under\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:143:(deftest canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:146:        ca (rows/canonical-under a {:lane-a/s1 :s1 :lane-a/s2 :s2})\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:147:        cb (rows/canonical-under b {:lane-b/s2 :s1 :lane-b/s1 :s2})]\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:150:           (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:156:           (:yin.k/kind (error #(rows/canonical-under a {:lane-a/s1 :s1})))))))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:168:      (is (= 2 (count (rows/canonical-numbering a)))))))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:176:    (is (= 2 (count (rows/canonical-numbering (make-task :x :y)))))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:212:    (is (= :noncanonical-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:248:    (is (= (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under wire {:child/s :child :root/s :root}))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:249:           (:yin.k/root (rows/emit (rows/decode (rows/canonical-under wire {:child/s :child :root/s :root}))))))))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:303:    (is (= (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under (rows/emit a) ident))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:304:           (:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under (rows/emit b) ident))))))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:322:        numbering (rows/canonical-numbering task)\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:328:    (is (= (:z/l (rows/canonical-numbering renamed)) (numbering :l)))))\\nsrvm-phase6a:test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:401:  (let [d (error #(rows/canonical-numbering (seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square)))]\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:34:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files docs/design | rg 'register'; nl -ba collab/numbering-probe/src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc | sed -n '355,380p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md | sed -n '14,25p;330,375p'; rg -n 'law|numbered by' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md | head -22; git show srvm-phase6a:src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | nl -ba | sed -n '2308,2320p' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.v2.divergence-register.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md\\n   355\\t       (if (empty? ids) {} (:numbering (search labels 0)))))))\\n   356\\t\\n   357\\t\\n   358\\t(defn emit-numbered\\n   359\\t  \\\"Emit under a supplied numbering. Useful for canonicality probes;\\n   360\\t   ordinary callers use emit, which computes the canonical numbering.\\\"\\n   361\\t  [task numbering]\\n   362\\t  (let [[id rows] (emit-node task numbering {})]\\n   363\\t    {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))}))\\n   364\\t\\n   365\\t\\n   366\\t(defn emit\\n   367\\t  \\\"Emit an abstract task as a self-contained carrier. This is a codec,\\n   368\\t   not lift-task: the caller supplies the complete abstract row tree.\\\"\\n   369\\t  ([task] (emit task default-budget))\\n   370\\t  ([task budget]\\n   371\\t   (let [[id rows] (emit-node task (canonical-numbering task budget) {})]\\n   372\\t     {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))})))\\n   373\\t\\n   374\\t\\n   375\\t(defn- structural?\\n   376\\t  [kind d x]\\n   377\\t  (cond\\n   378\\t    (and (= :cursor-cells kind) (= :C d)) (and (keyword? x) (some? (re-matches census-key (name x))))\\n   379\\t    (or (ref? d) (= :C d)) (keyword? x)\\n   380\\t    (refs? d) (and (vector? x) (every? keyword? x))\\n    14\\t(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a read-only prototype run\\n    15\\tagainst that code (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\\n    16\\t\\n    17\\t**Recommendation in one line:** adopt discovery-order numbering now (§7,\\n    18\\toption a): a function of structure, renaming-invariant, linear work,\\n    19\\tprovable by hand; it deletes carrier §6's refinement and search and\\n    20\\tkeeps every pinned root id except the one state only the search could\\n    21\\tnumber.\\n    22\\t\\n    23\\t## 1. Findings\\n    24\\t\\n    25\\t**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\\n   330\\t`[key value]` pairs; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\\n   331\\t(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\\n   332\\t`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) sorts before\\n   333\\t`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\\n   334\\tcounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties and is deferred; `'b`\\n   335\\tmeets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-sorts the set, `[:n 1]` against a body\\n   336\\tlabel, and `Y = cc-2`. Alias `[x x y]`: `x = cc-1` at index 0, `y = cc-2`\\n   337\\tat index 2. The example state of `collab/example-suspended-state.clj`\\n   338\\thas one cursor cell, first met in the act's environment under `c`, so\\n   339\\t`cc-1`; a one-cell state has the same rows under both procedures.\\n   340\\t\\n   341\\t## 7. Recommendation\\n   342\\t\\n   343\\t**(a) Adopt discovery order now.** 6b has not started and 6a is\\n   344\\tunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\\n   345\\ttests plus the amendments of §4: delete `check-label-bytes!`\\n   346\\t(`rows.cljc:212-230`), `positions` (`:285-325`), `classes` (`:326-330`),\\n   347\\t`refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`) and the label seeding\\n   348\\t(`:252-255`); keep `ce` (`:257-284`) with the numbered/unnumbered\\n   349\\tspelling; add the traversal, fixpoint, orbit rule and refusal (the\\n   350\\tprototype is 110 lines with helpers; 60-80 in place); delete the\\n   351\\t`:search` and `:label-bytes` budget keys (`:200-202`). Unchanged:\\n   352\\t`emit`, `emit-numbered`, `canonical-under`, the census and its numeric\\n   353\\torder (`:140-156`), `decode*`'s recompute-and-compare (`:570-575`),\\n   354\\tnumbering before `validate-frames` (`:576-578`; test\\n   355\\t`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`), the `:bytes`,\\n   356\\t`:rows`, `:depth` and `:work` bounds. Tests: `rows_test.cljc:171`\\n   357\\tbecomes the orbit-rule test (no `{:search 1}`); `:307-329` keeps\\n   358\\t`fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`, drops `55ff168e…`, `{:search 2}` and `cc-3`;\\n   359\\t`:400-404` becomes the D5 refusal test; `:406-421` is deleted. Cost:\\n   360\\tone implementation round and one review round, and the review can check\\n   361\\t§3 by hand. Risk: a canonicality bug, mitigated by §8 item 18's\\n   362\\tgenerator in CI and by the proof; and the D5 class, documented and\\n   363\\tnarrowable later without id churn.\\n   364\\t\\n   365\\t**(b) Keep the 6a design.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine\\n   366\\tharness and every reviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search,\\n   367\\tlabels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte, and a\\n   368\\tdefinition that is \\\"whatever the procedure outputs\\\". Not recommended.\\n   369\\t\\n   370\\t**(c) Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the\\n   371\\tharness (§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\\n   372\\t\\n   373\\t## 8. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\\n   374\\t\\n   375\\t12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\\n20:(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\\n225:`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by the draft's §6 procedure; the root carries\\n755:value per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\\n1170:   wrappers, §5.2), on the reference lane; laws 1-3 and 6; counts\\n1256:10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every row ×\\n1258:11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs.\\n1318:  positional values are nil-filled; the law is on canonical frames);\\n1555:In §9's law list delete \\\"Exact mutual inversion is required only between\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-HpfDxzRx' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:41:05.313 xcodebuild[75721:57373826]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:41:05.661 xcodebuild[75721:57373824] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-ODzGq3yQ' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-10-12 02:41:06.429 xcodebuild[75725:57373849]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-10-12 02:41:06.707 xcodebuild[75725:57373848] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n  2308\\t    (fail \\\"Cannot resume: parked continuation not found\\\"\\n  2309\\t          {:parked-id parked-id})))\\n  2310\\t\\n  2311\\t\\n  2312\\t(defn gensym\\n  2313\\t  \\\"Generate a unique ID and return [id updated-state].\\\"\\n  2314\\t  ([state] (gensym state \\\"id\\\"))\\n  2315\\t  ([state prefix]\\n  2316\\t   (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\\n  2317\\t         id (gen-id prefix id-counter)]\\n  2318\\t     [id (assoc state :id-counter (inc id-counter))])))\\n  2319\\t\\n  2320\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1792140000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:47:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_23\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-11 19:42:34 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 02:42:34 Asia/Bangkok\\n\\nDiscovery-order numbering remains a viable direction, but **this note is not sound as written**. D4 admits choices that change the root id, and nested ties can leave cursor cells unnumbered.\\n\\nReview was read-only at HEAD `7a6d7cc9`; phase 6a was inspected through Git at `d77da9f0`. No files were edited, staged, or committed. Completion timestamps use the available clock, which precedes the brief’s Created-GMT.\\n\\nFindings below use `note` for `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`, `probe` for `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, and `rows` for phase 6a’s `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`.\\n\\n| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **Blocking** | note:153, 183 | D4’s occurrence-locality condition does not preserve alias topology **within** an element. Equal `CE` does not imply interchangeability. `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` passes D4 and produces different roots after element reversal. | Require a proven alias-preserving correspondence between tied elements, including their internal traversal. Conservatively refuse uncertified cases. Equal bodies and disjoint identities alone are insufficient. |\\n| **Blocking** | note:153; probe:146–162 | Entering D4 elements can discover nested tie groups. The prototype processes only the old residue, then returns success. `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` returns an empty numbering and emits source ids. | Specify and implement processing of newly discovered deferred groups, including further fixpoint/orbit checks. Require total numbering before emission. |\\n| **High** | note:70–88, 219 | The stated refusal class is too narrow. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` refuses even though X and Y are interchangeable and there are no guest pairs or map keys. | Describe D5 by its actual predicate. Separate “not certified by this procedure” from “non-interchangeable,” and remove the unsupported rarity claim. |\\n| **High** | note:352–354; rows:570–575 | Decode invokes `emit`. A newly introduced lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` exception will propagate unless explicitly translated. Recompute-and-compare alone does not implement the proposed decode taxonomy. | Translate only `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to `:yin.k/undecodable` at recomputation, preserving resource refusals and validator precedence. |\\n| **Medium** | probe:48, 111–113, 131–139; note:299 | The prototype differs from D1–D5: numbered `CE` uses keyword `:yin.k/cc-n`, not integer `n`; parked-table values are incorrectly visited as `V`, although they are structural refs; the orbit walker omits `:refs`. Install children still use 6a numbering. | Make the prototype schema-driven and match the specified encoding. Test parked states, K frames, and children under discovery numbering in every scope. |\\n| **Medium** | note:18, 124, 192–196 | “Linear work” is not established. `CE` sorts unordered containers; repeated encoding and nested expansion add work. Acyclic DAGs can have large expanded traversals. | State a bound in terms of expanded positions, sorting, rounds, and encoded bytes. Account for that work before allocation under the retained budgets. |\\n| **Medium** | note:223–262 | Amendments leave stale wording: A2 leaves “no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity”; A5 leaves §10.10’s “never falls back to encounter order”; A8 leaves the old byte-string body-label explanation and step-1 reference. | Update those passages explicitly. Distinguish canonical traversal from arbitrary host enumeration. |\\n| **Low** | note:274–289 | The portable-id example changes the carried `id-counter`, so the two complete tasks are already structurally different. Some engine line references are inaccurate. | Hold every carried field, including the counter, equal in the example; correct the citations. |\\n\\n**1. Position inventory**\\n\\nF1 is substantially correct: I found **no omitted identity-bearing position** in the carrier §5 tables or phase 6a’s row schema.\\n\\nThe ordered positions cover:\\n\\n- Root free environment, store, module stores, parked table, waits, and result.\\n- Act environments, windows, K frames; tail K frames; captured acts.\\n- Closure bindings and module-store contents.\\n- Pending bare cursor-cell slots and guest value/envelope slots.\\n- Install children, each with an independent task-local numbering scope.\\n\\nOnly guest set elements and guest map entries lack a structural order. Census declaration edges are correctly excluded.\\n\\nOne wording correction: D1 should explicitly retain **numeric ascending register order** for windows, as the carrier specifies, rather than grouping windows indiscriminately under byte ordering. The prototype’s parked-table omission is an implementation defect, not an inventory omission.\\n\\n**2. Attempts to break D1–D5**\\n\\nI ran the prototype using `java -cp` with an existing classpath because the requested Clojure launcher attempted a prohibited cache write. All custom inputs were evaluated in memory.\\n\\nThe following constructor fixes the surrounding task exactly:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(require '[probe.discovery :as p]\\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows :as r]\\n         '[yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as t])\\n\\n(def b (t/cursor-cell-body :s 0))\\n(def c #(vector :cursor-cell %))\\n(def row #(vector :row %))\\n(def v #(row (r/node :vec (mapv c %))))\\n(def s #(row (r/node :set %)))\\n(def task\\n  (fn [ids result]\\n    (t/task-node (mapv #(vector % b) ids) result)))\\n```\\n\\nAll named cursor cells below have the same body.\\n\\n**Counterexample A — D4’s orbit lemma**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])]))\\n(task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:y :z]) (v [:x :x])]))\\n```\\n\\nThese differ only in set enumeration. Both pass D4.\\n\\n| Enumeration | Numbering | BLAKE3 root digest |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `[X X]`, `[Y Z]` | X=1, Y=2, Z=3 | `65ba4530bd8908e734a8be4fcc594d2989c5a8a7d1017779ef8f62991df38dab` |\\n| `[Y Z]`, `[X X]` | Y=1, Z=2, X=3 | `387f294a2f1963f2f2241e8527d8698dedff5d50de4f46bb762b456a01375090` |\\n\\nThe emitted multisets contain respectively `[1 1], [2 3]` and `[1 2], [3 3]`. There is no alias-preserving automorphism exchanging the two elements. Both abstract inputs successfully emit and decode under existing 6a.\\n\\n**Counterexample B — guest map keys and values**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(task [:x :y :z]\\n      (row (r/node :map [(c :x) (c :x) (c :y) (c :z)])))\\n\\n(task [:x :y :z]\\n      (row (r/node :map [(c :y) (c :z) (c :x) (c :x)])))\\n```\\n\\nThe roots differ:\\n\\n```text\\n409548a71f080cae6f3f66911409370d83a5df9e928135d95c497e9b770d9328\\nbe62ba43d9d07dc7b5e29375e195ab839ab37b721074af816e540615f6167ee9\\n```\\n\\nThis is the same missing alias distinction across an entry’s key and value.\\n\\n**Counterexample C — nested unordered containers**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(task [:x :y :z :w]\\n      (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)])\\n          (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]))\\n```\\n\\nThe prototype returns `{:numbering {}}`. Renaming X/Y/Z/W to Q/R/S/T changes the emitted root:\\n\\n```text\\nfddde4522dbebc4ee6fa2d78a1bd1d2691faccc26c4ca14ee17c620ecdcddf0b\\nabb32996b0ac30b720f17c6cfacf0323dcb16a47d58cc8e54c33571ce5d58080\\n```\\n\\nThe outer orbit step discovers inner ties but never processes them.\\n\\nOther requested challenges:\\n\\n- `[#{X Y} X]` correctly resolves to X=1, Y=2 through D3.\\n- `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` refuses because identities cross tie groups.\\n- A parked act containing an ordered X reference is ignored by the prototype, causing an unnecessary refusal.\\n- An install child using the review fixture gets its old 6a numbering. Switching all scopes to discovery changes the parent root, confirming that current child results do not validate the proposed rule.\\n\\n**3. Proof**\\n\\nThe proof fails at the orbit lemma’s claim that equal `CE` yields “the same internal pattern.” Body erasure removes exactly the alias information needed to establish that claim.\\n\\nThe renaming induction is reasonable for D1–D3 with a complete schema walk and fixed encoding. “No source id is read” is insufficient for permutation invariance: listed order remains an input to D4.\\n\\nD3’s monotone numbering supports a finite bound on productive rounds. It does not prove that D4 finishes nested discovery or that every reached cursor cell receives a number. Consequently, the asserted bijection, determinism, and cross-engine conclusion are not established.\\n\\nSorting and repeated expansion also invalidate the unqualified linear-work claim.\\n\\n**4. Refusal taxonomy and practical class**\\n\\nThe proposed families are appropriate:\\n\\n- Lift: `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\\n- Decode recomputation: `:yin.k/undecodable`, same kind.\\n- Actual budget exhaustion: retain qualified unsupported/resource refusal.\\n\\nA deliberate representational restriction is not a resource exhaustion. Run it at carrier validation step 6, after structural/canonical-form checks and before frame validation. Implement the decode translation explicitly.\\n\\nThe remaining class is **not demonstrated rare**. The two-group example above is a simple reusable collection, not an exotic graph. Moreover, repairing D4 conservatively will enlarge the refused class beyond what F4 currently describes. That limitation needs an accurate owner-facing description before adoption.\\n\\n**5. Impact on 6a and pins**\\n\\nThe proposed deletions are broadly correct: refinement, positions, class construction, exhaustive search, growing-label checks, and search/label-byte budget keys.\\n\\nKeep the carrier schema, BLAKE3 addressing, census numeric order, final row ordering, recompute-and-compare, and numbering-before-frame-validation order. Add a complete traversal, deferred processing, certified orbit rule, total-numbering assertion, refusal translation, and meaningful work accounting.\\n\\nI recomputed these pins:\\n\\n| Fixture | BLAKE3 digest | Result |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Two-cell fixture | `fab223d9b3540fdce0ccf2132f8a4041917a158a32295470a4936754742d3ac4` | Unchanged |\\n| Worked frame | `c4a7c7e6de3b44400efec5930bc3d85d86b29153b4b2d548acba630bfbc3da7d` | Unchanged |\\n| Review counterexample | `a3713a5e1d1a051d1c0aab255bdc48349993fbf21d68ba947e74fc4408c68c28` | Matches proposed pin |\\n| `[X X Y]` | `4993b5b192de7d903a90f7bc87dde8c7d5ac007c2b013ef62b998dc988282576` | Matches proposed pin |\\n| `[X Y Y]` | `536c810b07c7cab1dd812fcb1bbf0c7433dc41af4e39037c5f278e82abf1e032` | Matches; unchanged from 6a |\\n\\nThe symmetric-pair, repeated-pair, map, and ten-cursor-cell pins also reproduced. The prototype reproduced the reported 400-state statistics. Existing 6a row tests passed: **21 tests, 324 assertions**.\\n\\nConsumers include `emit`, child emission, `decode*`, and `canonical-under`. Continuation §5.4’s round-trip and cross-engine laws depend on canonical re-emission. `canonical-under` still recomputes through `emit`; its mathematical guarantee therefore inherits this defect.\\n\\nAcceptance items 12–20 need the three counterexamples above, multi-group cases, parked/K coverage, discovery-numbered children, total-numbering checks, and decode round trips. Random testing alone missed both blocking defects. Keep a large-input regression even when deleting its obsolete label-budget expectation.\\n\\n**6. Portable ids**\\n\\nThe rejection is sound **for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged** under the owner’s structure-only root law. Local counters, execution sequence, occurrence/counter hashes, and random names cannot replace structural canonicalization.\\n\\nCorrect the example by making both tasks’ carried counters equal—for example, consume an unrelated fresh name in the second history. Do not claim every imaginable portable naming scheme is impossible: ids recomputed from snapshot structure are effectively the canonicalization problem again.\\n\\nThe note calls this F7; F6 is prototype evidence.\\n\\n**7. Grounding, amendments, and portability**\\n\\nI spot-checked more than twelve cited locations against branch code:\\n\\n| Reference | Check |\\n|---|---|\\n| rows:37–57 | Row schema and identity-bearing positions |\\n| rows:140–145 | Numeric census order |\\n| rows:148–156 | Emitted collection ordering |\\n| rows:185–189 | Independent child numbering |\\n| rows:200–202 | Budget keys |\\n| rows:212–230 | Label-byte checking |\\n| rows:252–255 | Initial labels |\\n| rows:257–284 | Content encoding |\\n| rows:285–325 | Positions |\\n| rows:331–354 | Refinement and search |\\n| rows:570–578 | Recompute, compare, then frames |\\n| rows_test:28 | Two-cell fixture |\\n| rows_test:159 | Alias fixtures |\\n| rows_test:171 | Symmetric pair |\\n| rows_test:206 | Numbering precedes frame defect |\\n| rows_test:307–338 | Pins and numeric census |\\n| rows_test:392–421 | Seven-cursor-cell and large-input tests |\\n\\nThe scratch rows implementation and tests matched their branch versions by checksum. Engine citations need correction: `issue-ref` starts at 311, `gensym` at 2312, and unminted cursor creation is principally 557–568.\\n\\nAmendments should also:\\n\\n- State that `[:child]` deliberately replaces the existing `[:child <child root id>]`; “one change; nothing else changes” is inaccurate.\\n- Define `[:n k]` unambiguously as an integer and align the prototype.\\n- Remove the stale body-label explanation in continuation §5.5.\\n- Update §10.10’s encounter-order wording.\\n- Change the diagnostics’ “two kinds” count when adding a third lift refusal.\\n\\nThe direction is compatible with phase 5’s pure image derivations and scanners. Phase 6’s engine-independent frames and sharing requirements remain unchanged, but its equality laws require the repaired canonicality proof.\\n\\nRetain continuation §12’s portability rules: nil-valued keys remain present; membership uses `contains?`; large sequences use `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`; pinned fixtures are `.cljc` data rather than whitespace-sensitive EDN strings on Dart. These were inspected, not validated by new Node or Dart runs.\\n\\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\"}}\n    20\t                    (rows/node :cursor-cells (mapcat identity cursor-cells)) {} {} [] [] nil result nil]))\n    21\t\n    22\t\n    23\t(defn cursor-cell-body\n    24\t  [stream position]\n    25\t  (rows/node :cursor-cell-body [(rows/node :stream [stream {:peer stream}]) position]))\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\t(defn fixture\n    29\t  [a b x y]\n    30\t  (let [payload [:row (rows/node :map\n    31\t                                 [[:lit 'both] [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell x] [:cursor-cell y]])]\n    32\t                                  [:lit 'one] [:cursor-cell x]\n    33\t                                  [:lit 'streams] [:row (rows/node :set [[:row (rows/node :stream [a {:peer a}])]\n    34\t                                                                         [:row (rows/node :stream [b {:peer b}])]])]\n    35\t                                  [:lit 'cursors] [:row (rows/node :map [[:cursor-cell x] [:lit :x] [:cursor-cell y] [:lit :y]])]])]\n    36\t        task (task-node [[x (cursor-cell-body a 0)] [y (cursor-cell-body b 0)]] [:lit nil])]\n    37\t    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots task) 6 (rows/node :store ['payload payload])))))\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t(declare portable-frame)\n    41\t\n    42\t\n    43\t(defn- encoded\n    44\t  [x]\n    45\t  (cond (and (map? x) (contains? x :yin.k/row)) [:row (:yin.k/row x)]\n    46\t        (and (map? x) (contains? x :yin.k/cursor-cell)) [:cursor-cell (:yin.k/cursor-cell x)]\n    47\t        :else [:lit x]))\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\t(defn- portable-value\n   155\t    (is (= :missing-stream-correspondence\n   156\t           (:yin.k/kind (error #(rows/canonical-under a {:lane-a/s1 :s1})))))))\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\t(deftest alias-topology-and-census\n   160\t  (let [x :local/x y :local/y body (cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n   161\t        a (task-node [[x body] [y body]] [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell x] [:cursor-c